/* DataArcus DP-600 practice bank. Original questions written for DataArcus, aligned to the DP-600 skills outline taking effect 19 October 2026. Not Microsoft exam content. */
window.DP600={"v":"2026-10-19","q":[{"id":"PD-G-001","d":"prep","s":"get","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"Gateways","q":"Contoso has a SQL Server instance named SQL1 in its corporate datacenter. There is no network connectivity between the datacenter and any Azure virtual network. You have a Fabric workspace named WS1. Several analysts need to refresh a semantic model and a Dataflow Gen2 in WS1 by using data from SQL1. What should you deploy?","opts":["A standard on-premises data gateway","An on-premises data gateway in personal mode","A virtual network (VNet) data gateway","A OneLake shortcut to SQL1"],"ans":0,"exp":"Fabric cloud services need a gateway to reach on-premises sources. A standard on-premises data gateway is installed on a machine that can reach SQL1 and can be shared by multiple users across supported services, including semantic models, dataflows and pipelines.","why":["","Personal mode serves only one user, cannot be shared and works only with Power BI, so the Dataflow Gen2 and other analysts cannot use it.","A VNet data gateway reaches sources that are reachable from an Azure virtual network. SQL1 has no connectivity to Azure virtual networks.","Shortcuts point to file and table storage such as OneLake, ADLS Gen2 or S3. A SQL Server database is not a shortcut source."],"ar":"لمصادر on-premises يستخدمها عدة مستخدمين وخدمات Fabric استخدم on-premises data gateway بالوضع standard وليس personal mode.","ref":[{"t":"What is an on-premises data gateway?","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/data-integration/gateway/service-gateway-onprem"}]},{"id":"PD-G-002","d":"prep","s":"get","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Gateways","q":"Fabrikam has an Azure SQL database named DB1 that is accessible only through a private endpoint in an Azure virtual network. Public network access to DB1 is disabled. You need a Fabric pipeline and a Dataflow Gen2 to read from DB1. The solution must NOT require you to provision or maintain any virtual machines. What should you use?","opts":["A virtual network (VNet) data gateway","A standard on-premises data gateway installed on an Azure VM","A OneLake shortcut","Mirroring with a public endpoint"],"ans":0,"exp":"A VNet data gateway is a Microsoft managed service that injects into your virtual network and supports private endpoint scenarios, so traffic to DB1 never uses a public endpoint. Dataflow Gen2, pipelines, Copy job, mirroring and semantic models can run on it, and there is no gateway VM to manage. It requires a Fabric or Premium capacity, which is billed for the gateway uptime.","why":["","An on-premises gateway on an Azure VM would work technically, but it requires you to run and maintain the VM 24/7, which violates the requirement.","A shortcut does not connect to Azure SQL Database, and it does not solve private network access for pipelines and dataflows.","Public network access is disabled, so a public endpoint is not available."],"ar":"عند وجود private endpoint وعدم الرغبة في إدارة أجهزة افتراضية استخدم VNet data gateway المُدار من Microsoft.","ref":[{"t":"What is a virtual network (VNet) data gateway?","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/data-integration/vnet/overview"}]},{"id":"PD-G-003","d":"prep","s":"get","type":"yesno","diff":3,"tag":"Connections","q":"Your organization uses shareable cloud connections in Fabric, managed from <b>Manage connections and gateways</b>. A connection named ADLSConn points to an Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 account.<br>For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.","opts":["A user who has no permission on ADLSConn cannot use ADLSConn to create a new ADLS Gen2 shortcut in a lakehouse.","A user who is assigned the User role on ADLSConn can share ADLSConn with other users.","A tenant admin can block sharing of shareable cloud connections tenant-wide while still allowing a list of specific users to share connections."],"ans":[true,false,true],"exp":"ADLS and S3 shortcuts delegate authorization through cloud connections, and binding a connection to a shortcut requires permission on that connection. Connection roles are User, User with resharing and Owner, so only User with resharing or Owner can share. Tenant admins can turn on blocking of shareable cloud connections and add an allowlist of users who can still share.","why":["Yes. Setting a connection for a shortcut is a bind operation, and only users with permission on the connection can perform it.","No. The User role can only use the connection. Sharing requires User with resharing or Owner.","Yes. The Blocking shareable cloud connections setting supports an allowlist of users who can still share."],"ar":"مشاركة الـ connection تتطلب دور User with resharing أو Owner، وإنشاء shortcut عبر connection يتطلب صلاحية على تلك الـ connection.","ref":[{"t":"Data source management","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-factory/data-source-management"},{"t":"OneLake shortcuts","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/onelake/onelake-shortcuts"}]},{"id":"PD-G-004","d":"prep","s":"get","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Gateways","q":"An analyst at Contoso installed an on-premises data gateway in personal mode on a laptop to refresh a Power BI semantic model from a file share. You now create a Fabric pipeline in workspace WS1 that must copy data from the same file share, and the connection must be shared with two other data engineers. What should you do?","opts":["Install a standard on-premises data gateway and create a shared connection that uses it","Share the personal mode gateway with the two data engineers","Grant the data engineers the Contributor role in WS1 so they can use the personal gateway","Create a VNet data gateway and add the laptop to the virtual network"],"ans":0,"exp":"A personal mode gateway allows one user to connect to data sources, cannot be shared, and can be used only with Power BI. Pipelines and shared connections need a standard on-premises data gateway, which supports multiple users and all supported services.","why":["","Personal mode gateways cannot be shared with other users.","Workspace roles do not change the personal gateway limitations. It still works only for its owner and only with Power BI.","A VNet gateway is a managed service for virtual network sources, not for a file share on a laptop network, and adding a laptop to a VNet is not a supported pattern."],"ar":"الـ gateway بوضع personal لمستخدم واحد ولـ Power BI فقط، أما pipelines والمشاركة فتحتاج standard gateway.","ref":[{"t":"What is an on-premises data gateway?","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/data-integration/gateway/service-gateway-onprem"},{"t":"Power BI implementation planning: Data gateways","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/powerbi-implementation-planning-data-gateways"}]},{"id":"PD-G-005","d":"prep","s":"get","type":"order","diff":2,"tag":"Gateways","q":"Fabrikam has an on-premises SQL Server database named SalesDB. You need to create a Dataflow Gen2 in a Fabric workspace that loads data from SalesDB into a lakehouse. No gateway exists yet. Which three actions should you perform in sequence?","opts":["Install a standard on-premises data gateway on a server that can reach SalesDB, and register it with your tenant","In Manage connections and gateways, create a connection to SalesDB that uses the gateway","In the Dataflow Gen2, get data from SalesDB by using the connection and set the lakehouse as the data destination","Install an on-premises data gateway in personal mode on your workstation","Create a virtual network data gateway in your Azure subscription","Create an ADLS Gen2 shortcut that targets SalesDB"],"ans":[0,1,2],"exp":"First install and register a standard on-premises data gateway close to the source. Then create a connection (data source) bound to that gateway so credentials are stored and can be shared. Finally author the Dataflow Gen2 using that connection and configure the lakehouse data destination.","why":["Step 1.","Step 2.","Step 3.","Personal mode works only with Power BI and cannot serve Dataflow Gen2.","A VNet gateway is for sources reachable from an Azure virtual network, not an on-premises server without VNet connectivity.","Shortcuts do not target SQL Server databases."],"ar":"الترتيب: تثبيت standard gateway، ثم إنشاء connection عليه، ثم استخدامه داخل Dataflow Gen2.","ref":[{"t":"Data source management","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-factory/data-source-management"},{"t":"What is an on-premises data gateway?","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/data-integration/gateway/service-gateway-onprem"}]},{"id":"PD-G-006","d":"prep","s":"get","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"OneLake catalog","q":"You are a data analyst at Contoso. You need to find lakehouses and semantic models across all workspaces in your tenant that have been certified or promoted, so you can reuse trusted data instead of creating new items. What should you use in the OneLake catalog Explore tab?","opts":["The Endorsed items filter","The My items filter","The Govern tab","The Real-Time hub Data streams page"],"ans":0,"exp":"The OneLake catalog Explore tab has predefined filters that work across workspaces. Endorsed items lists items you are allowed to find that are certified or promoted, with certified items listed first.","why":["","My items shows only items that you own.","The Govern tab shows governance insights and recommended actions for data you own, not a discovery list of endorsed items.","Real-Time hub lists streaming data such as eventstreams and KQL tables, not lakehouses and semantic models."],"ar":"في OneLake catalog استخدم فلتر Endorsed items لإيجاد العناصر الموثوقة (certified ثم promoted).","ref":[{"t":"Find and explore data in the OneLake catalog","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/governance/onelake-catalog-explore"}]},{"id":"PD-G-007","d":"prep","s":"get","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"OneLake catalog","q":"You have a lakehouse named LH1 in workspace WS1. Before you rename a column in a table in LH1, you need to identify downstream items in other workspaces that depend on LH1. You want to start from the OneLake catalog. What should you do?","opts":["Open the item details of LH1, go to the Lineage tab, and run Impact analysis","Open the Secure tab and review the OneLake security roles for LH1","Open the Govern tab and review sensitivity label coverage","Search for LH1 in Real-Time hub and view the data stream details"],"ans":0,"exp":"The item details view in the OneLake catalog includes a Lineage tab that shows upstream and downstream relationships. From the Lineage tab you can open Impact analysis to assess the effect of changes on downstream workspaces and items.","why":["","The Secure tab manages workspace roles and OneLake security roles, not dependencies.","The Govern tab provides governance posture insights, not per-item downstream dependencies.","Real-Time hub covers streaming data and KQL tables, not lakehouse lineage."],"ar":"من تفاصيل العنصر في OneLake catalog افتح Lineage ثم Impact analysis لمعرفة العناصر المتأثرة.","ref":[{"t":"View item details in the OneLake catalog","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/governance/onelake-catalog-item-details"}]},{"id":"PD-G-008","d":"prep","s":"get","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"Real-Time hub","q":"Contoso already sends sensor readings to an Azure Event Hubs namespace. A data engineer wants to discover this existing Azure resource from within Fabric and quickly create an eventstream that ingests from it, with most connection fields prepopulated. Which Real-Time hub page should the engineer use?","opts":["Azure sources (Microsoft sources)","Fabric events","OneLake catalog Explore tab","Monitor hub"],"ans":0,"exp":"Real-Time hub lists streaming resources from Microsoft services, such as Azure Event Hubs, Azure IoT Hub and database CDC sources, on the Microsoft sources page (labeled Azure sources in the left menu). Selecting a source opens a wizard with prepopulated fields that creates an eventstream to bring the data into Fabric.","why":["","Fabric events covers system events such as workspace item, job, OneLake and capacity events, not Event Hubs data streams.","The OneLake catalog is for discovering Fabric items such as lakehouses and semantic models, not external streaming sources.","Monitor hub shows activity and run history, not streaming source discovery."],"ar":"لاكتشاف مصادر streaming من Azure مثل Event Hubs استخدم صفحة Microsoft sources في Real-Time hub.","ref":[{"t":"Introduction to Fabric Real-Time hub","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/real-time-hub/real-time-hub-overview"},{"t":"Discover streaming sources from Microsoft","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/real-time-intelligence/user-flow-3"}]},{"id":"PD-G-009","d":"prep","s":"get","type":"yesno","diff":2,"tag":"Discovery","q":"You are evaluating the discovery features in Microsoft Fabric for your analytics team.<br>For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.","opts":["Tables in KQL databases that you have access to automatically appear in Real-Time hub.","A Fabric administrator must provision Real-Time hub for the tenant before users can use it.","The OneLake catalog Explore tab can list a semantic model configured as discoverable even if you do not have access to it, so you can request access."],"ans":[true,false,true],"exp":"Real-Time hub automatically shows the eventstream outputs and KQL database tables that you can access. Every Fabric tenant is automatically provisioned with Real-Time hub, with no setup steps. The OneLake catalog also lists discoverable semantic models that you cannot yet access, so you can request access.","why":["Yes. Stream outputs from eventstreams and KQL database tables you can access show up automatically.","No. Real-Time hub is provisioned automatically for every tenant.","Yes. Discoverable semantic models are listed so users can request access."],"ar":"Real-Time hub مُفعّل تلقائياً ويعرض جداول KQL والـ streams، وOneLake catalog يعرض الـ semantic models القابلة للاكتشاف لطلب الوصول.","ref":[{"t":"Introduction to Fabric Real-Time hub","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/real-time-hub/real-time-hub-overview"},{"t":"Find and explore data in the OneLake catalog","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/governance/onelake-catalog-explore"}]},{"id":"PD-G-010","d":"prep","s":"get","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Real-Time hub","q":"You need to start a pipeline automatically whenever a new file is created in an Azure Blob Storage container, and you also want to route those notifications to an eventstream. You want to configure this from a central discovery experience in Fabric. What should you use?","opts":["Azure events in Real-Time hub","The OneLake catalog Explore tab","A OneLake shortcut to the container with shortcut caching enabled","A scheduled Copy job that runs every minute"],"ans":0,"exp":"Real-Time hub exposes Azure events, such as Azure Blob Storage events, alongside Fabric events. You can subscribe to these discrete events, send them to an eventstream, and use them to trigger downstream actions such as running a pipeline or sending alerts.","why":["","The OneLake catalog discovers Fabric items. It does not subscribe to storage events.","A shortcut gives zero-copy access to data but does not raise events that trigger a pipeline.","A schedule is time based, not event based, and would run even when no file arrives."],"ar":"لتفعيل إجراءات عند وصول ملف إلى Blob Storage اشترك في Azure events من Real-Time hub.","ref":[{"t":"Explore the Azure events page in Fabric Real-Time hub","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/real-time-hub/azure-events-page"},{"t":"Introduction to Fabric Real-Time hub","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/real-time-hub/real-time-hub-overview"}]},{"id":"PD-G-011","d":"prep","s":"get","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"Shortcuts","q":"Fabrikam stores 20 TB of Parquet files in an Amazon S3 bucket. You have a Fabric lakehouse named LH1. You need Spark notebooks in Fabric to read the files. The solution must avoid duplicating the data and must keep a single copy in S3. What should you use?","opts":["An Amazon S3 shortcut in LH1","A Copy job from S3 to LH1","Mirroring","A Dataflow Gen2 with S3 as the source"],"ans":0,"exp":"A OneLake shortcut is a virtual reference that makes external data appear as a folder or table in a lakehouse without copying it. Amazon S3 is a supported external shortcut source, and you can enable shortcut caching in the workspace settings to reduce cross-cloud egress costs.","why":["","A Copy job physically copies the data into OneLake, creating a second copy.","Mirroring replicates databases into OneLake. It creates a copy and is not used for files in S3.","A Dataflow Gen2 ingests and transforms data into a destination, which duplicates it."],"ar":"لاستخدام بيانات S3 بدون نسخها أنشئ shortcut في الـ lakehouse.","ref":[{"t":"OneLake shortcuts","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/onelake/onelake-shortcuts"},{"t":"Shortcuts in a lakehouse","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-engineering/lakehouse-shortcuts"}]},{"id":"PD-G-012","d":"prep","s":"get","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Mirroring","q":"Contoso runs its ETL outside Fabric and stores curated gold tables in an Azure SQL database. You need the tables continuously replicated into OneLake in near real time for Power BI reporting. The solution must require minimal setup, and the replication compute must not consume capacity charges. What should you use?","opts":["Mirroring","A Copy job with incremental copy","A pipeline with a Copy activity on a 15-minute schedule","A Dataflow Gen2"],"ans":0,"exp":"Mirroring continuously replicates operational databases into a read-only destination in OneLake with minimal setup. The Fabric compute used to replicate the data is free, and Microsoft recommends mirroring for curated gold data that only needs to land in Fabric for reporting. Each mirrored database also gets a read-only SQL analytics endpoint.","why":["","Copy job can do CDC replication (preview), but it is not free and offers more control than needed here.","A scheduled Copy activity is batch, not continuous, and requires you to manage incremental state.","Dataflow Gen2 is for Power Query transformations on refresh, not continuous replication."],"ar":"للنسخ المستمر لقاعدة بيانات تشغيلية إلى OneLake بإعداد بسيط وبدون تكلفة compute للنسخ استخدم Mirroring.","ref":[{"t":"Microsoft Fabric decision guide: Choose a data movement strategy","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-factory/decision-guide-data-movement"},{"t":"Open mirroring in Microsoft Fabric","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/mirroring/open-mirroring"}]},{"id":"PD-G-013","d":"prep","s":"get","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Copy job","q":"You need to ingest 40 tables from an on-premises SQL Server database into a bronze lakehouse every hour. CDC is not enabled on the source, but every table has a LastModified column. The first run must copy all rows, and later runs must copy only new or changed rows. You must not build a pipeline or maintain a watermark control table. What should you use?","opts":["A Copy job with incremental copy that uses LastModified as the watermark column","A pipeline with Lookup and Copy activities that store the watermark in a control table","Mirroring","A OneLake shortcut"],"ans":0,"exp":"Copy job natively supports full and incremental copy. In incremental mode the first run copies everything and later runs copy only rows whose watermark column value is larger than any previously copied value. Copy job tracks the state of the last successful run for you, so no pipeline or control table is needed.","why":["","This pattern works, but it requires building a pipeline and managing the watermark state yourself, which the requirements prohibit.","Mirroring requires the supported change feed mechanism and replicates to a read-only mirrored database rather than a bronze lakehouse table on a schedule.","Shortcuts cannot target a SQL Server database."],"ar":"Copy job يدعم incremental copy بعمود watermark ويتتبع الحالة تلقائياً بدون بناء pipeline.","ref":[{"t":"Incremental copy in Copy job","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-factory/incremental-copy-job"},{"t":"Microsoft Fabric decision guide: Choose a data movement strategy","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-factory/decision-guide-data-movement"}]},{"id":"PD-G-014","d":"prep","s":"get","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Pipelines","q":"Fabrikam needs a metadata-driven ingestion process. A control table lists 300 source objects. For each object, the process must copy data into a lakehouse, then run a notebook, then call a stored procedure in a warehouse, and send a notification on failure. Which should you use?","opts":["A pipeline that uses a ForEach loop with a parameterized Copy activity","A single Copy job","Mirroring","An eventstream"],"ans":0,"exp":"Pipelines provide orchestration, and Copy activities in pipelines support data object parameterization and metadata-driven ingestion. A pipeline can chain the copy with notebook, stored procedure and notification activities and control success and failure paths.","why":["","Copy job simplifies bulk, incremental and CDC copy but does not orchestrate notebooks, stored procedures and failure notifications on its own.","Mirroring only replicates supported databases into a read-only destination and has no orchestration.","Eventstreams process streaming events, not metadata-driven batch orchestration."],"ar":"للتنسيق المعقد والاستيعاب المعتمد على metadata استخدم pipeline مع Copy activity مُعلمة.","ref":[{"t":"Microsoft Fabric decision guide: Choose a data movement strategy","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-factory/decision-guide-data-movement"}]},{"id":"PD-G-015","d":"prep","s":"get","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"Dataflow Gen2","q":"A team of business analysts at Contoso is skilled in Power Query but does not write code. They need to connect to Excel files in SharePoint and a Salesforce source, merge and pivot the data, and load the result into a Fabric warehouse on a schedule. What should they use?","opts":["Dataflow Gen2","A Spark notebook","An eventstream","A OneLake shortcut"],"ans":0,"exp":"Dataflow Gen2 uses the Power Query experience, offers a large set of connectors and 300+ transformation functions, and lets you set a data destination such as a Fabric warehouse or lakehouse. It is aimed at no-code and low-code personas, including business analysts.","why":["","Notebooks require Spark code such as PySpark or Scala, which the team does not write.","Eventstreams are for real-time event ingestion, not scheduled batch transformation of files and SaaS data.","Shortcuts provide access without transformation and do not support SharePoint Excel merging or Salesforce as described."],"ar":"لمحللي الأعمال المعتادين على Power Query استخدم Dataflow Gen2 للتحويل والتحميل إلى warehouse.","ref":[{"t":"Microsoft Fabric decision guide: copy activity, Copy job, dataflow, Eventstream, or Spark","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/decision-guide-pipeline-dataflow-spark"},{"t":"What is Dataflow Gen2?","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-factory/dataflows-gen2-overview"}]},{"id":"PD-G-016","d":"prep","s":"get","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Notebooks","q":"Your data engineers are proficient in Python. They need to ingest customer review data from a REST API that uses custom pagination, parse nested JSON with open-source Python libraries, and write the results to Delta tables in a lakehouse named LH1. What should they use?","opts":["A Fabric notebook","A Copy job","Mirroring","A Dataflow Gen2 with the default destination set to LH1"],"ans":0,"exp":"Spark notebooks are code-first and support PySpark, Scala, Spark SQL and R plus open-source libraries, which makes them the best fit for custom API logic and complex parsing. The code can write directly to Delta tables in the lakehouse.","why":["","Copy job offers lightweight transformations only and is not designed for custom code logic with Python libraries.","Mirroring replicates supported databases and cannot call a REST API.","Dataflow Gen2 is low code with Power Query M, which does not use open-source Python libraries the team wants to reuse."],"ar":"عندما يتطلب الاستيعاب منطقاً برمجياً مخصصاً ومكتبات Python استخدم notebook.","ref":[{"t":"Microsoft Fabric decision guide: copy activity, Copy job, dataflow, Eventstream, or Spark","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/decision-guide-pipeline-dataflow-spark"}]},{"id":"PD-G-017","d":"prep","s":"get","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"Eventstreams","q":"Contoso has thousands of IoT devices that send telemetry to Azure IoT Hub. You need to capture the events in Fabric with low latency, apply simple no-code filtering, and route them to an eventhouse. What should you use?","opts":["An eventstream","A pipeline Copy activity","A Copy job","A Dataflow Gen2"],"ans":0,"exp":"Eventstreams are designed for real-time ingestion, transformation and routing of streaming data. They support sources such as Azure IoT Hub and Event Hubs, no-code processing, and destinations such as Eventhouse, Lakehouse, Activator and custom endpoints.","why":["","Copy activity is batch data movement, not continuous low-latency streaming.","Copy job handles bulk, incremental and CDC copy, not IoT event streams.","Dataflow Gen2 runs on refresh and is not a streaming engine."],"ar":"لبيانات IoT اللحظية استخدم eventstream وأرسلها إلى eventhouse.","ref":[{"t":"Microsoft Fabric decision guide: Choose a data movement strategy","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-factory/decision-guide-data-movement"}]},{"id":"PD-G-018","d":"prep","s":"get","type":"multi","n":2,"diff":2,"tag":"Ingest options","q":"You need to continuously replicate changes from a source database into Fabric by using change data capture (CDC). Which two Fabric options support CDC-based continuous replication? Each correct answer presents a complete solution. Select TWO.","opts":["Mirroring","Copy job","Pipeline Copy activity","Dataflow Gen2","OneLake shortcut"],"ans":[0,1],"exp":"According to the Fabric data movement decision guide, Mirroring and Copy job both support CDC-based continuous replication (eventstreams do too for streaming scenarios). Copy job adds scheduling, upsert and destination flexibility, while Mirroring is the simplest and free option with a read-only destination. Note that CDC replication in Copy job is currently a preview feature.","why":["","","The pipeline Copy activity supports batch copy and watermark incremental patterns you build yourself, but not native CDC continuous replication.","Dataflow Gen2 refreshes queries on a schedule and does not do CDC replication.","A shortcut references data in place. It does not replicate changes."],"ar":"Mirroring وCopy job كلاهما يدعم النسخ المستمر المعتمد على CDC.","ref":[{"t":"Microsoft Fabric decision guide: Choose a data movement strategy","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-factory/decision-guide-data-movement"}]},{"id":"PD-G-019","d":"prep","s":"get","type":"multi","n":2,"diff":3,"tag":"Shortcuts","q":"You have a lakehouse named LH1. A Spark job must write updated Delta data back through shortcuts in LH1 so the changes land in the shortcut target. Which two shortcut types support write operations? Each correct answer presents a complete solution. Select TWO.","opts":["OneLake shortcut to another lakehouse","Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 shortcut","Amazon S3 shortcut","Google Cloud Storage shortcut"],"ans":[0,1],"exp":"OneLake and ADLS Gen2 shortcuts support both read and write operations, including writing to Delta tables and files. Amazon S3 and Google Cloud Storage shortcuts are read-only, so writes must go to the source system by other means.","why":["","","Amazon S3 shortcuts are read-only.","Google Cloud Storage shortcuts are read-only."],"ar":"الكتابة عبر shortcut مدعومة فقط لأنواع OneLake وADLS Gen2، أما S3 وGCS فللقراءة فقط.","ref":[{"t":"Move shortcuts to a new region","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/onelake/shortcuts/move-shortcuts-region"},{"t":"OneLake shortcuts","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/onelake/onelake-shortcuts"}]},{"id":"PD-G-020","d":"prep","s":"get","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"Data stores","q":"Contoso has a BI team whose only skill is T-SQL. They need a store for a dimensional model where they can run INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE statements, use stored procedures, and wrap loads into several tables inside one multi-table transaction. Which Fabric data store should you recommend?","opts":["Warehouse","Lakehouse","Eventhouse","Mirrored database"],"ans":0,"exp":"Fabric Data Warehouse is the choice for enterprise data warehousing and SQL-based BI with full T-SQL DML and multi-table transaction support. Its primary write engine is T-SQL, which matches the team skills.","why":["","A lakehouse is written with Spark or Python. Its SQL analytics endpoint is read-only for tables and it lacks multi-table transactions.","An eventhouse uses KQL and is optimized for streaming and time-series data, not T-SQL DML.","A mirrored database is a read-only replica and cannot be a general-purpose writable store."],"ar":"لفريق يعتمد على T-SQL ويحتاج DML وmulti-table transactions اختر Warehouse.","ref":[{"t":"Microsoft Fabric decision guide: choose a data store","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/decision-guide-data-store"},{"t":"Choose an analytical data store in Microsoft Fabric","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/data-guide/technology-choices/fabric-analytical-data-stores"}]},{"id":"PD-G-021","d":"prep","s":"get","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Data stores","q":"Fabrikam collects application logs and device telemetry as semi-structured JSON at a high ingestion rate. Analysts query the data with KQL and need subsecond, time-based queries over billions of recent events. Which Fabric data store should you use?","opts":["Eventhouse","Warehouse","SQL database","Lakehouse"],"ans":0,"exp":"Eventhouses are built for streaming, time-based event data such as telemetry and logs. They handle structured, semi-structured and free-text data, use KQL as the primary engine, support high ingestion frequency, and deliver subsecond query runtimes for selective and aggregate queries.","why":["","A warehouse uses T-SQL and has limited support for semi-structured parsing and no KQL.","SQL database targets transactional OLTP workloads and moderate volumes, not high-rate telemetry analytics with KQL.","A lakehouse can store JSON, but it uses Spark and T-SQL rather than native KQL, and it is not optimized for subsecond streaming analytics."],"ar":"للسجلات والـ telemetry اللحظية مع KQL اختر Eventhouse.","ref":[{"t":"Microsoft Fabric decision guide: choose a data store","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/decision-guide-data-store"},{"t":"Eventhouse overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/real-time-intelligence/eventhouse"}]},{"id":"PD-G-022","d":"prep","s":"get","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Data stores","q":"Contoso is building an internal order-entry application. It needs a normalized relational schema, high-frequency single-row inserts and updates, highly selective lookups with low latency, and referential integrity. The data must also be available for analytics in OneLake without building ETL. Which Fabric data store should you choose?","opts":["SQL database in Fabric","Warehouse","Lakehouse","Eventhouse"],"ans":0,"exp":"SQL database in Fabric is the recommended store for operational, transactional (OLTP) and normalized workloads. It handles high-frequency updates and selective lookups, and it is automatically mirrored to OneLake in Delta format so other Fabric engines can analyze the data.","why":["","A warehouse is optimized for large scans and aggregations, not low-latency selective lookups and OLTP updates.","A lakehouse is designed for big data engineering, and updates and deletes are only moderately efficient.","An eventhouse is append oriented with low efficiency for updates and deletes."],"ar":"لأحمال OLTP والتحديثات المتكررة اختر SQL database في Fabric، وهي تُنسخ تلقائياً إلى OneLake.","ref":[{"t":"Microsoft Fabric decision guide: choose a data store","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/decision-guide-data-store"},{"t":"Choose an analytical data store in Microsoft Fabric","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/data-guide/technology-choices/fabric-analytical-data-stores"}]},{"id":"PD-G-023","d":"prep","s":"get","type":"multi","n":2,"diff":2,"tag":"Data stores","q":"You are choosing Fabric data stores for a team that will write all data by using T-SQL only. For which two data stores is T-SQL the primary engine for write operations? Each correct answer presents a complete solution. Select TWO.","opts":["Lakehouse","Warehouse","Eventhouse","SQL database"],"ans":[1,3],"exp":"Warehouse and SQL database both use T-SQL as the primary engine for reads and writes. A lakehouse is written by Spark or Python, and an eventhouse is written by KQL.","why":["Lakehouse writes use Spark (PySpark, Spark SQL, Scala, R) or Python. The SQL analytics endpoint is read-only for tables.","","Eventhouse writes use KQL commands and ingestion, not T-SQL.",""],"ar":"الكتابة بـ T-SQL تكون في Warehouse وSQL database، أما Lakehouse فبـ Spark وEventhouse بـ KQL.","ref":[{"t":"Choose an analytical data store in Microsoft Fabric","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/data-guide/technology-choices/fabric-analytical-data-stores"}]},{"id":"PD-G-024","d":"prep","s":"get","type":"yesno","diff":2,"tag":"Data stores","q":"You are comparing data stores in Microsoft Fabric.<br>For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.","opts":["You can use the SQL analytics endpoint of a lakehouse to run UPDATE statements against lakehouse Delta tables.","Data in a KQL database in an eventhouse is available in OneLake in Delta format by default, without any configuration.","A lakehouse can store unstructured files, such as images, alongside Delta tables."],"ans":[false,false,true],"exp":"The lakehouse SQL analytics endpoint is read-only for tables, so data changes are made with Spark or other writers. All Fabric data stores are available in OneLake in open format by default except KQL databases, where OneLake availability is an opt-in feature. A lakehouse stores both files, including unstructured data, and managed Delta tables.","why":["No. The SQL analytics endpoint is read-only for tables. Use Spark or another writer.","No. OneLake availability for a KQL database is opt-in.","Yes. The Files area of a lakehouse holds raw and unstructured data."],"ar":"الـ SQL analytics endpoint للقراءة فقط، وOneLake availability في Eventhouse اختيارية وليست افتراضية.","ref":[{"t":"Microsoft Fabric decision guide: choose a data store","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/decision-guide-data-store"},{"t":"Store data in Microsoft Fabric","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/store-data"}]},{"id":"PD-G-025","d":"prep","s":"get","type":"order","diff":3,"tag":"Eventhouse OneLake availability","q":"You have an eventhouse that contains a KQL database named KQLDB1 with a table named Telemetry that already holds six months of data. You have a lakehouse named LH1. You need Spark users in LH1 to query all Telemetry data, including historical rows, without copying the data, and then build a Direct Lake report on it. Which three actions should you perform in sequence?","opts":["In LH1, create a table shortcut that targets the Telemetry table by using the Microsoft OneLake source","Turn on OneLake availability for KQLDB1 and select Apply to existing tables","Create a Direct Lake semantic model that uses the Telemetry table in LH1","Turn on OneLake integration in the settings of a semantic model","Create a Copy job that copies Telemetry into LH1","Run a purge command on Telemetry to remove old rows"],"ans":[1,0,2],"exp":"Turning on OneLake availability exposes KQL data in Delta Lake format in OneLake. Selecting Apply to existing tables includes historic backfill. Once the Delta representation exists, you create a OneLake shortcut from the lakehouse and then use the table in a Direct Lake semantic model.","why":["Step 2.","Step 1.","Step 3.","OneLake integration applies to Import semantic models exporting to Delta, not to eventhouses.","A Copy job duplicates the data, which violates the requirement.","Purge is not allowed while OneLake availability is on and it would remove required history."],"ar":"فعّل OneLake availability مع Apply to existing tables، ثم أنشئ shortcut في الـ lakehouse، ثم ابنِ Direct Lake model.","ref":[{"t":"Turn on OneLake availability for an eventhouse","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/real-time-intelligence/event-house-onelake-availability"}]},{"id":"PD-G-026","d":"prep","s":"get","type":"single","diff":3,"tag":"Eventhouse OneLake availability","q":"You have a KQL database named KQLDB1 with OneLake availability turned on for all tables. A compliance request requires you to permanently delete all rows for one customer from a table named Events. What should you do?","opts":["Turn off OneLake availability, perform the delete, and then turn OneLake availability back on","Run the delete command directly, because OneLake availability propagates deletes automatically","Delete the files for that customer from the Delta folder in OneLake by using OneLake file explorer","Run OPTIMIZE and VACUUM on the Delta table in OneLake"],"ans":0,"exp":"While OneLake availability is on, you cannot delete, truncate or purge data, rename tables, alter column types, or apply row-level security. You must turn off OneLake availability, perform the task, and then turn it back on. Turning it off soft deletes the OneLake copy, and turning it on again restores all data including historic backfill.","why":["","Delete, truncate and purge are blocked while OneLake availability is turned on.","The Delta table produced by OneLake availability is read-only, and editing files would not change the KQL database.","The OneLake copy is read-only and cannot be optimized after creation, and VACUUM would not delete specific customer rows."],"ar":"لا يمكن الحذف أو purge أثناء تفعيل OneLake availability، أوقفها أولاً ثم نفّذ ثم أعد تفعيلها.","ref":[{"t":"Turn on OneLake availability for an eventhouse","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/real-time-intelligence/event-house-onelake-availability"}]},{"id":"PD-G-027","d":"prep","s":"get","type":"single","diff":3,"tag":"Eventhouse OneLake availability","q":"You turned on OneLake availability for a KQL table named Orders that receives a slow trickle of events. Users of a lakehouse shortcut to Orders report that new rows can take hours to appear. You need new data to appear in OneLake within about 5 minutes. What should you run?","opts":["<code>.alter-merge table Orders policy mirroring dataformat=parquet with (IsEnabled=true, TargetLatencyInMinutes=5)</code>","<code>.alter table Orders policy retention softdelete = 5m</code>","<code>.alter table Orders policy streamingingestion enable</code>","<code>.show table Orders mirroring operations</code>"],"ans":0,"exp":"OneLake availability uses a mirroring policy. By default the eventhouse can delay writes up to 3 hours, or until files of about 200 to 256 MB exist, to avoid many small Parquet files. You can set TargetLatencyInMinutes between 5 minutes and 3 hours with the alter-merge mirroring policy command. Shorter latency can produce more small files and slower queries.","why":["","The retention policy controls how long data is kept, and a 5 minute retention would delete data.","Streaming ingestion affects ingestion into the KQL database, not the delay for writing Delta files to OneLake.","This command only monitors mirroring latency. It does not change it."],"ar":"لتقليل زمن ظهور البيانات في OneLake عدّل mirroring policy بقيمة TargetLatencyInMinutes، والافتراضي حتى 3 ساعات.","ref":[{"t":"Turn on OneLake availability for an eventhouse","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/real-time-intelligence/event-house-onelake-availability"}]},{"id":"PD-G-028","d":"prep","s":"get","type":"yesno","diff":3,"tag":"Eventhouse OneLake availability","q":"You plan to turn on OneLake availability for a KQL database named KQLDB1 in an eventhouse.<br>For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.","opts":["Data removed from KQLDB1 at the end of its retention period is also removed from OneLake.","Turning on OneLake availability adds a separate storage charge for the Delta copy in OneLake.","While OneLake availability is turned on, you can rename tables in KQLDB1."],"ans":[true,false,false],"exp":"The KQL database retention policy also applies to the data in OneLake. Microsoft documents that there is no extra storage cost to turn on OneLake availability. Renaming tables, altering column types, applying row-level security and deleting or purging data are blocked while the feature is on.","why":["Yes. The data retention policy is applied to the OneLake data as well.","No. There is no extra storage cost for OneLake availability.","No. Renaming tables is blocked until you turn off OneLake availability."],"ar":"سياسة الاحتفاظ تنطبق على نسخة OneLake، ولا توجد تكلفة تخزين إضافية، ولا يمكن إعادة تسمية الجداول أثناء التفعيل.","ref":[{"t":"Turn on OneLake availability for an eventhouse","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/real-time-intelligence/event-house-onelake-availability"}]},{"id":"PD-G-029","d":"prep","s":"get","type":"order","diff":2,"tag":"Semantic model OneLake integration","q":"You have an Import mode semantic model named SM1 in a workspace on an F64 capacity. Data scientists need to read the SM1 import tables from Spark in a lakehouse named LH1 without building another ETL process. Which three actions should you perform in sequence?","opts":["In LH1, create a shortcut by using the Microsoft OneLake source and select the SM1 tables","Turn on OneLake integration in the SM1 settings","Convert the SM1 tables to DirectQuery storage mode","Run a manual or scheduled refresh of SM1","Turn on OneLake availability for SM1 in Real-Time hub","Switch SM1 to Direct Lake on SQL analytics endpoint"],"ans":[1,3,0],"exp":"OneLake integration for semantic models writes Import table data to Delta tables in OneLake. After you turn it on in the model settings, at least one manual or scheduled refresh must run before data is written. Then you can create OneLake shortcuts in a lakehouse that point to the exported tables.","why":["Step 3.","Step 1.","DirectQuery tables cannot be exported to Delta, so this would remove data from the export.","Step 2.","OneLake availability is an eventhouse feature, not a semantic model setting.","Export applies to Import tables. Direct Lake models read Delta tables and do not export import data."],"ar":"فعّل OneLake integration، ثم نفّذ refresh ليُكتب Delta، ثم أنشئ shortcut في الـ lakehouse.","ref":[{"t":"OneLake integration for semantic models","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/enterprise/powerbi/onelake-integration-overview"}]},{"id":"PD-G-030","d":"prep","s":"get","type":"multi","n":2,"diff":3,"tag":"Semantic model OneLake integration","q":"You have a composite semantic model named SM1 on a Fabric capacity with OneLake integration turned on. SM1 contains an Import table named Sales, an Import table named Customer, a DirectQuery table named Inventory, a calculation group table named TimeIntel, and several measures. After a refresh, which two objects will NOT be available as exported Delta tables in OneLake? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. Select TWO.","opts":["Sales","Inventory","TimeIntel","Customer"],"ans":[1,2],"exp":"OneLake integration exports only Import mode table data. Measures, DirectQuery tables, hybrid tables, calculation group tables and system managed aggregation tables cannot be exported to Delta format. The feature also requires a Power BI Premium P or Fabric F SKU and is not supported on Pro or Premium Per User.","why":["Sales is an Import table and is exported.","","","Customer is an Import table and is exported."],"ar":"OneLake integration تصدّر جداول Import فقط، ولا تصدّر DirectQuery أو calculation groups أو measures.","ref":[{"t":"OneLake integration for semantic models","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/enterprise/powerbi/onelake-integration-overview"}]},{"id":"PD-T-001","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"SQL analytics endpoint views","q":"You have a Fabric workspace named WS1 that contains a lakehouse named LH1. Analysts query LH1 by using its SQL analytics endpoint. You need to give the analysts a reusable object that returns only completed orders from the Sales table. The solution must not copy data and must not require Spark. What should you create in the SQL analytics endpoint?","opts":["A view","A table created by using CREATE TABLE AS SELECT","An AFTER INSERT trigger on the Sales table","A materialized view"],"ans":0,"exp":"The SQL analytics endpoint is read-only over the Delta tables, but you can still create T-SQL views, functions and stored procedures on top of them. A view with a WHERE clause gives analysts a reusable, filtered projection without copying data. Creating tables and running DML are supported only in a Fabric Warehouse.","why":["","CTAS creates and loads a table. Creating tables is supported only in Warehouse, not in the SQL analytics endpoint of a lakehouse.","Triggers are not supported in Fabric Data Warehouse or the SQL analytics endpoint.","Materialized views are listed as unsupported in the Fabric T-SQL surface area."],"ar":"في SQL analytics endpoint للـ lakehouse يمكنك إنشاء view أو function أو stored procedure، لكن لا يمكنك إنشاء جداول أو تعديل البيانات.","ref":[{"t":"T-SQL surface area in Fabric Data Warehouse","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/tsql-surface-area"},{"t":"What is the SQL analytics endpoint for a lakehouse?","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-engineering/lakehouse-sql-analytics-endpoint"}]},{"id":"PD-T-002","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"yesno","diff":2,"tag":"SQL analytics endpoint limits","q":"You have a Fabric lakehouse named LH1 that contains a Delta table named Customer. You connect to the SQL analytics endpoint of LH1 by using SQL Server Management Studio.<br><br>For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.","opts":["You can create a stored procedure in the SQL analytics endpoint of LH1.","You can run an UPDATE statement against dbo.Customer from the SQL analytics endpoint of LH1.","You can run CREATE TABLE AS SELECT in the SQL analytics endpoint to persist a new table in LH1."],"ans":[true,false,false],"exp":"The SQL analytics endpoint operates in read-only mode over the lakehouse Delta tables. You can extend it with your own schemas, views, functions and stored procedures, and apply SQL security. To insert, update or delete data, or to create new tables, use Spark in the lakehouse or use a Fabric Warehouse.","why":["Yes. Stored procedures, views and functions can be created in the endpoint to encapsulate logic.","No. The endpoint is read-only over Delta tables. Use Spark to modify lakehouse data.","No. CREATE TABLE and DML are supported only in Warehouse, not in the SQL analytics endpoint."],"ar":"الـ SQL analytics endpoint للقراءة فقط على جداول Delta: مسموح views وfunctions وstored procedures، وغير مسموح UPDATE أو CTAS.","ref":[{"t":"What is the SQL analytics endpoint for a lakehouse?","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-engineering/lakehouse-sql-analytics-endpoint"},{"t":"T-SQL surface area in Fabric Data Warehouse","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/tsql-surface-area"}]},{"id":"PD-T-003","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"single","diff":3,"tag":"Table-valued functions","q":"You have a Fabric warehouse named WH1 that contains a table named dbo.Sales. You need to create a reusable object that meets the following requirements:<br>- Accepts a parameter named @Region.<br>- Returns only the rows for that region.<br>- Can be referenced in the FROM clause of a query and joined to other tables.<br><br>What should you create?","opts":["An inline table-valued function","A view","A stored procedure","A scalar user-defined function"],"ans":0,"exp":"An inline table-valued function (TVF) returns a table from a single SELECT statement, accepts parameters, and can be used wherever a table expression is allowed, including the FROM clause and joins. This makes it ideal for parameterized filtering logic. Fabric Warehouse and the SQL analytics endpoint support creating functions in T-SQL.","why":["","A view cannot accept parameters, so it cannot filter by a caller-supplied region.","A stored procedure can accept parameters, but its result set cannot be referenced in the FROM clause or joined like a table.","A scalar UDF returns a single value, not a set of rows. Scalar UDFs are also currently a preview feature in Fabric Data Warehouse."],"ar":"عندما تحتاج كائن يقبل parameter ويُستخدم داخل FROM مثل جدول، استخدم inline table-valued function وليس view أو stored procedure.","ref":[{"t":"User-defined functions","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/user-defined-functions/user-defined-functions"},{"t":"Create a scalar user-defined function (preview)","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/how-to-inline-udf"}]},{"id":"PD-T-004","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Stored procedures","q":"You have a Fabric warehouse named WH1. A pipeline loads new rows into a staging table named stg.Orders every hour. After each load, you must apply business rules and move the cleansed rows into dbo.Orders. What should you implement in WH1?","opts":["A stored procedure that the pipeline runs after the load activity","An AFTER INSERT trigger on stg.Orders","A synonym that points dbo.Orders to stg.Orders","A materialized view over stg.Orders"],"ans":0,"exp":"Fabric Warehouse supports T-SQL stored procedures, which can contain INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE and MERGE logic. A pipeline can call the procedure after the copy activity completes, giving an orchestrated, repeatable transformation step. Triggers, synonyms and materialized views are listed as unsupported commands in Fabric Warehouse.","why":["","Triggers are not supported in Fabric Data Warehouse.","Synonyms are not supported, and a synonym would not apply any business rules.","Materialized views are not supported in Fabric Data Warehouse."],"ar":"بما أن triggers غير مدعومة في Fabric Warehouse، ضع منطق التحويل في stored procedure وشغّلها من pipeline بعد التحميل.","ref":[{"t":"T-SQL surface area in Fabric Data Warehouse","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/tsql-surface-area"},{"t":"Lakehouse SQL analytics endpoint use cases","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-engineering/lakehouse-sql-analytics-endpoint-use-cases"}]},{"id":"PD-T-005","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"multi","n":3,"diff":1,"tag":"SQL analytics endpoint objects","q":"You have a Fabric lakehouse named LH1. You plan to use T-SQL in the SQL analytics endpoint of LH1 to encapsulate business logic. Which three types of objects can you create? Each correct answer presents a complete solution. Select THREE.","opts":["Tables","Views","Functions","Stored procedures","Triggers"],"ans":[1,2,3],"exp":"The SQL analytics endpoint lets you create views, functions and stored procedures on top of the Delta tables in the lakehouse, and apply SQL security. The autogenerated tables are read-only in the endpoint. To create tables with T-SQL, use a Fabric Warehouse, or use Spark in the lakehouse.","why":["Creating tables is supported only in Warehouse, not in the SQL analytics endpoint.","","","","Triggers are not supported in Fabric Data Warehouse or the SQL analytics endpoint."],"ar":"الكائنات المسموحة في SQL analytics endpoint هي views وfunctions وstored procedures فقط، أما الجداول فتُنشأ عبر Spark أو في Warehouse.","ref":[{"t":"What is the SQL analytics endpoint for a lakehouse?","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-engineering/lakehouse-sql-analytics-endpoint"},{"t":"T-SQL surface area in Fabric Data Warehouse","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/tsql-surface-area"}]},{"id":"PD-T-006","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Surrogate keys","q":"You are implementing a star schema in a Fabric warehouse named WH1. You need the CustomerKey column to generate surrogate key values automatically when rows are inserted.<br><pre>CREATE TABLE dbo.DimCustomer\n(\n    CustomerKey ______ IDENTITY,\n    CustomerID VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL,\n    CustomerName VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL\n);</pre>Which data type should you use?","opts":["INT","BIGINT","SMALLINT","DECIMAL(18,0)"],"ans":1,"exp":"In Fabric Data Warehouse, IDENTITY columns support only the bigint data type. The system generates unique positive values automatically, which makes IDENTITY a natural fit for dimension surrogate keys. You cannot specify a custom seed or increment.","why":["","INT is a common choice in SQL Server, but Fabric Warehouse IDENTITY columns require BIGINT.","SMALLINT is not supported for IDENTITY columns in Fabric Warehouse.","DECIMAL is not supported for IDENTITY columns in Fabric Warehouse."],"ar":"في Fabric Warehouse يجب أن يكون عمود IDENTITY من نوع BIGINT، وهو مناسب لإنشاء surrogate key للـ dimension.","ref":[{"t":"IDENTITY columns in Fabric Data Warehouse","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/identity"},{"t":"Migrate IDENTITY columns to Fabric Data Warehouse","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/migrate-identity-columns"}]},{"id":"PD-T-007","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"yesno","diff":3,"tag":"IDENTITY behavior","q":"You have a Fabric warehouse named WH1. You create a dimension table named dbo.DimProduct that uses an IDENTITY column named ProductKey as the surrogate key.<br><br>For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.","opts":["You can define ProductKey as <code>BIGINT IDENTITY(1,1)</code> so that values start at 1 and increase by 1.","The values generated for ProductKey are unique but can contain gaps between load operations.","You can insert a special member row with ProductKey = -1 for Unknown by using SET IDENTITY_INSERT ON."],"ans":[false,true,true],"exp":"Fabric Warehouse manages IDENTITY values internally: SEED and INCREMENT are not supported, and values are unique but not necessarily sequential because ingestion runs in parallel across compute nodes. SET IDENTITY_INSERT lets you insert explicit values, such as sentinel members for Unknown or Not applicable, and you then run DBCC CHECKIDENT with RESEED to realign the range.","why":["No. SEED and INCREMENT parameters are not supported in Fabric Warehouse. You declare the column as BIGINT IDENTITY.","Yes. Uniqueness is guaranteed (unless IDENTITY_INSERT is used), but gaps and non-sequential ranges can occur.","Yes. The IDENTITY tutorial inserts sentinel rows with -1 and -2 by using SET IDENTITY_INSERT ON."],"ar":"في Fabric لا يمكن تحديد seed أو increment للـ IDENTITY، والقيم فريدة لكن قد تحتوي فجوات، ويمكن إدخال صف Unknown بقيمة -1 عبر IDENTITY_INSERT.","ref":[{"t":"IDENTITY columns in Fabric Data Warehouse","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/identity"},{"t":"Use IDENTITY columns in Fabric Data Warehouse","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/tutorial-identity"}]},{"id":"PD-T-008","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"SCD type 2","q":"You are designing a dimension named DimSalesperson in a Fabric warehouse. Salespeople are sometimes reassigned to a different sales region. Reports must attribute historical sales to the region the salesperson belonged to at the time of each sale, and also show the current region for new sales. Which type of slowly changing dimension (SCD) should you implement for the region attribute?","opts":["SCD type 1","SCD type 2","SCD type 3","A junk dimension"],"ans":1,"exp":"SCD type 2 inserts a new, time-based version of the dimension member when a tracked attribute changes, and expires the previous version. Because each version has its own surrogate key, historical facts stay linked to the version that was valid when they occurred. This preserves an accurate historical perspective.","why":["","SCD type 1 overwrites the row, so all historical sales would roll up to the new region as if the salesperson had always been there.","SCD type 3 keeps only limited history in extra columns (for example previous region), not a full version history for every change.","A junk dimension consolidates many low-cardinality flags and indicators. It does not track historical change."],"ar":"إذا كان يجب ربط المبيعات التاريخية بالقيمة التي كانت سارية وقتها، استخدم SCD type 2 الذي يضيف صفًا جديدًا لكل إصدار.","ref":[{"t":"Dimensional modeling in Microsoft Fabric Warehouse: Dimension tables","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/dimensional-modeling-dimension-tables"}]},{"id":"PD-T-009","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"SCD type 3","q":"You manage a dimension named DimStore in a Fabric warehouse. When a store is moved to a new district, the business wants to see only the current district and the immediately previous district for each store. The dimension must not grow by adding new rows for these changes. Which approach should you use?","opts":["SCD type 3: add a PreviousDistrict column and overwrite the existing row","SCD type 2: expire the current row and insert a new version","SCD type 1: overwrite the District column only","Store the district as a measure in the fact table"],"ans":0,"exp":"SCD type 3 tracks limited history with attributes. When the district changes, the existing row is updated: the old value moves to a column such as PreviousDistrict and the new value is written to District. No new rows are inserted, which matches the requirement.","why":["","SCD type 2 inserts a new row for each change, which violates the requirement that the dimension must not grow.","SCD type 1 overwrites the value and loses the previous district completely.","District is a descriptive text attribute, not a numeric measure. Moving it to the fact table does not provide current and previous values on the store."],"ar":"SCD type 3 يحفظ تاريخًا محدودًا (القيمة الحالية والسابقة) في أعمدة إضافية ضمن نفس الصف دون إضافة صفوف جديدة.","ref":[{"t":"Dimensional modeling in Microsoft Fabric Warehouse: Dimension tables","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/dimensional-modeling-dimension-tables"},{"t":"Dimensional modeling in Microsoft Fabric Warehouse: Load tables","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/dimensional-modeling-load-tables"}]},{"id":"PD-T-010","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"order","diff":3,"tag":"SCD type 2 load","q":"You have a Fabric warehouse named WH1 that contains a dimension named dbo.DimCustomer. DimCustomer uses an IDENTITY surrogate key, a CustomerID business key, and the columns ValidFrom, ValidTo and IsCurrent to implement SCD type 2. You are writing a stored procedure to process changes from the source system.<br><br>You need to load new and changed customers correctly. Which four actions should you perform in sequence?","opts":["Load the source rows into a staging table in WH1.","Truncate dbo.DimCustomer and reload all rows from the staging table.","Update the current rows of changed customers: set ValidTo to the load date and IsCurrent to 0.","Insert rows for new and changed customers with ValidFrom set to the load date, ValidTo set to 9999-12-31, and IsCurrent set to 1.","Join the staging table to dbo.DimCustomer on CustomerID where IsCurrent = 1 to identify new and changed members.","Drop the IDENTITY property from CustomerKey before inserting the new rows."],"ans":[0,4,2,3],"exp":"Stage the source data, then match it to the current dimension rows by business key to detect new and changed members. For SCD type 2 changes, first expire the current version (end date and current flag set to false), then insert the new current version. The surrogate key is generated automatically, so each version gets its own key.","why":["Step 1: stage the source data.","Never truncate and reload a dimension with generated surrogate keys: it invalidates fact keys and can lose historical versions.","Step 3: expire the current versions of changed members.","Step 4: insert the new current versions.","Step 2: compare staging to current rows on the business key.","Not needed. IDENTITY generates new keys for inserted rows automatically."],"ar":"تسلسل SCD type 2: staging ثم مقارنة على business key ثم إنهاء الإصدار الحالي (IsCurrent = 0) ثم إدراج الإصدار الجديد، ولا تعمل truncate للـ dimension.","ref":[{"t":"Dimensional modeling in Microsoft Fabric Warehouse: Load tables","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/dimensional-modeling-load-tables"},{"t":"Dimensional modeling in Microsoft Fabric Warehouse: Dimension tables","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/dimensional-modeling-dimension-tables"}]},{"id":"PD-T-011","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"single","diff":3,"tag":"Table constraints","q":"You have a Fabric warehouse named WH1 that contains a fact table named dbo.FactSales and a dimension table named dbo.DimCustomer. You want Power BI Desktop to detect the relationship between the tables automatically. You need to define a foreign key on FactSales.CustomerKey that references DimCustomer.CustomerKey. Which statement should you run?","opts":["<code>ALTER TABLE dbo.FactSales ADD CONSTRAINT FK_Sales_Customer FOREIGN KEY (CustomerKey) REFERENCES dbo.DimCustomer (CustomerKey) NOT ENFORCED;</code>","<code>ALTER TABLE dbo.FactSales ADD CONSTRAINT FK_Sales_Customer FOREIGN KEY (CustomerKey) REFERENCES dbo.DimCustomer (CustomerKey);</code>","<code>ALTER TABLE dbo.FactSales ADD CONSTRAINT FK_Sales_Customer FOREIGN KEY (CustomerKey) REFERENCES dbo.DimCustomer (CustomerKey) ON DELETE CASCADE;</code>","<code>CREATE TRIGGER trg_CheckCustomer ON dbo.FactSales AFTER INSERT AS ...</code>"],"ans":0,"exp":"Fabric Warehouse supports PRIMARY KEY, UNIQUE and FOREIGN KEY constraints only when NOT ENFORCED is specified. Unenforced foreign keys still help modeling tools such as Power BI Desktop detect relationships. Because the engine does not enforce them, your ETL process must check referential integrity when loading fact tables.","why":["","Without NOT ENFORCED the statement is blocked: constraints are supported only with the NOT ENFORCED option.","Enforced cascading actions are not supported. Only unenforced constraints can be added.","Triggers are not supported in Fabric Warehouse."],"ar":"في Fabric Warehouse يجب إضافة NOT ENFORCED للـ foreign key، وهو يساعد Power BI على اكتشاف العلاقات لكن فحص التكامل مسؤولية ETL.","ref":[{"t":"T-SQL surface area in Fabric Data Warehouse","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/tsql-surface-area"},{"t":"Dimensional modeling in Microsoft Fabric Warehouse: Dimension tables","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/dimensional-modeling-dimension-tables"}]},{"id":"PD-T-012","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Denormalize snowflake","q":"You have a Fabric warehouse named WH1 that contains three normalized tables: DimProduct, DimSubcategory and DimCategory. A Power BI semantic model must expose a Category > Subcategory > Product hierarchy. You need to provide the data for the hierarchy with the least development effort and without duplicating stored data. What should you do?","opts":["Create a view in WH1 that joins the three tables and returns a single denormalized product dimension","Import the three tables and build the hierarchy across the related tables in the semantic model","Create a bridge table between DimProduct and DimCategory","Create a many-to-many relationship between DimSubcategory and DimCategory"],"ans":0,"exp":"A hierarchy in a semantic model can only be based on columns from a single table. When the warehouse stores a snowflake dimension, the recommended approach is to deliver a denormalized result by using a view that joins the snowflake tables together. The view does not duplicate stored data.","why":["","Semantic model hierarchies cannot span columns from multiple tables.","A bridge table resolves many-to-many relationships. It does not flatten a hierarchy into one table.","Subcategory to category is a one-to-many relationship, and a many-to-many relationship still leaves the hierarchy spread over several tables."],"ar":"لتسطيح snowflake dimension أنشئ view تربط الجداول وتُرجع dimension واحدة denormalized، لأن hierarchy في الـ semantic model تكون من جدول واحد.","ref":[{"t":"Dimensional modeling in Microsoft Fabric Warehouse: Dimension tables","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/dimensional-modeling-dimension-tables"}]},{"id":"PD-T-013","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"yesno","diff":2,"tag":"Denormalization","q":"You are designing the gold layer of a Fabric lakehouse as a star schema that will be used by Power BI semantic models.<br><br>For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.","opts":["Dimension tables should usually be denormalized, for example by storing subcategory and category attributes directly in the product dimension.","A snowflake dimension stores dimension data across several related, normalized tables.","A hierarchy in a Power BI semantic model can combine levels that come from columns in different tables."],"ans":[true,true,false],"exp":"Dimension tables are almost always denormalized: storing flattened hierarchies costs some redundant storage but improves query performance and usability. A snowflake dimension is the normalized exception, spreading the dimension across related tables. Because a semantic model hierarchy must use columns from a single table, snowflake dimensions should be flattened, for example with a view.","why":["Yes. Denormalized dimensions trade a little redundant storage for better query performance and usability.","Yes. For example Product, Subcategory and Category stored as three related tables.","No. A hierarchy in a semantic model can only be based on columns from a single table."],"ar":"الـ dimensions عادةً denormalized، والـ snowflake هو الاستثناء المُطبَّع، وhierarchy في Power BI يجب أن تكون من أعمدة جدول واحد.","ref":[{"t":"Dimensional modeling in Microsoft Fabric Warehouse: Dimension tables","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/dimensional-modeling-dimension-tables"}]},{"id":"PD-T-014","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"yesno","diff":2,"tag":"Add columns (ALTER TABLE)","q":"You have a Fabric warehouse named WH1 that contains a populated table named dbo.DimCustomer. You need to enrich the table with a new Segment column and remove an obsolete column.<br><br>For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.","opts":["<code>ALTER TABLE dbo.DimCustomer ADD Segment VARCHAR(20) NULL;</code> is supported.","<code>ALTER TABLE dbo.DimCustomer ADD Segment VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL DEFAULT 'Retail';</code> is supported.","<code>ALTER TABLE dbo.DimCustomer DROP COLUMN LegacyCode;</code> is supported."],"ans":[true,false,true],"exp":"Fabric Warehouse supports a subset of ALTER TABLE operations. You can ADD nullable columns of supported data types and you can DROP COLUMN. Adding a NOT NULL column with a default is not in the supported subset, so you add the column as nullable and populate it with an UPDATE.","why":["Yes. Adding nullable columns is supported.","No. Only nullable columns can be added with ALTER TABLE ADD.","Yes. DROP COLUMN is supported in Warehouse."],"ar":"في Fabric Warehouse يمكنك إضافة عمود جديد بشرط أن يكون nullable، ويمكنك حذف الأعمدة عبر DROP COLUMN.","ref":[{"t":"T-SQL surface area in Fabric Data Warehouse","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/tsql-surface-area"}]},{"id":"PD-T-015","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"CTAS aggregate table","q":"You have a Fabric workspace that contains a lakehouse named LH1 and a warehouse named WH1. LH1 contains a Delta table named Sales. WH1 contains a table named DimDate. You need to create a persisted monthly aggregate table by using the following query.<br><pre>____ dbo.AggSalesByMonth\nAS\nSELECT d.[Year], d.[Month], SUM(s.Amount) AS TotalAmount\nFROM LH1.dbo.Sales AS s\nJOIN WH1.dbo.DimDate AS d ON s.DateKey = d.DateKey\nGROUP BY d.[Year], d.[Month];</pre>What should you do?","opts":["Run the statement as CREATE TABLE in WH1","Run the statement as CREATE TABLE in the SQL analytics endpoint of LH1","Run the statement as CREATE VIEW in the SQL analytics endpoint of LH1","Run the statement as CREATE TABLE in the semantic model of WH1"],"ans":0,"exp":"CREATE TABLE AS SELECT (CTAS) in a Fabric Warehouse creates and loads a new table from a query in one parallel operation. The SELECT can use three-part names to read lakehouse tables through their SQL analytics endpoint in the same workspace. The destination must be the warehouse, because the SQL analytics endpoint cannot create tables.","why":["","The SQL analytics endpoint is read-only over lakehouse tables and does not support creating tables.","A view is not persisted. The requirement is a persisted aggregate table.","A semantic model does not run T-SQL DDL."],"ar":"لإنشاء جدول تجميعي دائم من بيانات lakehouse استخدم CTAS داخل Warehouse مع three-part naming.","ref":[{"t":"Ingest data into your Warehouse using Transact-SQL","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/ingest-data-tsql"},{"t":"Query the warehouse or SQL analytics endpoint","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/query-warehouse"}]},{"id":"PD-T-016","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"single","diff":3,"tag":"Power Query Group by","q":"You are building a Dataflow Gen2 in Fabric. A query named Orders contains the columns OrderID, Region and CustomerID. Each row is a unique order, and customers can place many orders. You need to produce one row per region that shows the number of unique customers. You use <b>Group by</b> on Region. Which operation should you select for the new column?","opts":["Count distinct values on CustomerID","Count rows","Count distinct rows","All rows"],"ans":0,"exp":"Count distinct values calculates the number of distinct values in a column within each group, which gives the number of unique customers per region. This operation is available in Power Query Online, which is the editor used by Dataflow Gen2.","why":["","Count rows returns the number of orders per region, not unique customers.","Count distinct rows counts distinct whole rows. Because every order row is unique (OrderID), it returns the order count.","All rows returns a nested table per group with no aggregation."],"ar":"لعدّ العملاء الفريدين لكل مجموعة في Dataflow Gen2 استخدم Group by مع عملية Count distinct values على العمود المطلوب.","ref":[{"t":"Grouping or summarizing rows","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-query/group-by"}]},{"id":"PD-T-017","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"order","diff":2,"tag":"PySpark aggregate table","q":"You have a Fabric lakehouse named LH1 that contains the Delta tables fact_sale and dimension_city. You need to use a PySpark notebook to create a new Delta table named aggregate_sale_by_date_city that summarizes sales by date and city. Which four actions should you perform in sequence?","opts":["Read fact_sale and dimension_city into DataFrames by using <code>spark.read.format(\"delta\").load()</code>.","Run CREATE TABLE AS SELECT in the SQL analytics endpoint of LH1.","Join the fact DataFrame to the city DataFrame on CityKey.","Group the joined DataFrame by date and city and aggregate the measures by using <code>groupBy().agg()</code>.","Write the result by using <code>df.write.mode(\"overwrite\").format(\"delta\").saveAsTable(\"aggregate_sale_by_date_city\")</code>.","Refresh the SQL analytics endpoint metadata before reading the source tables."],"ans":[0,2,3,4],"exp":"In a lakehouse, Spark is the engine that writes tables. You load the source Delta tables into DataFrames, join fact to dimension, aggregate with groupBy and agg, and then save the result as a managed Delta table. Fabric registers the new table automatically so it appears in the lakehouse and its SQL analytics endpoint.","why":["Step 1: load the source tables.","The SQL analytics endpoint cannot create tables.","Step 2: join fact and dimension.","Step 3: aggregate.","Step 4: persist the aggregate as a Delta table.","Not required. Spark reads the Delta tables directly from OneLake."],"ar":"في lakehouse يتم إنشاء الجداول التجميعية بـ Spark: قراءة Delta ثم join ثم groupBy/agg ثم saveAsTable.","ref":[{"t":"Lakehouse tutorial: Prepare and transform data in the lakehouse","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-engineering/tutorial-lakehouse-data-preparation"}]},{"id":"PD-T-018","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Filter groups (HAVING)","q":"You have a Fabric warehouse named WH1. You need to return only the customers whose total sales in 2026 exceed 50,000.<br><pre>SELECT CustomerKey, SUM(SalesAmount) AS TotalSales\nFROM dbo.FactSales\nWHERE OrderDateKey &gt;= 20260101 AND OrderDateKey &lt; 20270101\nGROUP BY CustomerKey\n________ ;</pre>Which clause should you add?","opts":["<code>HAVING SUM(SalesAmount) &gt; 50000</code>","<code>WHERE SUM(SalesAmount) &gt; 50000</code>","<code>HAVING SalesAmount &gt; 50000</code>","<code>QUALIFY SUM(SalesAmount) &gt; 50000</code>"],"ans":0,"exp":"WHERE filters individual rows before grouping, while HAVING filters the groups produced by GROUP BY. A condition that uses an aggregate function, such as SUM, must be placed in the HAVING clause.","why":["","An aggregate cannot be used in a WHERE clause, and a second WHERE clause after GROUP BY is invalid.","SalesAmount is neither in the GROUP BY clause nor inside an aggregate function, so it cannot be referenced in HAVING.","QUALIFY is not a T-SQL clause."],"ar":"استخدم WHERE لتصفية الصفوف قبل التجميع وHAVING لتصفية المجموعات بشرط يعتمد على دالة تجميع مثل SUM.","ref":[{"t":"SELECT - HAVING clause (Transact-SQL)","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/queries/select-having-transact-sql"},{"t":"SELECT - GROUP BY clause (Transact-SQL)","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/queries/select-group-by-transact-sql"}]},{"id":"PD-T-019","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"Power Query merge","q":"You are building a Dataflow Gen2 that contains two queries: Sales (Date, CountryID, Units) and Countries (ID, Country). You need to identify the Sales rows whose CountryID does not exist in Countries so that you can report data quality issues. You select Sales and then select <b>Merge queries</b>. Which join kind should you use?","opts":["Left anti","Left outer","Inner","Right anti"],"ans":0,"exp":"A left anti join returns only the rows from the left table that have no matching rows in the right table. With Sales as the left table, the result contains exactly the orphan sales rows.","why":["","Left outer returns all Sales rows, both matched and unmatched.","Inner returns only the matching rows, which is the opposite of the requirement.","Right anti returns Countries rows that have no sales, not orphan sales rows."],"ar":"لإيجاد صفوف الجدول الأيسر التي لا يوجد لها تطابق في الجدول الأيمن استخدم Merge بنوع Left anti.","ref":[{"t":"Left anti join","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-query/merge-queries-left-anti"},{"t":"Merge queries overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-query/merge-queries-overview"}]},{"id":"PD-T-020","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"multi","n":3,"diff":2,"tag":"Power Query join kinds","q":"In a Dataflow Gen2, you merge a left query named Orders with a right query named Customers on CustomerID. Which three join kinds include the Orders rows that have no matching customer in the result? Each correct answer presents a complete solution. Select THREE.","opts":["Inner","Left outer","Right anti","Full outer","Left anti"],"ans":[1,3,4],"exp":"Left outer keeps all rows from the left table, full outer keeps all rows from both tables, and left anti keeps only the left rows without a match. All three therefore include unmatched Orders rows. Inner returns only matches, and right anti returns only unmatched rows from the right table.","why":["Inner returns only rows that match in both tables.","","Right anti returns only Customers rows that have no matching order.","",""],"ar":"صفوف الجدول الأيسر غير المتطابقة تظهر في Left outer وFull outer وLeft anti، ولا تظهر في Inner أو Right anti.","ref":[{"t":"Merge queries overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-query/merge-queries-overview"},{"t":"JoinKind.Type","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powerquery-m/joinkind-type"}]},{"id":"PD-T-021","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"SQL joins","q":"You have a Fabric warehouse named WH1 that contains dbo.DimCustomer and dbo.FactSales. You need to list the customers that have never placed an order. Which query should you use?","opts":["<code>SELECT c.CustomerKey FROM dbo.DimCustomer AS c LEFT OUTER JOIN dbo.FactSales AS f ON f.CustomerKey = c.CustomerKey WHERE f.CustomerKey IS NULL;</code>","<code>SELECT c.CustomerKey FROM dbo.DimCustomer AS c INNER JOIN dbo.FactSales AS f ON f.CustomerKey = c.CustomerKey;</code>","<code>SELECT c.CustomerKey FROM dbo.DimCustomer AS c CROSS JOIN dbo.FactSales AS f;</code>","<code>SELECT c.CustomerKey FROM dbo.DimCustomer AS c LEFT OUTER JOIN dbo.FactSales AS f ON f.CustomerKey = c.CustomerKey WHERE c.CustomerKey IS NULL;</code>"],"ans":0,"exp":"A LEFT OUTER JOIN keeps every customer and returns NULL for the fact columns when no sale matches. Filtering on the right-side key being NULL keeps only the customers without orders. This pattern is also useful in ETL integrity checks because Fabric Warehouse does not enforce foreign keys.","why":["","INNER JOIN returns only customers that have orders.","CROSS JOIN returns every combination of customers and sales rows, with no matching logic.","c.CustomerKey comes from the preserved left table and is never NULL here, so the query returns no rows."],"ar":"لإيجاد العملاء بدون طلبات استخدم LEFT JOIN ثم WHERE على مفتاح الجدول الأيمن IS NULL.","ref":[{"t":"SELECT examples (Transact-SQL)","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/queries/select-examples-transact-sql"},{"t":"Dimensional modeling in Microsoft Fabric Warehouse: Load tables","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/dimensional-modeling-load-tables"}]},{"id":"PD-T-022","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"single","diff":3,"tag":"PySpark joins","q":"You have a Fabric notebook with two DataFrames: df_stage contains customers from today's extract, and df_dim contains the existing rows of the customer dimension. Both have a CustomerID column. You need a DataFrame that contains only the staged customers that do not yet exist in the dimension, with only the df_stage columns.<br><pre>new_customers = df_stage.join(df_dim, on=\"CustomerID\", how=\"________\")</pre>Which value should you use?","opts":["left_anti","left_semi","left","inner"],"ans":0,"exp":"A left anti join returns the rows of the left DataFrame that have no match in the right DataFrame, and it returns only the left-side columns. This is the standard way to find new business keys before inserting them into a dimension.","why":["","left_semi returns the staged customers that already exist in the dimension, which is the opposite set.","left returns all staged rows, matched and unmatched, and adds the df_dim columns.","inner returns only customers that exist in both DataFrames."],"ar":"في PySpark استخدم how=\"left_anti\" لإرجاع صفوف الجدول الأيسر التي لا يوجد لها مقابل في الجدول الأيمن، مثل العملاء الجدد.","ref":[{"t":"PySpark basics","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/databricks/pyspark/basics"},{"t":"Upsert into a Delta Lake table using merge","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/databricks/delta/merge"}]},{"id":"PD-T-023","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"single","diff":3,"tag":"Deduplicate with ROW_NUMBER","q":"You have a Fabric warehouse named WH1. A staging table named stg.Customer contains several versions of some customers. Each version has a ModifiedDate value. You need to keep only the most recent version of each CustomerID.<br><pre>WITH Ranked AS (\n  SELECT *,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (________) AS rn\n  FROM stg.Customer\n)\nSELECT * FROM Ranked WHERE ________;</pre>How should you complete the query?","opts":["<code>PARTITION BY CustomerID ORDER BY ModifiedDate DESC</code> and <code>rn = 1</code>","<code>PARTITION BY ModifiedDate ORDER BY CustomerID DESC</code> and <code>rn = 1</code>","<code>PARTITION BY CustomerID ORDER BY ModifiedDate ASC</code> and <code>rn = 1</code>","<code>PARTITION BY CustomerID ORDER BY ModifiedDate DESC</code> and <code>rn &gt; 1</code>"],"ans":0,"exp":"ROW_NUMBER restarts numbering for each partition. Partitioning by the business key and ordering by ModifiedDate descending gives the latest version the number 1 in each partition, so filtering on rn = 1 keeps one row per customer. Rows with rn greater than 1 are the older duplicates.","why":["","Partitioning by ModifiedDate groups rows by date instead of by customer.","Ascending order assigns 1 to the oldest version.","rn &gt; 1 returns the older duplicates instead of the latest version."],"ar":"لإزالة التكرار مع الاحتفاظ بأحدث صف استخدم ROW_NUMBER مع PARTITION BY المفتاح وORDER BY التاريخ تنازليًا ثم rn = 1.","ref":[{"t":"ROW_NUMBER (Transact-SQL)","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/row-number-transact-sql"},{"t":"Remove duplicate rows from a SQL Server table by using a script","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/sql/database-engine/development/remove-duplicate-rows-sql-server-tab"}]},{"id":"PD-T-024","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"PySpark duplicates","q":"You have a Spark DataFrame named df in a Fabric notebook. Some orders were ingested twice, so the same OrderID appears in several rows with different LoadTimestamp values. You need one row per OrderID, and it does not matter which row is kept. Which code should you use?","opts":["<code>df.dropDuplicates([\"OrderID\"])</code>","<code>df.distinct()</code>","<code>df.na.drop(subset=[\"OrderID\"])</code>","<code>df.groupBy(\"OrderID\").count()</code>"],"ans":0,"exp":"dropDuplicates with a column list compares only the listed columns, so it keeps one row for each OrderID even when other columns differ. Data Wrangler in Fabric notebooks offers the same operation as Drop duplicate rows based on one or more columns and generates equivalent PySpark code.","why":["","distinct compares all columns. Rows with different LoadTimestamp values are not duplicates, so they remain.","na.drop removes rows with null values. It does not remove duplicates.","groupBy().count() returns only OrderID and a count, losing the other columns."],"ar":"لإزالة التكرار بناءً على أعمدة محددة في PySpark استخدم dropDuplicates مع قائمة الأعمدة، أما distinct فيقارن كل الأعمدة.","ref":[{"t":"How to use Data Wrangler on Spark DataFrames","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-science/data-wrangler-spark"},{"t":"PySpark basics","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/databricks/pyspark/basics"}]},{"id":"PD-T-025","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Power Query duplicates","q":"You have a Dataflow Gen2 query that contains an Email column. After you apply <b>Remove duplicates</b> to Email, the output still contains both Anna@Contoso.com and anna@contoso.com. You need these values to be treated as duplicates. What should you do?","opts":["Add a step that transforms Email to lowercase before the Remove duplicates step","Replace Remove duplicates with Keep duplicates","Sort the Email column in ascending order before Remove duplicates","Change the Email column data type to Any"],"ans":0,"exp":"Power Query is case-sensitive when it compares values, so values that differ only in case are not considered duplicates. Applying a lowercase or uppercase transform before Remove duplicates normalizes the values so the duplicates are removed.","why":["","Keep duplicates filters to the duplicated rows. It does not merge case variants.","Sorting does not change comparison rules, and Power Query does not guarantee that sort order is preserved through duplicate removal.","The data type does not change case-sensitive text comparison."],"ar":"Power Query حساس لحالة الأحرف، لذلك حوّل النص إلى lowercase أو uppercase قبل Remove duplicates.","ref":[{"t":"Working with duplicate values","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-query/working-with-duplicates"}]},{"id":"PD-T-026","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"PySpark nulls","q":"You have a Spark DataFrame named df that contains the columns OrderID, Quantity and Discount. Some Discount values are null. You need to replace the null values in Discount with 0 without changing any other column. Which code should you use?","opts":["<code>df.na.fill(0, subset=[\"Discount\"])</code>","<code>df.na.drop(subset=[\"Discount\"])</code>","<code>df.na.fill(0)</code>","<code>df.dropDuplicates([\"Discount\"])</code>"],"ans":0,"exp":"na.fill (also available as fillna) replaces null values with a specified value. The subset parameter limits the replacement to the listed columns, so only Discount is changed.","why":["","na.drop removes the rows that have a null Discount instead of filling them.","Without subset, the fill applies to every compatible column, such as Quantity.","dropDuplicates removes duplicate rows. It does not handle nulls."],"ar":"لاستبدال القيم الفارغة في عمود واحد فقط استخدم na.fill أو fillna مع subset.","ref":[{"t":"PySpark basics","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/databricks/pyspark/basics"},{"t":"How to use Data Wrangler on Spark DataFrames","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-science/data-wrangler-spark"}]},{"id":"PD-T-027","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"order","diff":2,"tag":"Power Query missing values","q":"You import a report file into a Dataflow Gen2. The Region column contains a value only in the first row of each block of customers. The other rows in the block contain empty text, not null. You need a list of unique Region and Customer combinations. Which three actions should you perform in sequence?","opts":["Select Region and use <b>Replace values</b> to replace empty values with null.","Select Region and select <b>Fill</b> > <b>Down</b>.","Select Region and Customer and select <b>Remove duplicates</b>.","Select Region and select <b>Fill</b> > <b>Up</b>.","Select Region and select <b>Remove empty</b>."],"ans":[0,1,2],"exp":"Fill down only replaces null values, so empty text must first be converted to null by using Replace values. Fill down then copies each region to the rows below it. Only after the region is populated on every row can Remove duplicates on Region and Customer produce the correct unique combinations.","why":["Step 1: convert empty text to null so the fill operation can act on it.","Step 2: propagate the region value to the rows below.","Step 3: remove duplicates after the keys are complete.","Fill up copies the value from below, which assigns customers to the wrong region here.","Remove empty would delete the customer rows that are missing a region."],"ar":"Fill down يعمل على null فقط، لذلك استبدل القيم الفارغة بـ null أولًا ثم Fill down ثم Remove duplicates.","ref":[{"t":"Fill values in a column","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-query/fill-values-column"},{"t":"Working with duplicate values","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-query/working-with-duplicates"},{"t":"Filter by values in a column","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-query/filter-values"}]},{"id":"PD-T-028","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"yesno","diff":2,"tag":"Missing and null values","q":"You are cleaning data for a Fabric lakehouse by using Dataflow Gen2 and notebooks.<br><br>For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.","opts":["In Power Query, <b>Remove empty</b> on a column removes rows where the column is null and rows where it is blank.","In Power Query, <b>Fill down</b> replaces null values with the last non-null value above them in the same column.","In PySpark, <code>df.na.drop(\"all\")</code> removes every row that contains at least one null value."],"ans":[true,true,false],"exp":"Remove empty applies two filter rules: one removes null values and one removes blank values. Fill down propagates the previous non-null value into the null cells below it. In PySpark, na.drop(\"any\") removes rows with any null, while na.drop(\"all\") removes only rows in which all the considered columns are null.","why":["Yes. Remove empty filters out both null and blank values.","Yes. That is the definition of fill down.","No. \"all\" removes only rows where every column (or every column in the subset) is null. Use \"any\" to drop rows with at least one null."],"ar":"Remove empty يزيل null والفراغ، وFill down يملأ null بالقيمة السابقة، وna.drop(\"all\") يحذف فقط الصفوف الفارغة بالكامل.","ref":[{"t":"Filter by values in a column","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-query/filter-values"},{"t":"Fill values in a column","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-query/fill-values-column"},{"t":"PySpark basics","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/databricks/pyspark/basics"}]},{"id":"PD-T-029","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Convert types (T-SQL)","q":"You have a Fabric warehouse named WH1. A staging table named stg.Orders has an Amount column of type VARCHAR(50). Most values are numbers, but some rows contain text such as N/A. You load dbo.Orders, where Amount is DECIMAL(18,2), by using INSERT...SELECT. The load must not fail, and invalid values must be stored as NULL. Which expression should you use for Amount?","opts":["<code>TRY_CAST(Amount AS DECIMAL(18,2))</code>","<code>CAST(Amount AS DECIMAL(18,2))</code>","<code>CONVERT(DECIMAL(18,2), Amount)</code>","<code>CAST(Amount AS MONEY)</code>"],"ans":0,"exp":"TRY_CAST returns the value converted to the target type when the conversion succeeds, and returns NULL when it fails. It is supported in Fabric Warehouse and the SQL analytics endpoint, which makes it a simple way to load mixed-quality text into typed columns without failing the statement.","why":["","CAST raises an error on values such as N/A, which fails the whole INSERT.","CONVERT also raises an error when a value cannot be converted.","Converting N/A to money still fails, and money is not a supported column type for Fabric Warehouse tables."],"ar":"استخدم TRY_CAST لتحويل النص إلى رقم بحيث تتحول القيم غير الصالحة إلى NULL بدل فشل التحميل.","ref":[{"t":"TRY_CAST (Transact-SQL)","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/try-cast-transact-sql"},{"t":"Data types in Fabric Data Warehouse","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/data-types"}]},{"id":"PD-T-030","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Conditional column","q":"In a Dataflow Gen2, you use <b>Add column</b> > <b>Conditional column</b> to create a PriceTier column that returns Low, Medium or High based on UnitPrice. The query loads to a lakehouse table. You notice that the new column has the Any data type. You need PriceTier to be stored as text. What should you do?","opts":["Add a step that changes the PriceTier data type to Text","Add an index column before the conditional column","Change the data destination update method to Append","Convert the query to a function"],"ans":0,"exp":"New conditional columns do not have a data type defined, so Power Query assigns Any. You add a Change type step (or set the type in the column header) to define Text before loading, which gives the destination a proper string column.","why":["","An index column adds sequential numbers. It does not set the type of another column.","The update method controls whether rows are appended or replaced. It does not set column types.","Converting the query to a function does not assign a data type to the column."],"ar":"الأعمدة الشرطية الجديدة في Power Query تأتي بنوع Any، لذلك أضف خطوة Change type لتحديد النوع قبل التحميل.","ref":[{"t":"Add a conditional column","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-query/add-conditional-column"},{"t":"Data types in Power Query","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-query/data-types"}]},{"id":"PD-T-031","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"multi","n":2,"diff":2,"tag":"Warehouse data types","q":"You are migrating a SQL Server table to a Fabric warehouse named WH1 by using CREATE TABLE. Which two column definitions are valid for a table in WH1? Each correct answer presents a complete solution. Select TWO.","opts":["<code>CustomerName NVARCHAR(100)</code>","<code>OrderTime DATETIME2(6)</code>","<code>Amount MONEY</code>","<code>CustomerName VARCHAR(100)</code>","<code>Quantity TINYINT</code>"],"ans":[1,3],"exp":"Fabric Warehouse tables support a subset of T-SQL types. Use varchar instead of nvarchar, datetime2 (up to 6 digits of fractional seconds) instead of datetime, decimal instead of money, and smallint instead of tinyint. Unsupported types can still be used for variables and expressions, but not for persisted columns.","why":["nchar and nvarchar are not supported for tables. Use char or varchar.","","money is not supported. Use decimal.","","tinyint is not supported. Use smallint."],"ar":"في Fabric Warehouse استخدم varchar بدل nvarchar، وdatetime2 بدل datetime، وdecimal بدل money، وsmallint بدل tinyint.","ref":[{"t":"Data types in Fabric Data Warehouse","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/data-types"}]},{"id":"PD-T-032","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"Convert types (PySpark)","q":"You read a CSV file into a Spark DataFrame named df in a Fabric notebook. The Quantity column is loaded as a string. Before you save the DataFrame as a Delta table, you need Quantity to be an integer column. Which code should you use?","opts":["<code>df = df.withColumn(\"Quantity\", col(\"Quantity\").cast(\"int\"))</code>","<code>df = df.withColumnRenamed(\"Quantity\", \"int\")</code>","<code>df = df.select(col(\"Quantity\").alias(\"int\"))</code>","<code>df = df.na.fill(0, subset=[\"Quantity\"])</code>"],"ans":0,"exp":"withColumn replaces the Quantity column with a new expression, and cast(\"int\") converts the string values to integers. Values that cannot be converted become null, so you might combine the cast with a null check. Data Wrangler offers the same step as Change column type.","why":["","withColumnRenamed only renames the column to int. It does not change the data type.","alias only renames the output column. The type stays string.","na.fill replaces nulls. It does not convert the column type."],"ar":"لتغيير نوع عمود في PySpark استخدم withColumn مع cast، أما alias وwithColumnRenamed فتغيّر الاسم فقط.","ref":[{"t":"PySpark basics","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/databricks/pyspark/basics"},{"t":"How to use Data Wrangler on Spark DataFrames","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-science/data-wrangler-spark"}]},{"id":"PD-T-033","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Filter (PySpark)","q":"You have a Spark DataFrame named df that contains Country and Amount columns. You need to keep only the rows where Country is US and Amount is greater than 1000. Which code should you use?","opts":["<code>df.filter((col(\"Country\") == \"US\") &amp; (col(\"Amount\") &gt; 1000))</code>","<code>df.filter(col(\"Country\") == \"US\" &amp; col(\"Amount\") &gt; 1000)</code>","<code>df.filter((col(\"Country\") == \"US\") and (col(\"Amount\") &gt; 1000))</code>","<code>df.filter((col(\"Country\") == \"US\") | (col(\"Amount\") &gt; 1000))</code>"],"ans":0,"exp":"In PySpark, you combine column conditions with the & (AND) and | (OR) operators, and each condition must be wrapped in parentheses because & binds more tightly than comparison operators in Python.","why":["","Without parentheses, Python evaluates \"US\" &amp; col(\"Amount\") first, which produces an error or the wrong logic.","The Python keyword and cannot be used to combine Column expressions. Use &amp;.","| is OR, so rows that meet only one of the conditions are also returned."],"ar":"في PySpark اجمع الشروط بـ & أو | مع وضع كل شرط بين أقواس، ولا تستخدم and.","ref":[{"t":"PySpark basics","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/databricks/pyspark/basics"}]},{"id":"PD-T-034","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"order","diff":2,"tag":"Dataflow Gen2 enrichment","q":"You need to use a Dataflow Gen2 to load a Sales table into a Fabric lakehouse named LH1. Each sales row must be enriched with the product Category from a Product source and with a calculated LineTotal column. Which four actions should you perform in sequence?","opts":["Connect to the sources and create the Sales and Product queries.","In the Sales query, select <b>Merge queries</b> with Product on ProductID by using a Left outer join, and expand the Category column.","Add a custom column named LineTotal with the formula <code>[Quantity] * [UnitPrice]</code> and set its data type to Decimal number.","Add a Lakehouse data destination for the Sales query that targets LH1, and then publish the dataflow.","Create a OneLake shortcut to the Product table in LH1.","Create a view in the SQL analytics endpoint of LH1 that joins Sales and Product."],"ans":[0,1,2,3],"exp":"In Dataflow Gen2 you first create the source queries, then enrich Sales by merging with Product (Left outer keeps sales that have no product match) and expanding Category. Next you add the calculated column and set its type. Finally you configure a data destination for the query and publish so the refresh writes the result to LH1.","why":["Step 1.","Step 2: merge and expand to add Category.","Step 3: add and type the calculated column.","Step 4: set the destination and publish.","A shortcut exposes data but does not enrich Sales in the dataflow.","A view in the endpoint does not load the enriched table into LH1."],"ar":"في Dataflow Gen2: أنشئ الاستعلامات ثم Merge وexpand ثم Custom column مع تحديد النوع ثم data destination ثم publish.","ref":[{"t":"Merge queries overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-query/merge-queries-overview"},{"t":"Add a custom column","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-query/add-custom-column"},{"t":"Module 2: Transform data with a dataflow in Data Factory","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-factory/tutorial-end-to-end-dataflow"}]},{"id":"PD-T-035","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"multi","n":2,"diff":3,"tag":"Dataflow Gen2 destinations","q":"You create a Dataflow Gen2 from a Fabric workspace. You configure a Fabric warehouse named WH1 as the data destination for a query named Orders. Which two statements are true? Each correct answer presents a complete solution. Select TWO.","opts":["Only a fixed schema is supported, so if you change the query schema you must reconfigure the destination mapping.","You can enable automatic settings so that the table is dropped and recreated whenever the schema changes.","With the Append update method, each refresh adds the dataflow output to the existing rows in the table.","The Replace update method is not supported for a warehouse destination.","Dynamic schema is supported when the update method is Append."],"ans":[0,2],"exp":"When loading into a warehouse, only fixed schema is supported, so schema changes in the query require you to update the mapping manually. Append adds the output rows to the existing table on every refresh, while Replace drops the rows and reloads them. Automatic settings are supported only for Lakehouse and Azure SQL database destinations.","why":["","Automatic (managed) settings are currently supported only for Lakehouse and Azure SQL database destinations.","","Most destinations, including Warehouse, support both Append and Replace. Only KQL databases and Azure Data Explorer do not support Replace.","Schema options on publish apply only to Replace. With Append, schema changes are not possible."],"ar":"عند التحميل إلى Warehouse من Dataflow Gen2 يكون المخطط fixed فقط، وAppend يضيف الصفوف في كل refresh، والإعدادات التلقائية للـ Lakehouse وAzure SQL فقط.","ref":[{"t":"Dataflow Gen2 data destinations and managed settings","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-factory/dataflow-gen2-data-destinations-and-managed-settings"}]},{"id":"PD-Q-001","d":"prep","s":"query","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"Visual query editor","q":"You have a Fabric workspace named WS1 that contains a warehouse named WH1. WH1 contains tables named DimCity and FactSale. A business analyst uses the Visual query editor in WH1 to merge DimCity and FactSale on CityKey. The analyst needs other users to be able to query the merged result as a reusable object that always reflects the current data in both tables, without writing T-SQL. What should the analyst do?","opts":["Select <b>Save as view</b>","Select <b>Save as table</b>","Select <b>Download Excel file</b>","Move the query to the <b>Shared queries</b> folder"],"ans":0,"exp":"The Visual query editor can save the query that has load enabled as a view by using <b>Save as view</b>. You pick a schema you can create views in, provide a name and review the generated SQL. A view stores only the query definition, so every query against it reads the current data in DimCity and FactSale.","why":["","Save as table runs CREATE TABLE AS SELECT and stores a point-in-time copy of the results, so it does not reflect later changes in the source tables.","Downloading to Excel produces a static file outside the warehouse, not a reusable database object.","Shared queries lets others open and run the saved query in the editor, but it does not create a database object that other tools and users can query."],"ar":"استخدم Save as view في Visual query editor لإنشاء view يعرض البيانات الحالية دائمًا بدون كتابة T-SQL.","ref":[{"t":"Query using the visual query editor","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/visual-query-editor"}]},{"id":"PD-Q-002","d":"prep","s":"query","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Visual query editor","q":"You have a Fabric warehouse named WH1. You build a visual query in the Visual query editor that filters and aggregates FactSale by month. You need to materialize the query results into a new table named dbo.MonthlySales in WH1 directly from the Visual query editor. Which T-SQL statement does the editor use to create the table?","opts":["CREATE TABLE AS SELECT (CTAS)","SELECT ... INTO","INSERT INTO ... EXEC","MERGE"],"ans":0,"exp":"<b>Save as table</b> in the Visual query editor saves the results of the query that has load enabled into a new table by using a CREATE TABLE AS SELECT statement. You choose the target warehouse, a schema that you can create tables in, and the table name, and the new table then appears in the Explorer.","why":["","SELECT ... INTO is a T-SQL pattern you could write yourself, but the documented behavior of Save as table is CTAS.","INSERT INTO ... EXEC requires an existing table and a stored procedure; it is not what Save as table generates.","MERGE upserts into an existing target table; it does not create a new table from query results."],"ar":"زر Save as table في Visual query editor ينشئ جدولًا جديدًا من النتائج باستخدام CREATE TABLE AS SELECT.","ref":[{"t":"Query using the visual query editor","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/visual-query-editor"}]},{"id":"PD-Q-003","d":"prep","s":"query","type":"yesno","diff":2,"tag":"Visual query editor","q":"You have a Fabric workspace named WS1 that contains a warehouse named WH1. Users in WS1 use the Visual query editor to analyze data in WH1.<br>For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.","opts":["A user can use the Visual query editor to update rows in a WH1 table by applying a transformation step.","<b>Visualize results</b> is not supported for a visual query whose SQL includes an ORDER BY clause.","A user who has the Viewer role in WS1 can move their visual query from <b>My queries</b> to <b>Shared queries</b>."],"ans":[false,true,false],"exp":"The Visual query editor runs only read-only DQL (SELECT) statements; DDL and DML are not supported. Visualize results currently does not support SQL queries that contain an ORDER BY clause. All workspace users can save queries in My queries, but users in the Viewer role and shared recipients of the warehouse cannot move queries to the Shared queries folder.","why":["No. Only read-only SELECT statements run in the Visual query editor; DML such as UPDATE is not supported.","Yes. This is a documented limitation of Visualize results.","No. Viewers and shared recipients are restricted from moving queries to Shared queries."],"ar":"Visual query editor للقراءة فقط (SELECT)، و Visualize results لا يدعم ORDER BY، و Viewer لا يستطيع نقل الاستعلام إلى Shared queries.","ref":[{"t":"Query using the visual query editor","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/visual-query-editor"}]},{"id":"PD-Q-004","d":"prep","s":"query","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Visual query editor","q":"You have a Fabric warehouse named WH1. In the Visual query editor, you drag the Customer and Orders tables onto the canvas and use <b>Merge queries as new</b> to join them. When you select <b>View SQL</b>, the script shows only a SELECT from Customer and not the merged result. You need the SQL script to reflect the merge step. What should you do?","opts":["Open the context menu of the Merge query and select <b>Enable load</b>, then expand the merged columns","Rewrite the join in the SQL query editor and then return to the visual query","Select <b>Save as view</b> first and then select <b>View SQL</b>","Add a <b>Group by</b> step after the Merge step"],"ans":0,"exp":"When a visual query joins two or more tables, only the query that has load enabled is reflected in the SQL script. To see the merge in View SQL, enable load on the Merge query from its context menu and expand the merged table columns so the steps are reflected in the script.","why":["","The SQL query editor is a separate experience; writing SQL there does not change which visual query is shown in View SQL.","Save as view also uses the query that has load enabled, so it would still use the wrong query.","Adding a Group by step does not change which query has load enabled."],"ar":"في Visual query editor يظهر في View SQL فقط الاستعلام المفعّل عليه Enable load، لذا فعّله على استعلام Merge.","ref":[{"t":"Query using the visual query editor","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/visual-query-editor"}]},{"id":"PD-Q-005","d":"prep","s":"query","type":"order","diff":3,"tag":"Visual query editor","q":"You have a Fabric workspace named WS1 that contains a warehouse named SalesWH and a warehouse named MarketingWH. SalesWH contains a table named store_sales. MarketingWH contains a table named item. From SalesWH, you need to use the Visual query editor to join store_sales to item and persist the result as a view in SalesWH, without writing any T-SQL.<br>Which four actions should you perform in sequence?","opts":["In the SalesWH Explorer, select <b>+ Warehouses</b> and add MarketingWH","Create a shortcut to MarketingWH.dbo.item in SalesWH","Open a new visual query and drag store_sales and item onto the canvas","Use <b>Merge queries as new</b>, select the common key column in each table, and select the join kind","Select <b>Save as view</b>, choose the schema and name, and confirm the SQL statement","Run <b>EXEC sp_addlinkedserver</b> to register MarketingWH"],"ans":[0,2,3,4],"exp":"To build a cross-warehouse visual query, first add the other warehouse to the Explorer by using + Warehouses. Then drag tables from both warehouses onto the canvas, add a Merge queries operation on the common key, and finally use Save as view to persist the query in SalesWH. Items in the same workspace are queried in place, so no shortcut or linked server is needed.","why":["Step 1: makes MarketingWH objects available in the Explorer.","Distractor: warehouses in the same workspace can be queried directly; a warehouse does not hold OneLake shortcuts to another warehouse table.","Step 2: tables are added to the canvas by drag and drop.","Step 3: Merge queries is the Power Query operation that performs the join.","Step 4: Save as view creates the view in SalesWH.","Distractor: linked servers are not used in Fabric; cross-warehouse queries use three-part naming in the same workspace."],"ar":"لإنشاء استعلام بصري بين مستودعين: أضف المستودع عبر + Warehouses، اسحب الجداول، استخدم Merge queries ثم Save as view.","ref":[{"t":"Query using the visual query editor","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/visual-query-editor"},{"t":"Query the warehouse or SQL analytics endpoint","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/query-warehouse"}]},{"id":"PD-Q-006","d":"prep","s":"query","type":"single","diff":3,"tag":"Visual query editor","q":"You have a lakehouse named LH1. You open the SQL analytics endpoint of LH1 and build a visual query with several Power Query steps. When you try to save it as a view, the editor displays a banner stating \"The query is not supported as a warehouse view, since it cannot be fully translated to SQL\". What is the most likely cause?","opts":["One of the applied steps does not support query folding","The SQL analytics endpoint is read-only, so views cannot be created in it","The query references more than one table","The query does not include a Group by step"],"ans":0,"exp":"The Visual query editor supports only the subset of Power Query operations that support query folding. When a step cannot be folded, the query cannot be fully translated to SQL, so View SQL and Save as view are blocked and the banner appears. You can use the Power Query step folding indicators to find and replace the step that breaks folding.","why":["","The SQL analytics endpoint is read-only for table data, but you can create views, functions and security objects in it.","Queries that merge multiple tables are fully supported and can be saved as views.","A Group by step is optional; its absence does not prevent translation to SQL."],"ar":"إذا ظهر أن الاستعلام لا يمكن ترجمته إلى SQL فالسبب غالبًا خطوة لا تدعم query folding.","ref":[{"t":"Query using the visual query editor","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/visual-query-editor"},{"t":"What is the SQL analytics endpoint for a lakehouse?","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-engineering/lakehouse-sql-analytics-endpoint"}]},{"id":"PD-Q-007","d":"prep","s":"query","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"T-SQL aggregation","q":"You have a Fabric warehouse named WH1 that contains a table named dbo.Sales. You need a query that returns each region whose total sales amount for 2026 exceeds 100,000.<pre>SELECT Region, SUM(Amount) AS TotalAmount\nFROM dbo.Sales\nWHERE OrderYear = 2026\nGROUP BY Region\n_______ SUM(Amount) &gt; 100000;</pre>Which clause should you use to complete the query?","opts":["HAVING","WHERE","ORDER BY","QUALIFY"],"ans":0,"exp":"WHERE removes rows before grouping, and HAVING filters the groups produced by GROUP BY. Because the condition uses the aggregate SUM(Amount), it must be placed in a HAVING clause.","why":["","WHERE is evaluated before grouping and cannot reference an aggregate such as SUM.","ORDER BY sorts the result set; it does not filter groups.","QUALIFY is not a T-SQL clause."],"ar":"استخدم HAVING لتصفية المجموعات بعد GROUP BY عندما يعتمد الشرط على دالة تجميع مثل SUM.","ref":[{"t":"SELECT - GROUP BY clause (Transact-SQL)","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/queries/select-group-by-transact-sql?view=fabric"}]},{"id":"PD-Q-008","d":"prep","s":"query","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"T-SQL window functions","q":"You have a Fabric warehouse that contains a table named dbo.RepSales with the following rows: Rep A 500, Rep B 500, Rep C 300, Rep D 200. You run the following query.<pre>SELECT Rep, Amount,\n  DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY Amount DESC) AS Rnk\nFROM dbo.RepSales;</pre>What value of Rnk is returned for Rep C?","opts":["2","3","1","4"],"ans":0,"exp":"DENSE_RANK assigns the same rank to ties and does not leave gaps. Rep A and Rep B share rank 1, so Rep C receives rank 2 and Rep D receives rank 3. RANK would have returned 3 for Rep C because it skips the ranks consumed by ties.","why":["","3 is what RANK would return, because RANK leaves a gap after tied rows.","Rank 1 is shared only by the two rows with the highest amount, 500.","4 is the position of Rep D in the sorted list, not the rank of Rep C."],"ar":"DENSE_RANK يعطي نفس الترتيب للقيم المتساوية بدون فجوات، أما RANK فيترك فجوة بعد التعادل.","ref":[{"t":"DENSE_RANK (Transact-SQL)","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/dense-rank-transact-sql?view=fabric"},{"t":"Ranking Functions (Transact-SQL)","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/ranking-functions-transact-sql?view=fabric"}]},{"id":"PD-Q-009","d":"prep","s":"query","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"T-SQL CTE","q":"You have a lakehouse named LH1. The dbo.CustomerChanges table contains multiple rows per CustomerID, each with a ModifiedDate. In the SQL analytics endpoint of LH1, you need to return only the most recent row for each customer.<pre>WITH Ranked AS (\n  SELECT *,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (_______) AS rn\n  FROM dbo.CustomerChanges\n)\nSELECT * FROM Ranked WHERE rn = 1;</pre>Which expression should you use to complete the OVER clause?","opts":["PARTITION BY CustomerID ORDER BY ModifiedDate DESC","PARTITION BY ModifiedDate ORDER BY CustomerID","ORDER BY CustomerID, ModifiedDate DESC","PARTITION BY CustomerID ORDER BY ModifiedDate ASC"],"ans":0,"exp":"ROW_NUMBER restarts at 1 for each partition. Partitioning by CustomerID and ordering by ModifiedDate descending assigns rn = 1 to the latest row of each customer, and the outer query keeps only those rows. CTEs and window functions are supported in both the Warehouse and the SQL analytics endpoint.","why":["","Partitioning by ModifiedDate would number rows within each date, not within each customer.","Without PARTITION BY, numbering runs across the whole table, so only one row in the entire table gets rn = 1.","Ascending order assigns rn = 1 to the oldest row for each customer."],"ar":"لإرجاع أحدث صف لكل عميل استخدم ROW_NUMBER مع PARTITION BY CustomerID و ORDER BY التاريخ تنازليًا ثم rn = 1.","ref":[{"t":"ROW_NUMBER (Transact-SQL)","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/row-number-transact-sql?view=fabric"},{"t":"T-SQL surface area in Fabric Data Warehouse","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/tsql-surface-area"}]},{"id":"PD-Q-010","d":"prep","s":"query","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"Cross-database query","q":"You have a Fabric workspace named WS1 that contains a warehouse named WH1 and a lakehouse named LH1. LH1 contains a Delta table named Customers. You are writing a query in WH1 and need to join dbo.Orders in WH1 to the Customers table in LH1 without copying data. How should you reference the Customers table?","opts":["LH1.dbo.Customers","WS1.LH1.dbo.Customers","OPENROWSET(BULK 'LH1/Tables/Customers')","dbo.Customers@LH1"],"ans":0,"exp":"Within the same workspace, you can add the SQL analytics endpoint of LH1 to the WH1 Explorer and reference its tables by using three-part naming: database.schema.table. The first part is the name of the warehouse or SQL analytics endpoint item, so the reference is LH1.dbo.Customers.","why":["","Four-part names that include the workspace are not the documented syntax for cross-database queries in the same workspace.","OPENROWSET reads files directly and is not the way to reference a lakehouse table in a cross-database join.","The @ syntax is not valid T-SQL for referencing another database."],"ar":"في نفس workspace استخدم three-part naming مثل LH1.dbo.Customers للاستعلام عبر المستودع و lakehouse بدون نسخ البيانات.","ref":[{"t":"Query the warehouse or SQL analytics endpoint","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/query-warehouse"}]},{"id":"PD-Q-011","d":"prep","s":"query","type":"yesno","diff":2,"tag":"SQL analytics endpoint","q":"You have a Fabric workspace that contains a lakehouse named LH1 and a warehouse named WH1. You connect to the SQL analytics endpoint of LH1 and to WH1 by using T-SQL.<br>For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.","opts":["You can create a view in the SQL analytics endpoint of LH1 that aggregates a Delta table in LH1.","You can run an UPDATE statement against a Delta table through the SQL analytics endpoint of LH1.","From WH1, you can run INSERT INTO WH1.dbo.Affiliation SELECT * FROM LH1.dbo.Affiliation."],"ans":[true,false,true],"exp":"The SQL analytics endpoint is a read-only T-SQL surface over the Delta tables of a lakehouse: you can create views, functions and procedures, but you cannot create, alter or modify tables. DML is supported only in the Warehouse. Three-part naming lets you read from the LH1 endpoint and insert the results into a WH1 table in the same workspace.","why":["Yes. Views, functions and procedures can be created on top of the lakehouse tables.","No. INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE are supported only in the Warehouse, not in the SQL analytics endpoint.","Yes. Cross-database queries can insert data from one database into another by using three-part naming."],"ar":"SQL analytics endpoint للقراءة فقط للجداول لكنه يسمح بإنشاء views، أما التعديل على البيانات فيتم في Warehouse.","ref":[{"t":"T-SQL surface area in Fabric Data Warehouse","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/tsql-surface-area"},{"t":"Query the warehouse or SQL analytics endpoint","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/query-warehouse"}]},{"id":"PD-Q-012","d":"prep","s":"query","type":"multi","n":2,"diff":3,"tag":"T-SQL surface area","q":"You are migrating a set of reporting queries from SQL Server to a Fabric warehouse named WH1. You need to identify which queries will require a rewrite because they use T-SQL constructs that WH1 does not support. Which two constructs should you identify? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. Select TWO.","opts":["A recursive CTE that walks an employee-manager hierarchy","A SELECT statement that ends with FOR XML PATH","Two sequential CTEs defined in the same WITH clause","A LAG() window function with PARTITION BY","SELECT TOP (10) with ORDER BY"],"ans":[0,1],"exp":"The T-SQL surface area article lists recursive queries and SELECT ... FOR XML among the commands that are not supported in Fabric Data Warehouse and the SQL analytics endpoint. Standard and sequential CTEs, window functions such as LAG, and TOP with ORDER BY are supported.","why":["","","Sequential CTEs are supported (standard and sequential CTEs are GA).","Window functions such as LAG are supported in Warehouse and the SQL analytics endpoint.","TOP with ORDER BY is standard supported syntax."],"ar":"Fabric Warehouse لا يدعم recursive queries ولا FOR XML، لكنه يدعم CTEs العادية والمتتالية و window functions.","ref":[{"t":"T-SQL surface area in Fabric Data Warehouse","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/tsql-surface-area"}]},{"id":"PD-Q-013","d":"prep","s":"query","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"T-SQL window functions","q":"You have a Fabric warehouse that contains a table named dbo.DailySales with columns StoreID, SalesDate and Amount. You need a running total of Amount for each store, ordered by date, that starts over for each store.<pre>SELECT StoreID, SalesDate, Amount,\n  SUM(Amount) OVER (_______) AS RunningTotal\nFROM dbo.DailySales;</pre>Which expression should you use to complete the query?","opts":["PARTITION BY StoreID ORDER BY SalesDate ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW","ORDER BY StoreID ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW","PARTITION BY StoreID","PARTITION BY SalesDate ORDER BY StoreID ROWS BETWEEN CURRENT ROW AND UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING"],"ans":0,"exp":"PARTITION BY StoreID restarts the calculation for each store, ORDER BY SalesDate defines the sequence, and the ROWS frame from UNBOUNDED PRECEDING to CURRENT ROW sums every earlier row plus the current row. That produces a cumulative total per store.","why":["","Without PARTITION BY StoreID, the total does not restart for each store, and ordering by StoreID does not order by date.","Without ORDER BY, SUM OVER returns the grand total for the store on every row, not a running total.","Partitioning by date and framing to UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING produces a reverse cumulative sum across stores within a date."],"ar":"للمجموع التراكمي لكل متجر استخدم SUM OVER مع PARTITION BY و ORDER BY و ROWS UNBOUNDED PRECEDING.","ref":[{"t":"SELECT - OVER clause (Transact-SQL)","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/queries/select-over-clause-transact-sql?view=fabric"}]},{"id":"PD-Q-014","d":"prep","s":"query","type":"multi","n":2,"diff":1,"tag":"T-SQL aggregation","q":"You are writing a T-SQL query against a Fabric warehouse that groups dbo.Orders by CustomerID. You need to know where an aggregate function such as COUNT(*) can be referenced directly in this query. Which two clauses should you identify? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. Select TWO.","opts":["The SELECT list","The HAVING clause","The WHERE clause","The GROUP BY clause"],"ans":[0,1],"exp":"In a grouped query, aggregate functions calculate a summary value per group and can appear in the SELECT list and in the HAVING clause, which filters groups. WHERE removes individual rows before grouping, so it cannot contain aggregates, and GROUP BY expressions cannot include aggregate functions.","why":["","","WHERE is evaluated before grouping and cannot reference aggregates.","You can group by an expression only if it does not include aggregate functions."],"ar":"دوال التجميع تستخدم في SELECT و HAVING، ولا تستخدم في WHERE أو GROUP BY.","ref":[{"t":"SELECT - GROUP BY clause (Transact-SQL)","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/queries/select-group-by-transact-sql?view=fabric"},{"t":"SELECT examples (Transact-SQL)","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/queries/select-examples-transact-sql?view=fabric"}]},{"id":"PD-Q-015","d":"prep","s":"query","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"T-SQL window functions","q":"You have a Fabric warehouse that contains a table named dbo.MonthlyRevenue with one row per month for 2026. You run the following query.<pre>SELECT MonthNo, Revenue,\n  LAG(Revenue) OVER (ORDER BY MonthNo) AS PrevRevenue\nFROM dbo.MonthlyRevenue;</pre>What value does PrevRevenue return for MonthNo 1?","opts":["NULL","0","The Revenue value of MonthNo 1","The Revenue value of MonthNo 12"],"ans":0,"exp":"LAG returns the value from a previous row at the given offset, which defaults to 1. When the offset goes beyond the start of the partition and no default argument is supplied, LAG returns NULL. To return 0 instead, specify it as the third argument, for example LAG(Revenue, 1, 0).","why":["","0 is returned only if you pass 0 as the default argument.","LAG reads a previous row, not the current row.","LAG does not wrap around to the last row of the partition."],"ar":"LAG يعيد NULL للصف الأول ما لم تحدد قيمة default مثل LAG(Revenue, 1, 0).","ref":[{"t":"LAG (Transact-SQL)","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/lag-transact-sql?view=fabric"}]},{"id":"PD-Q-016","d":"prep","s":"query","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"KQL summarize bin","q":"You have an eventhouse named EH1 that contains a KQL database with a table named Telemetry. You run the following query in a KQL queryset.<pre>Telemetry\n| where Timestamp &gt; ago(1d)\n| summarize Events = count() by bin(Timestamp, 1h)</pre>What does the query return?","opts":["One row per hour for the last 24 hours, with the number of events in each hour","One row per day for all data, with the number of events in each day","The first 24 rows of the table sorted by Timestamp","A single row with the total number of events in the last hour"],"ans":0,"exp":"ago(1d) returns the time one day before the query runs, so where keeps the last 24 hours. bin(Timestamp, 1h) rounds each timestamp down to the hour, and summarize count() by that bin returns one row per hourly bucket with its event count.","why":["","The bin size is 1h, not 1d, and the where filter limits the data to the last day.","summarize aggregates rows; it does not return raw rows.","The filter covers one day and the grouping is by hour, so there are multiple rows."],"ar":"ago(1d) يحدد آخر 24 ساعة و bin(Timestamp, 1h) يجمع النتائج في فترات ساعية مع summarize.","ref":[{"t":"Tutorial: Use aggregation functions","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/kusto/query/tutorials/use-aggregation-functions?view=microsoft-fabric"},{"t":"KQL quick reference","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/kusto/query/kql-quick-reference?view=microsoft-fabric"}]},{"id":"PD-Q-017","d":"prep","s":"query","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"KQL top","q":"You have a KQL database that contains a table named DeviceReadings with a numeric column named Temperature. You run the following query.<pre>DeviceReadings\n| top 5 by Temperature</pre>What does the query return?","opts":["The five rows with the highest Temperature values, with nulls last","The five rows with the lowest Temperature values","Any five rows, in no particular order","The five distinct Temperature values that occur most often"],"ans":0,"exp":"The top operator returns the first N records sorted by the specified expression. The default sort order is desc, and the default for desc is nulls last, so the query returns the five highest temperatures. top 5 by Temperature is equivalent to sort by Temperature | take 5.","why":["","Lowest values require top 5 by Temperature asc.","That describes take 5 without sorting, not top.","Most frequent values require summarize count() by Temperature followed by top."],"ar":"top في KQL يرتب تنازليًا بشكل افتراضي مع nulls last، لذلك top 5 by يعيد أعلى خمس قيم.","ref":[{"t":"top operator","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/kusto/query/top-operator?view=microsoft-fabric"}]},{"id":"PD-Q-018","d":"prep","s":"query","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"KQL render","q":"You have a KQL database that contains a table named Orders. You need to display the daily order count for the last 30 days as a line chart whose x-axis is time.<pre>Orders\n| where OrderTime &gt; ago(30d)\n| summarize OrderCount = count() by bin(OrderTime, 1d)\n| _______</pre>Which line should you use to complete the query?","opts":["render timechart","render piechart","project timechart","extend chart = \"timechart\""],"ans":0,"exp":"The render operator tells the client how to visualize the results. timechart is a line graph in which the first column is the x-axis and must be a datetime, and numeric columns become y-axes. The bin(OrderTime, 1d) column provides the datetime x-axis.","why":["","A pie chart shows parts of a whole by category, not a trend over time.","project selects columns; it does not render a visualization.","extend adds a calculated column and has no effect on visualization."],"ar":"استخدم render timechart لعرض النتائج كخط زمني حيث العمود الأول datetime.","ref":[{"t":"render operator","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/kusto/query/render-operator?view=microsoft-fabric"}]},{"id":"PD-Q-019","d":"prep","s":"query","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"KQL project extend","q":"You have a KQL database that contains a table named Sensor with the columns DeviceId, Temp, Humidity and Timestamp. You run the following query.<pre>Sensor\n| project DeviceId, Temp\n| extend TempF = Temp * 1.8 + 32</pre>Which columns are in the result?","opts":["DeviceId, Temp, TempF","DeviceId, TempF","DeviceId, Temp, Humidity, Timestamp, TempF","TempF only"],"ans":0,"exp":"project keeps only the listed columns, in the order specified, so the pipeline continues with DeviceId and Temp. extend then appends a calculated column, TempF, and keeps the existing columns. The result has DeviceId, Temp and TempF.","why":["","extend does not remove existing columns, so Temp remains.","Humidity and Timestamp were removed by project before extend ran.","extend appends to the input columns; it does not replace them."],"ar":"project يختار الأعمدة المطلوبة فقط، و extend يضيف عمودًا محسوبًا مع الإبقاء على الأعمدة الحالية.","ref":[{"t":"extend operator","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/kusto/query/extend-operator?view=microsoft-fabric"},{"t":"KQL quick reference","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/kusto/query/kql-quick-reference?view=microsoft-fabric"}]},{"id":"PD-Q-020","d":"prep","s":"query","type":"single","diff":3,"tag":"KQL join","q":"You have a KQL database that contains tables named Sessions and Clicks. Sessions can contain several rows with the same SessionId. You run the following query and notice that some Sessions rows with a matching SessionId are missing from the output.<pre>Sessions\n| join (Clicks) on SessionId</pre>You need every matching row from both tables to be returned. What should you do?","opts":["Change the query to <code>join kind=inner</code>","Change the query to <code>join kind=leftsemi</code>","Change the query to <code>join kind=leftanti</code>","Add <code>| distinct SessionId</code> before the join"],"ans":0,"exp":"When kind is not specified, KQL uses the innerunique flavor, which deduplicates the left table on the join key before matching, keeping only one row per key. kind=inner is a standard inner join that returns all matching combinations of rows from both tables.","why":["","leftsemi returns only columns from the left table for matching rows, and does not return matches from the right table.","leftanti returns left rows that have no match, which is the opposite of the requirement.","distinct removes even more duplicate left rows, making the problem worse."],"ar":"الـ join الافتراضي في KQL هو innerunique ويزيل التكرار من الجدول الأيسر، استخدم kind=inner لإرجاع كل التطابقات.","ref":[{"t":"join operator","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/kusto/query/join-operator?view=microsoft-fabric"},{"t":"innerunique join","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/kusto/query/join-innerunique?view=microsoft-fabric"}]},{"id":"PD-Q-021","d":"prep","s":"query","type":"yesno","diff":2,"tag":"KQL where","q":"You query a KQL database in Fabric by using a KQL queryset. A table named Logs contains a string column named Message.<br>For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.","opts":["<code>Logs | where Message has \"timeout\"</code> matches a row whose Message is \"Connection TIMEOUT occurred\".","<code>Logs | take 10</code> returns the 10 most recent rows by ingestion time.","<code>Logs | where Message contains \"time\"</code> matches a row whose Message is \"Connection timeout occurred\"."],"ans":[true,false,true],"exp":"has looks for a whole term and is case-insensitive, so \"timeout\" matches \"TIMEOUT\". contains looks for any substring match, so \"time\" matches inside \"timeout\". take returns the specified number of records without any guaranteed order; use top or sort to get the most recent rows.","why":["Yes. has matches whole terms and is not case-sensitive (has_cs is the case-sensitive version).","No. take (or limit) returns arbitrary rows and is mainly for testing; it does not sort.","Yes. contains matches any substring."],"ar":"has يبحث عن كلمة كاملة بدون حساسية لحالة الأحرف، و contains يبحث عن أي جزء من النص، و take لا يرتب النتائج.","ref":[{"t":"KQL quick reference","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/kusto/query/kql-quick-reference?view=microsoft-fabric"},{"t":"has_cs operator","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/kusto/query/has-cs-operator?view=microsoft-fabric"}]},{"id":"PD-Q-022","d":"prep","s":"query","type":"order","diff":2,"tag":"KQL query pipeline","q":"You have an eventhouse that contains a table named Payments with columns Amount, Region and PaymentTime. You need to write a KQL query that shows the average payment amount per day for the last 7 days as a time chart. The query must filter as early as possible.<br>Which three operators should you add in sequence after <code>Payments</code>?","opts":["<code>| where PaymentTime &gt; ago(7d)</code>","<code>| summarize AvgAmount = avg(Amount) by bin(PaymentTime, 1d)</code>","<code>| render timechart</code>","<code>| top 7 by PaymentTime</code>","<code>| project-away Amount</code>"],"ans":[0,1,2],"exp":"KQL processes operators in pipe order. Filter first with where and ago(7d) to reduce the data processed by later steps, then aggregate by daily bins with summarize, and finally use render timechart to visualize the datetime column on the x-axis.","why":["Step 1: filtering early improves performance.","Step 2: summarize with bin creates one row per day.","Step 3: render must be the last operator to visualize the result.","Distractor: top 7 returns seven raw rows, not seven days of aggregates.","Distractor: removing Amount would make the average impossible."],"ar":"رتب استعلام KQL: where للتصفية أولًا، ثم summarize مع bin، ثم render timechart في النهاية.","ref":[{"t":"Tutorial: Use aggregation functions","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/kusto/query/tutorials/use-aggregation-functions?view=microsoft-fabric"},{"t":"render operator","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/kusto/query/render-operator?view=microsoft-fabric"}]},{"id":"PD-Q-023","d":"prep","s":"query","type":"multi","n":2,"diff":1,"tag":"KQL project","q":"You have a KQL database that contains a table named Events with 40 columns. You need to write a query that returns fewer columns than the source table, without aggregating the data. Which two operators can you use? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. Select TWO.","opts":["project","project-away","extend","where","take"],"ans":[0,1],"exp":"project selects the columns to include, in the order specified, and project-away selects the columns to exclude from the output. Both reduce the column set without aggregation.","why":["","","extend adds calculated columns; it never removes columns.","where filters rows, not columns.","take limits the number of rows, not columns."],"ar":"لتقليل الأعمدة في KQL استخدم project لتحديد الأعمدة أو project-away لاستبعادها.","ref":[{"t":"KQL quick reference","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/kusto/query/kql-quick-reference?view=microsoft-fabric"}]},{"id":"PD-Q-024","d":"prep","s":"query","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"DAX queries","q":"You have a semantic model named SM1 that contains a measure named [Total Sales]. In DAX query view, you run the following query and receive an error.<pre>EVALUATE\n    [Total Sales]</pre>You need to return the value of [Total Sales] as a result. How should you modify the query?","opts":["<code>EVALUATE { [Total Sales] }</code>","<code>EVALUATE CALCULATE ( [Total Sales] )</code>","<code>DEFINE [Total Sales] EVALUATE</code>","<code>RETURN [Total Sales]</code>"],"ans":0,"exp":"EVALUATE requires a table expression. To return a scalar value, wrap the expression in a table constructor with curly braces or use ROW(), for example EVALUATE { [Total Sales] } or EVALUATE ROW(\"Sales\", [Total Sales]).","why":["","CALCULATE returns a scalar, so EVALUATE still receives a non-table expression.","DEFINE introduces definitions such as MEASURE or VAR and must be followed by EVALUATE with a table expression.","RETURN is used with VAR inside expressions; it is not a query statement."],"ar":"EVALUATE يحتاج جدولًا، لذلك ضع القيمة المفردة داخل { } أو استخدم ROW().","ref":[{"t":"DAX queries","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/dax-queries"}]},{"id":"PD-Q-025","d":"prep","s":"query","type":"single","diff":3,"tag":"SUMMARIZECOLUMNS","q":"You have a semantic model that contains a Product table and a Sales table. Product contains five colors, but only three colors have sales. You run the following query.<pre>EVALUATE\nSUMMARIZECOLUMNS (\n    'Product'[Color],\n    \"Total Qty\", SUM ( Sales[Qty] )\n)</pre>How many rows does the query return?","opts":["3","5","1","6"],"ans":0,"exp":"SUMMARIZECOLUMNS returns only rows for which at least one of the supplied expressions returns a non-blank value. The two colors with no sales produce BLANK for Total Qty, so they are removed and three rows are returned. To include items with no data, wrap the expression in ADDMISSINGITEMS.","why":["","Five rows would require ADDMISSINGITEMS, because rows where all expressions are blank are removed.","SUMMARIZECOLUMNS groups by Color, so it returns one row per color with data, not a single total.","SUMMARIZECOLUMNS does not add a total row unless you use ROLLUPADDISSUBTOTAL."],"ar":"SUMMARIZECOLUMNS يحذف الصفوف التي تكون فيها كل التعبيرات BLANK، استخدم ADDMISSINGITEMS لإظهارها.","ref":[{"t":"SUMMARIZECOLUMNS","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/summarizecolumns-function-dax"},{"t":"ADDMISSINGITEMS","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/addmissingitems-function-dax"}]},{"id":"PD-Q-026","d":"prep","s":"query","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"DEFINE MEASURE","q":"You open a semantic model named SM1 in Power BI Desktop. SM1 contains a measure named Sales[Margin %]. In DAX query view, you run the following query, which uses a different formula for the measure.<pre>DEFINE\n    MEASURE Sales[Margin %] =\n        DIVIDE ( [Total Margin], [Total Cost] )\nEVALUATE\nSUMMARIZECOLUMNS ( 'Date'[Year], \"Margin\", [Margin %] )</pre>You do not select any CodeLens actions or other buttons. What is the result?","opts":["The query uses the new formula, and the model measure is unchanged","The query fails because a measure named Margin % already exists in the model","The model measure is overwritten with the new formula","The query uses the formula of the existing model measure and ignores the DEFINE block"],"ans":0,"exp":"Measures created with DEFINE MEASURE are DAX query scoped measures. When a measure of the same name exists in the model, the query uses the formula in the DEFINE block, but only for the duration of the query; the model measure is not affected. To save the change, you would use the Update model: Overwrite measure CodeLens or Update model with changes.","why":["","Query definitions can override model measures with the same name; this is a common way to test changes.","The model changes only when you select a CodeLens action or Update model with changes.","A query scoped definition overrides the model measure within the query."],"ar":"DEFINE MEASURE يغيّر المقياس داخل الاستعلام فقط ولا يعدّل النموذج إلا عند اختيار Update model.","ref":[{"t":"DAX queries","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/dax-queries"},{"t":"Work with DAX query view","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/transform-model/dax-query-view"}]},{"id":"PD-Q-027","d":"prep","s":"query","type":"single","diff":3,"tag":"TOPN","q":"You have a semantic model that contains a Customer table and a measure named [Revenue]. You run the following query in DAX query view.<pre>EVALUATE\nTOPN ( 5, VALUES ( Customer[Name] ), [Revenue], DESC )</pre>The fifth and sixth highest customers have exactly the same revenue. Which statement describes the result?","opts":["Six rows are returned, and the order of the rows is not guaranteed","Five rows are returned, sorted by revenue descending","Five rows are returned, and one of the tied customers is chosen at random","The query fails because of duplicate values in the order by expression"],"ans":0,"exp":"If there is a tie in the order by values at the N-th row, TOPN returns all tied rows, so it can return more than N rows. TOPN also does not guarantee any sort order for its output; add an ORDER BY clause to the DAX query to sort the result.","why":["","TOPN includes all rows tied at the N-th position, and its output is not guaranteed to be sorted.","TOPN does not drop tied rows at random.","Ties are valid; they only affect how many rows are returned."],"ar":"TOPN يعيد كل الصفوف المتعادلة عند الترتيب N فقد يعيد أكثر من N، ولا يضمن الترتيب فاستخدم ORDER BY.","ref":[{"t":"TOPN","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/topn-function-dax"},{"t":"DAX queries","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/dax-queries"}]},{"id":"PD-Q-028","d":"prep","s":"query","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"CALCULATETABLE","q":"You have a semantic model that contains a Sales table related to a Product table. In DAX query view, you need a query that returns the rows of the Sales table for products whose color is Red.<pre>EVALUATE\n_______ ( Sales, 'Product'[Color] = \"Red\" )</pre>Which function should you use to complete the query?","opts":["CALCULATETABLE","CALCULATE","SUMMARIZECOLUMNS","TOPN"],"ans":0,"exp":"CALCULATETABLE evaluates a table expression in a modified filter context. The Boolean filter on Product[Color] propagates through the relationship to Sales, so the query returns the Sales rows for red products. CALCULATE performs the same filter modification but for an expression that returns a scalar value.","why":["","CALCULATE returns a scalar value, so it cannot return the Sales table.","SUMMARIZECOLUMNS requires group by columns and does not accept a table as its first argument in this way.","TOPN takes a row count as its first argument and does not apply filter arguments."],"ar":"استخدم CALCULATETABLE لإرجاع جدول مع تعديل filter context، أما CALCULATE فيعيد قيمة مفردة.","ref":[{"t":"CALCULATETABLE","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/calculatetable-function-dax"}]},{"id":"PD-Q-029","d":"prep","s":"query","type":"yesno","diff":2,"tag":"DAX query view","q":"Your team uses DAX query view to analyze Power BI semantic models in a Fabric workspace.<br>For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.","opts":["You can open DAX query view for a published semantic model in the Fabric portal by selecting <b>Write DAX queries</b>.","When Power BI Desktop is live connected to a published semantic model, you can use <b>Update model with changes</b> to overwrite a model measure.","You can use Performance Analyzer to copy the DAX query generated by a report visual and run it in DAX query view."],"ans":[true,false,true],"exp":"In the Power BI service or Fabric portal, Write DAX queries opens DAX query view for a semantic model from the workspace context menu or the details page. When live connected, you cannot view or edit model measures, and Update model options are not available for report measures. Performance Analyzer lets you copy a visual query or run it directly in DAX query view.","why":["Yes. Write DAX queries is available from the context menu and the semantic model details page.","No. When live connected you cannot edit model measures; Update model actions are not available.","Yes. Performance Analyzer includes an option to run the visual query in DAX query view."],"ar":"DAX query view متاح في الخدمة عبر Write DAX queries، ولا يمكن تعديل مقاييس النموذج عند live connection.","ref":[{"t":"Work with DAX query view","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/transform-model/dax-query-view"}]},{"id":"PD-Q-030","d":"prep","s":"query","type":"order","diff":3,"tag":"DAX query view","q":"You open a semantic model named SM1 in Power BI Desktop. SM1 contains a measure named [Avg Order Value] that returns incorrect results. You need to use DAX query view to see the current formula, test a corrected formula grouped by year, and then save the corrected formula to the model.<br>Which four actions should you perform in sequence?","opts":["In DAX query view, right-click [Avg Order Value] and select <b>Quick queries</b> &gt; <b>Define and evaluate</b>","Edit the formula in the <b>DEFINE MEASURE</b> block and add 'Date'[Year] as a group by column in SUMMARIZECOLUMNS","Select <b>Run</b> and review the results","Select the <b>Update model: Overwrite measure</b> CodeLens above the DEFINE MEASURE block","Create a calculated table that contains the DAX query","Publish SM1 and run the query from Performance Analyzer"],"ans":[0,1,2,3],"exp":"Define and evaluate creates a query that shows the measure result and its formula in a DEFINE MEASURE block that you can modify. You edit the query scoped measure, add group by columns, and run it to validate. When the formula in the DEFINE block differs from the model measure, the Update model: Overwrite measure CodeLens saves the new formula to the model.","why":["Step 1: generates DEFINE MEASURE plus EVALUATE with SUMMARIZECOLUMNS.","Step 2: change the query scoped formula and add grouping.","Step 3: running the query tests the formula without changing the model.","Step 4: CodeLens overwrites the model measure.","Distractor: a calculated table stores data in the model and is not needed to test a measure.","Distractor: Performance Analyzer captures visual queries; it does not update measures."],"ar":"في DAX query view: Define and evaluate، عدّل DEFINE MEASURE، شغّل الاستعلام، ثم Update model: Overwrite measure.","ref":[{"t":"Work with DAX query view","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/transform-model/dax-query-view"}]},{"id":"SM-D-001","d":"model","s":"design","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"Storage modes","q":"Contoso has an Azure SQL database that receives new sales transactions every few seconds. You are building a semantic model named SM1 in Power BI Desktop. Report users must see transactions within one minute of them being written to the database. The fact table contains 400 million rows and the database is well indexed. Scheduled refresh cannot run more than eight times per day. Which storage mode should you use for the Sales fact table?","opts":["Import","DirectQuery","Dual","Direct Lake"],"ans":1,"exp":"DirectQuery never imports data into the model. Each visual query is translated into a native SQL query that runs against the source, so results reflect the source data at query time. Microsoft recommends DirectQuery for fact tables that store large data volumes or that must deliver near real-time results.","why":["Import stores a cached copy of the data, so new rows are visible only after a refresh, and eight refreshes per day cannot deliver one-minute latency.","","Dual is intended for dimension tables that are queried together with DirectQuery fact tables. For a large, fast-changing fact table, the cached part would be stale between refreshes.","Direct Lake reads Delta tables in OneLake. The source here is an Azure SQL database, not a Fabric item with Delta tables."],"ar":"استخدم DirectQuery لجداول الحقائق الكبيرة التي تحتاج بيانات شبه لحظية لأن الاستعلام يُرسل مباشرة إلى المصدر.","ref":[{"t":"Composite model guidance in Power BI Desktop","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/composite-model-guidance"},{"t":"DirectQuery in Power BI","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/connect-data/desktop-directquery-about"}]},{"id":"SM-D-002","d":"model","s":"design","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Storage modes","q":"You have a Fabric workspace named WS1 on an F64 capacity. WS1 contains a lakehouse named LH1 that stores 2 billion rows of sales data in Delta tables. Spark notebooks load new data into LH1 every hour. You need to create a semantic model for LH1 that meets the following requirements:<br>- Queries must be processed by the VertiPaq engine for Import-like performance.<br>- The model must not copy the full data volume during each refresh.<br>- Refreshes must complete in seconds.<br>Which storage mode should you use?","opts":["Import with incremental refresh","DirectQuery to the SQL analytics endpoint of LH1","Direct Lake","Dual"],"ans":2,"exp":"Direct Lake loads column data from Delta tables in OneLake directly into memory and queries it with the VertiPaq engine, giving performance comparable to Import. A Direct Lake refresh is a framing operation that copies only metadata that points to the latest Parquet files, so it usually completes in seconds and does not duplicate the data.","why":["Import with incremental refresh still copies data into the model and consumes memory and CPU during refresh, which takes far longer than seconds for large partitions.","DirectQuery federates queries to the SQL analytics endpoint instead of using VertiPaq, so it does not deliver Import-like performance.","","Dual keeps a cached Import copy that must be refreshed, and it is intended for dimension tables in composite models."],"ar":"Direct Lake يجمع بين أداء VertiPaq وتحديث سريع يعتمد على framing للبيانات الوصفية فقط دون نسخ البيانات.","ref":[{"t":"Direct Lake overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-overview"},{"t":"How Direct Lake works","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-how-it-works"}]},{"id":"SM-D-003","d":"model","s":"design","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Dual storage mode","q":"You have a semantic model named SM1 that connects to a Fabric warehouse named WH1. The Sales fact table uses DirectQuery storage mode. The Product, Customer, and Date dimension tables come from WH1 and are currently set to Import. Report pages contain slicers on the dimension tables and visuals that summarize Sales by dimension attributes. You notice that the relationships between the dimension tables and Sales are limited relationships and that queries are inefficient. The Sales table must remain in DirectQuery storage mode because of its size. You need to improve performance while keeping slicers fast. What should you do?","opts":["Change the Sales table to Import.","Change the dimension tables to Dual.","Change the dimension tables to DirectQuery and enable Assume referential integrity.","Set the cross-filter direction of each relationship to Both."],"ans":1,"exp":"A table in Dual storage mode can act as Import or DirectQuery depending on the query. Slicer queries that touch only the dimension are answered from the cache, while queries that join the dimension to the DirectQuery fact table are sent to the source as one native query. Because the Dual tables and the fact table are in the same source group, the relationships become regular relationships instead of limited ones.","why":["Importing the fact table would remove the need for DirectQuery entirely, but the requirements state that Sales must remain in DirectQuery storage mode.","","Switching the dimensions to DirectQuery would make the relationships regular, but slicer queries would then also go to the source and become slower.","Cross-filter direction does not change whether a relationship is limited or regular, and bi-directional filters usually hurt performance."],"ar":"اجعل جداول الأبعاد Dual عندما تُستعلم مع جدول حقائق DirectQuery من نفس المصدر لتجنب limited relationships.","ref":[{"t":"Table storage mode in Power BI semantic models","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/transform-model/desktop-storage-mode"},{"t":"Composite model guidance in Power BI Desktop","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/composite-model-guidance"}]},{"id":"SM-D-004","d":"model","s":"design","type":"yesno","diff":2,"tag":"Storage modes","q":"You are designing semantic models that use different table storage modes. For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.","opts":["A calculated table in a model uses Import storage mode even when its DAX expression references DirectQuery tables.","A Dual table that is related to a DirectQuery table from a different source group participates in a regular relationship with that table.","Refreshing a Direct Lake semantic model performs framing, which updates metadata to reference the latest Delta table files instead of copying the data."],"ans":[true,false,true],"exp":"Calculated tables are always Import tables, even if they refer to DirectQuery tables. Relationships across source groups are evaluated as limited relationships, and a Dual table related to a DirectQuery table from another source behaves as an Import table in that case. A Direct Lake refresh is a low-cost framing operation that analyzes Delta table metadata and points the model at the latest Parquet files.","why":["Yes. Calculated tables always use Import storage mode.","No. Cross source group relationships are limited relationships.","Yes. Direct Lake refresh copies only metadata (framing)."],"ar":"الجداول المحسوبة دائما Import، والعلاقات بين مجموعات مصادر مختلفة تكون limited، وتحديث Direct Lake هو framing للبيانات الوصفية.","ref":[{"t":"Composite model guidance in Power BI Desktop","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/composite-model-guidance"},{"t":"Direct Lake overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-overview"}]},{"id":"SM-D-005","d":"model","s":"design","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"Star schema","q":"You are building an Import semantic model named SM1 from a source that stores products in three normalized tables: Product, ProductSubcategory, and ProductCategory. Report authors need a single drill-down hierarchy of Category, Subcategory, and Product in the Data pane, and the model should be as simple as possible for them. What should you do?","opts":["Load all three tables and create one-to-many relationships between them.","Merge the three queries in Power Query into a single denormalized Product table and create the hierarchy in that table.","Load all three tables and set all relationships to bi-directional.","Create a calculation group that contains one calculation item per level."],"ans":1,"exp":"Snowflake dimensions can be mimicked in a semantic model, but a hierarchy cannot span columns from more than one table, and extra tables add relationship chains and Data pane clutter. Integrating the normalized tables into one denormalized dimension table is generally the better choice and lets you define a hierarchy from the highest to the lowest grain.","why":["Keeping the snowflake design means a hierarchy cannot include columns from multiple tables, and filters must traverse longer relationship chains.","","Bi-directional relationships do not allow a cross-table hierarchy and can hurt performance and create ambiguity.","Calculation groups modify measures. They do not create a navigation hierarchy of dimension attributes."],"ar":"ادمج جداول snowflake في جدول بُعد واحد لتتمكن من إنشاء hierarchy وتبسيط النموذج.","ref":[{"t":"Understand star schema and the importance for Power BI","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/star-schema"}]},{"id":"SM-D-006","d":"model","s":"design","type":"multi","diff":2,"tag":"Star schema","q":"You are designing a star schema semantic model for Fabrikam. The Sales source table contains OrderNumber, OrderLineNumber, ProductCode, Quantity, and Amount. The Product source table has no single unique column: products are identified by a combination of ProductCode and Region. Report users must filter Sales by order number, and filter and group Sales by product attributes. Which two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. Select TWO.","opts":["Keep OrderNumber in the Sales table as a degenerate dimension.","Create a separate OrderNumber dimension table that contains only the OrderNumber column.","Add an index column to the Product query in Power Query, merge it into the Sales query, and use it as the key of a one-to-many relationship.","Create a many-to-many relationship between Product[ProductCode] and Sales[ProductCode].","Store Quantity and Amount in the Product table."],"ans":[0,2],"n":2,"exp":"An attribute of the fact table that is needed only for filtering, such as an order number, can stay in the fact table as a degenerate dimension, avoiding a single-column table. Model relationships require a single unique column on the one side, so when a dimension lacks one you add a surrogate key, for example a Power Query index column, and merge it into the fact query to create a one-to-many relationship.","why":["","A table with only the order number column increases model size and Data pane clutter without benefit.","","Relating dimension and fact through a non-unique column creates a many-to-many relationship. Dimension tables should use a unique ID column as the one side.","Mixing measures into a dimension table breaks the star schema separation of facts and dimensions."],"ar":"استخدم degenerate dimension لرقم الطلب، وأضف surrogate key عبر index column عندما لا يوجد عمود فريد في جدول البعد.","ref":[{"t":"Understand star schema and the importance for Power BI","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/star-schema"}]},{"id":"SM-D-007","d":"model","s":"design","type":"yesno","diff":1,"tag":"Star schema","q":"You are reviewing star schema design principles for a Power BI semantic model. For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.","opts":["Each model table has a property that you set to mark it as a dimension table or a fact table.","A hierarchy in a semantic model can include columns from only one table.","Fact tables should load data at a consistent grain."],"ans":[false,true,true],"exp":"There is no table property that sets a table as dimension or fact. The table type is determined by relationships: the one side is a dimension and the many side is a fact. Hierarchies cannot span columns from multiple tables, which is one reason to denormalize snowflake dimensions. Microsoft guidance says fact tables should always load data at a consistent grain.","why":["No. The relationship cardinality determines the table role.","Yes. A hierarchy cannot comprise columns from more than one table.","Yes. Consistent grain is a core star schema rule."],"ar":"دور الجدول (بعد أو حقيقة) يُحدد من العلاقات وليس بخاصية، والـ hierarchy محصورة في جدول واحد.","ref":[{"t":"Understand star schema and the importance for Power BI","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/star-schema"}]},{"id":"SM-D-008","d":"model","s":"design","type":"order","diff":2,"tag":"Bridge tables","q":"You have a semantic model that contains an Account dimension table, a Customer dimension table, and a Transaction fact table related to Account. A customer can hold several accounts, and an account can have several joint holders. You have a source table named AccountCustomer that stores one row per AccountID and CustomerID pair. You need to report transaction amounts by customer. Which three actions should you perform in sequence?","opts":["Add AccountCustomer to the model as a bridging table.","Create a one-to-many relationship from Account[AccountID] to AccountCustomer[AccountID] and from Customer[CustomerID] to AccountCustomer[CustomerID].","Set the cross-filter direction of the Account to AccountCustomer relationship to Both.","Create a many-to-many relationship directly between Customer and Account.","Set every relationship in the model to Both."],"ans":[0,1,2],"exp":"To relate many-to-many dimensions, add a bridging table that stores the associations, create one-to-many relationships from each dimension to the bridge, and then set one relationship (Account to AccountCustomer) to filter in both directions so that filters from Customer can reach Account and continue to the Transaction fact table. Because an account can have joint holders, a transaction is counted once for each holder, so the sum of the customer rows can exceed the grand total, which is expected for many-to-many analysis.","why":["Step 1: the bridge stores customer-account associations.","Step 2: one-to-many relationships connect each dimension to the bridge.","Step 3: one bi-directional relationship lets the filter continue to the fact table.","Distractor: relating dimensions directly with many-to-many cardinality is not recommended because dimensions should use a unique ID on the one side.","Distractor: making every relationship bi-directional can create ambiguity and hurt performance."],"ar":"لحل علاقة many-to-many بين بعدين استخدم bridge table مع علاقتين one-to-many وعلاقة واحدة فقط بتصفية Both.","ref":[{"t":"Many-to-many relationship guidance","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/relationships-many-to-many"}]},{"id":"SM-D-009","d":"model","s":"design","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"USERELATIONSHIP","q":"You have a semantic model with a Sales table and a DimDate table. There are two relationships: an active relationship from DimDate[DateKey] to Sales[OrderDateKey] and an inactive relationship from DimDate[DateKey] to Sales[ShipDateKey]. A measure named [Sales Amount] already exists. No row-level security is defined. You need a measure that returns sales by ship date when DimDate is used on a visual axis. Which expression should you use?","opts":["<code>CALCULATE([Sales Amount], CROSSFILTER(Sales[ShipDateKey], DimDate[DateKey], BOTH))</code>","<code>CALCULATE([Sales Amount], USERELATIONSHIP(Sales[ShipDateKey], DimDate[DateKey]))</code>","<code>CALCULATE([Sales Amount], ALL(Sales[OrderDateKey]))</code>","<code>SUMX(Sales, RELATED(DimDate[DateKey]) * [Sales Amount])</code>"],"ans":1,"exp":"USERELATIONSHIP engages an inactive relationship for the duration of the CALCULATE evaluation, and the active relationship between the same tables automatically becomes inactive. It can be used only in functions that accept filter arguments, such as CALCULATE. It returns an error if RLS is defined for the table in which the measure is included.","why":["CROSSFILTER changes the direction of a relationship or disables it, but it does not activate an inactive relationship.","","Removing filters from OrderDateKey still propagates the DimDate filter through the active order date relationship.","RELATED follows the active relationship, and multiplying by a key is meaningless."],"ar":"استخدم USERELATIONSHIP داخل CALCULATE لتفعيل علاقة غير نشطة مثل تاريخ الشحن.","ref":[{"t":"USERELATIONSHIP","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/userelationship-function-dax"},{"t":"Model relationships in Power BI Desktop","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/transform-model/desktop-relationships-understand"}]},{"id":"SM-D-010","d":"model","s":"design","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Role-playing dimensions","q":"You have an Import semantic model for flight data. The Flight fact table has DepartureAirport and ArrivalAirport columns. Both are related to a single Airport dimension table, with the arrival relationship active and the departure relationship inactive. Report users need a page that filters by departure airport with a slicer while a table visual groups flights by arrival airport. The model also uses row-level security. What should you do?","opts":["Write measures that use USERELATIONSHIP for the departure relationship.","Create a calculated table named Departure Airport as a copy of Airport, relate it with an active relationship to Flight[DepartureAirport], and remove the inactive relationship.","Set the arrival relationship cross-filter direction to Both.","Change both relationships to many-to-many cardinality and make both active."],"ans":1,"exp":"Only one active filter path can exist between two tables, so a single role-playing Airport table cannot filter by departure and arrival at the same time. Microsoft recommends one table per role, each with an active relationship, especially when RLS is used, because RLS filters never propagate through inactive relationships even when USERELATIONSHIP is used. Renaming columns with a Departure or Arrival prefix makes visuals self-describing.","why":["USERELATIONSHIP works inside measures only, cannot provide simultaneous filtering by both roles, and fails when RLS is defined on the table in which the measure is included.","","Bi-directional filtering does not create a second active path to the Flight table.","Two active paths between the same tables would be ambiguous, and many-to-many cardinality does not remove that restriction."],"ar":"عندما تحتاج التصفية بعدة أدوار في نفس الوقت أو مع RLS، انسخ جدول البعد لكل دور بعلاقة نشطة.","ref":[{"t":"Active vs inactive relationship guidance","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/relationships-active-inactive"},{"t":"Understand star schema and the importance for Power BI","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/star-schema"}]},{"id":"SM-D-011","d":"model","s":"design","type":"single","diff":3,"tag":"Many-to-many relationships","q":"Your semantic model contains a Product dimension table (one row per product, with Category, Subcategory, and Color columns) and a Sales fact table. You add a Target table that stores sales targets by Category and year. You need to relate Target to Product so that targets are filtered by product category, and you must prevent misleading target values when visuals group by Color or Subcategory. Which design should you implement?","opts":["A one-to-many relationship from Product[Category] to Target[Category], and a measure that uses SUM(Target[TargetQuantity]).","A many-to-many relationship between Product[Category] and Target[Category] that filters from Product to Target, plus a measure that returns BLANK when columns below Category are filtered.","A many-to-many relationship between Product[Category] and Target[Category] with cross-filter direction set to Both, and a visible TargetQuantity column.","A relationship between Target[Category] and Sales[ProductKey] with cardinality one-to-one."],"ans":1,"exp":"When a fact table is stored at a higher grain than the dimension and the join column is not a date, Category has duplicate values on both sides, so a many-to-many relationship is required. It should filter in a single direction from the dimension to the fact table. Because grouping by a lower-level column such as Color would propagate multiple categories and misstate targets, you control summarization with a measure that uses ISFILTERED on lower-level columns to return BLANK, and hide the summarizable fact columns.","why":["Product[Category] is not unique in the Product table, so it cannot be the one side of a one-to-many relationship.","","Bi-directional filtering is not needed, and leaving TargetQuantity visible lets users create implicit sums that show misleading values at Color level.","Target and Sales are not related by product key, and one-to-one relationships require unique values on both sides."],"ar":"لربط حقائق بمستوى أعلى من البعد استخدم علاقة many-to-many أحادية الاتجاه وقياس يعيد BLANK عند التصفية بمستويات أدنى.","ref":[{"t":"Many-to-many relationship guidance","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/relationships-many-to-many"}]},{"id":"SM-D-012","d":"model","s":"design","type":"yesno","diff":3,"tag":"Relationships","q":"You are configuring relationships in a Power BI semantic model. For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.","opts":["Row-level security filters propagate through an inactive relationship when a measure activates it by using USERELATIONSHIP.","A one-to-one relationship always filters in both directions.","A relationship with many-to-many cardinality can be configured to filter in a single direction from either table."],"ans":[false,true,true],"exp":"RLS filters propagate only through active relationships and never through inactive ones, even when USERELATIONSHIP is used in a measure. For one-to-one relationships, cross-filter direction is always Both. For many-to-many cardinality, you can choose Single from Table1 to Table2, Single from Table2 to Table1, or Both.","why":["No. RLS never propagates through inactive relationships.","Yes. One-to-one relationships are always bi-directional.","Yes. Many-to-many relationships support either single direction or Both."],"ar":"RLS لا تمر عبر العلاقات غير النشطة، وعلاقة one-to-one دائما Both، وعلاقة many-to-many تسمح باتجاه واحد من أي جدول.","ref":[{"t":"Model relationships in Power BI Desktop","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/transform-model/desktop-relationships-understand"},{"t":"Active vs inactive relationship guidance","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/relationships-active-inactive"}]},{"id":"SM-D-013","d":"model","s":"design","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Cross-filter direction","q":"Your semantic model has Customer and Product dimension tables related to a Sales fact table by single-direction one-to-many relationships. Analysts need a measure that shows, for each product, the number of distinct customer countries that purchased it. You must not change the model relationships because bi-directional relationships caused slow slicers in the past. What should you use in the measure?","opts":["<code>CALCULATE(DISTINCTCOUNT(Customer[Country]), CROSSFILTER(Customer[CustomerCode], Sales[CustomerCode], BOTH))</code>","<code>CALCULATE(DISTINCTCOUNT(Customer[Country]), USERELATIONSHIP(Customer[CustomerCode], Sales[CustomerCode]))</code>","<code>CALCULATE(DISTINCTCOUNT(Customer[Country]), REMOVEFILTERS(Product))</code>","<code>DISTINCTCOUNT(RELATED(Customer[Country]))</code>"],"ans":0,"exp":"Dimension-to-dimension analysis requires the filter from Product to flow through Sales to Customer. Instead of making the relationship bi-directional in the model, Microsoft recommends enabling bi-directional filtering only inside a measure by using CROSSFILTER, which modifies the filter direction during the evaluation of the expression.","why":["","The relationship is already active, and USERELATIONSHIP does not change the filter direction.","Removing product filters returns the count of countries for all products, not for each product.","RELATED requires a row context and DISTINCTCOUNT expects a column reference, so this expression is invalid."],"ar":"بدلا من جعل العلاقة Both في النموذج، استخدم CROSSFILTER داخل القياس لتفعيل التصفية الثنائية عند الحاجة فقط.","ref":[{"t":"Bi-directional relationship guidance","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/relationships-bidirectional-filtering"}]},{"id":"SM-D-014","d":"model","s":"design","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"Iterators","q":"You have a Sales table that contains Quantity and UnitPrice columns but no line total column. You need a measure named Revenue that returns the correct sum of Quantity multiplied by UnitPrice at every level of a visual, including totals. Which DAX expression should you use?","opts":["<code>SUM(Sales[Quantity]) * SUM(Sales[UnitPrice])</code>","<code>SUMX(Sales, Sales[Quantity] * Sales[UnitPrice])</code>","<code>SUM(Sales[Quantity]) * MAX(Sales[UnitPrice])</code>","<code>SUM(Sales[Quantity] * Sales[UnitPrice])</code>"],"ans":1,"exp":"SUMX is an iterator: it evaluates the expression for each row of the table in row context and then sums the results. This produces the correct line-level multiplication before aggregation, so subtotals and grand totals are correct.","why":["Multiplying two aggregated sums gives a meaningless number whenever more than one row is in context.","","Using the maximum price overstates revenue for any row with a lower price.","SUM accepts only a column reference, not an expression, so this is invalid."],"ar":"استخدم SUMX لحساب قيمة لكل صف ثم جمعها عندما تحتاج عملية حسابية على مستوى الصف.","ref":[{"t":"SUMX","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/sumx-function-dax"}]},{"id":"SM-D-015","d":"model","s":"design","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"RANKX","q":"You have a measure named [Sales Amount]. You need a measure that ranks products by [Sales Amount] from highest to lowest in a table visual that lists Product[ProductName]. Products with equal sales must share a rank, and the next product must receive the next consecutive rank number (for example 1, 2, 2, 3). Which expression should you use?","opts":["<code>RANKX(ALL(Product[ProductName]), [Sales Amount], , DESC, Dense)</code>","<code>RANKX(ALL(Product[ProductName]), [Sales Amount])</code>","<code>RANKX(Product, [Sales Amount], , DESC, Dense)</code>","<code>RANKX(ALL(Product[ProductName]), [Sales Amount], , ASC, Dense)</code>"],"ans":0,"exp":"RANKX evaluates the expression for every row of the table argument and returns the rank of the current value. ALL removes the visual filter so that every product is compared. DESC (the default) ranks the highest value as 1, and the Dense ties option assigns the next consecutive rank after a tie, whereas Skip (the default) leaves a gap.","why":["","The default ties behavior is Skip, which would give 1, 2, 2, 4.","Without ALL, the Product table is filtered to the current product on each row, so every product ranks 1.","ASC ranks the lowest sales value as 1."],"ar":"استخدم RANKX مع ALL و Dense للحصول على ترتيب متتال بدون فجوات عند التعادل.","ref":[{"t":"RANKX","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/rankx-function-dax"}]},{"id":"SM-D-016","d":"model","s":"design","type":"single","diff":3,"tag":"KEEPFILTERS","q":"You have the following measure:<pre>Red Sales =\nCALCULATE(\n    [Sales Amount],\n    Product[Color] = \"Red\"\n)</pre>A report page has a slicer on Product[Color]. When a user selects only Blue, the measure still returns the sales for red products. You need the measure to return sales for red products only when Red is included in the current filter context, and to return BLANK otherwise. How should you rewrite the filter argument?","opts":["<code>FILTER(ALL(Product[Color]), Product[Color] = \"Red\")</code>","<code>KEEPFILTERS(Product[Color] = \"Red\")</code>","<code>REMOVEFILTERS(Product[Color]), Product[Color] = \"Red\"</code>","<code>ALLSELECTED(Product[Color])</code>"],"ans":1,"exp":"By default, a CALCULATE filter argument on a column overwrites existing filters on the same column. KEEPFILTERS changes that behavior so the new filter is intersected with the existing filter context. When the slicer selects Blue, the intersection of Blue and Red is empty, so the measure returns BLANK.","why":["FILTER over ALL(Product[Color]) ignores the slicer and still overwrites the existing color filter.","","REMOVEFILTERS explicitly clears the slicer filter, which is the opposite of the requirement.","ALLSELECTED restores the slicer selection but does not restrict the result to red products."],"ar":"KEEPFILTERS يجعل فلتر CALCULATE يتقاطع مع الفلاتر الحالية بدلا من استبدالها.","ref":[{"t":"KEEPFILTERS","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/keepfilters-function-dax"},{"t":"Avoid using FILTER as a filter argument","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/best-practices/dax-avoid-avoid-filter-as-filter-argument"}]},{"id":"SM-D-017","d":"model","s":"design","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"REMOVEFILTERS","q":"You need a measure that returns each product category share of total sales. The denominator must be the sales of all categories, even when a report slicer filters Product[Category]. Filters on other columns, such as year, must still apply. Complete the measure:<pre>Category % =\nDIVIDE(\n    [Sales Amount],\n    CALCULATE([Sales Amount], ______ )\n)</pre>Which expression should you use in the blank?","opts":["<code>ALLSELECTED(Product[Category])</code>","<code>KEEPFILTERS(Product[Category])</code>","<code>REMOVEFILTERS(Product[Category])</code>","<code>VALUES(Product[Category])</code>"],"ans":2,"exp":"REMOVEFILTERS clears filters from the specified column during the CALCULATE evaluation, so the denominator includes all categories regardless of the visual row or the slicer. Filters on other columns, such as year, remain in effect. ALL(Product[Category]) would behave the same way as a filter modifier, but REMOVEFILTERS is the recommended function when available.","why":["ALLSELECTED keeps the slicer selection, so the denominator would be only the selected categories.","KEEPFILTERS modifies how filter arguments combine and does not remove filters.","","VALUES returns the categories visible in the current context, so the denominator would equal the numerator on each row."],"ar":"استخدم REMOVEFILTERS لإزالة الفلاتر من عمود معين عند حساب المقام في نسب المساهمة.","ref":[{"t":"REMOVEFILTERS","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/removefilters-function-dax"},{"t":"CALCULATE","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/calculate-function-dax"}]},{"id":"SM-D-018","d":"model","s":"design","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Window functions","q":"You have a table visual that shows DimDate[CalendarYear] and a measure named [Sales Amount]. You need a measure that returns the sales of the previous year on each row by using a DAX window function. Which expression should you use?","opts":["<code>CALCULATE([Sales Amount], OFFSET(1, ALLSELECTED(DimDate[CalendarYear]), ORDERBY(DimDate[CalendarYear], ASC)))</code>","<code>CALCULATE([Sales Amount], INDEX(-1, ALLSELECTED(DimDate[CalendarYear]), ORDERBY(DimDate[CalendarYear], ASC)))</code>","<code>CALCULATE([Sales Amount], OFFSET(-1, ALLSELECTED(DimDate[CalendarYear]), ORDERBY(DimDate[CalendarYear], ASC)))</code>","<code>CALCULATE([Sales Amount], WINDOW(1, ABS, -1, ABS, ALLSELECTED(DimDate[CalendarYear]), ORDERBY(DimDate[CalendarYear], ASC)))</code>"],"ans":2,"exp":"OFFSET returns a single row positioned before or after the current row by the given delta. A negative delta moves backwards in the sort order defined by ORDERBY, so OFFSET(-1, ..., ORDERBY(Year, ASC)) returns the previous year, and CALCULATE evaluates the measure in that filter context.","why":["A positive delta returns the next year, not the previous one.","INDEX uses an absolute position: -1 is always the last year in the partition, not the year before the current row.","","WINDOW from position 1 to the last position returns all years, so it would return total sales."],"ar":"OFFSET بقيمة سالبة ترجع الصف السابق حسب ORDERBY، مثل سنة سابقة.","ref":[{"t":"OFFSET","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/offset-function-dax"},{"t":"Understanding ORDERBY, PARTITIONBY, and MATCHBY functions","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/best-practices/dax-understand-orderby"}]},{"id":"SM-D-019","d":"model","s":"design","type":"single","diff":3,"tag":"Window functions","q":"A matrix visual shows DimDate[CalendarYear] and DimDate[MonthNumber] on rows and a measure named [Sales Amount]. You need a year-to-date running total that restarts at the first month of each year. Which expression should you use?","opts":["<code>CALCULATE([Sales Amount], WINDOW(-2, REL, 0, REL, ALLSELECTED(DimDate[CalendarYear], DimDate[MonthNumber]), ORDERBY(DimDate[MonthNumber]), PARTITIONBY(DimDate[CalendarYear])))</code>","<code>CALCULATE([Sales Amount], WINDOW(1, ABS, 0, REL, ALLSELECTED(DimDate[CalendarYear], DimDate[MonthNumber]), ORDERBY(DimDate[MonthNumber]), PARTITIONBY(DimDate[CalendarYear])))</code>","<code>CALCULATE([Sales Amount], WINDOW(1, ABS, -1, ABS, ALLSELECTED(DimDate[CalendarYear], DimDate[MonthNumber]), ORDERBY(DimDate[MonthNumber]), PARTITIONBY(DimDate[CalendarYear])))</code>","<code>CALCULATE([Sales Amount], WINDOW(1, ABS, 0, REL, ALLSELECTED(DimDate[CalendarYear], DimDate[MonthNumber]), ORDERBY(DimDate[CalendarYear], DimDate[MonthNumber])))</code>"],"ans":1,"exp":"WINDOW returns the rows between a start and an end position. A start of 1 with ABS is the first row of the partition, and an end of 0 with REL is the current row, which defines a running total. PARTITIONBY(CalendarYear) splits the rows so the window restarts for each year, and ORDERBY(MonthNumber) sorts months within each year.","why":["A relative start of -2 creates a rolling three-month window, not a year-to-date total.","","An end of -1 ABS is the last row of the partition, so every month shows the full-year total.","Without PARTITIONBY, the window never restarts, so it produces a running total across all years."],"ar":"WINDOW من 1 ABS إلى 0 REL مع PARTITIONBY للسنة يعطي مجموعا تراكميا يبدأ من جديد كل سنة.","ref":[{"t":"WINDOW","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/window-function-dax"},{"t":"Understanding ORDERBY, PARTITIONBY, and MATCHBY functions","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/best-practices/dax-understand-orderby"}]},{"id":"SM-D-020","d":"model","s":"design","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Information functions","q":"You have a matrix visual with Product[Category] and Product[Subcategory] on rows. You need a measure that returns the percentage of the parent total: at subcategory level it must divide by the category total, and at category level it must divide by the grand total. The page also has a slicer on Product[Subcategory], so the measure must detect the level of the matrix row, not whether a slicer filters the column. Which function should you use to test the current level?","opts":["ISFILTERED","HASONEVALUE","ISINSCOPE","ISCROSSFILTERED"],"ans":2,"exp":"ISINSCOPE returns TRUE when the specified column is the current level in a hierarchy of levels, for example when the matrix is grouping by Subcategory. It is the recommended function for percent-of-parent calculations because it is not affected by slicer filters in the same way as ISFILTERED or HASONEVALUE.","why":["ISFILTERED returns TRUE whenever the column is filtered directly, including by the slicer, even at category level or on the total row.","HASONEVALUE returns TRUE if a slicer selects a single subcategory, even on category or total rows.","","ISCROSSFILTERED returns TRUE when the column or a related column is filtered, which is even broader than ISFILTERED."],"ar":"استخدم ISINSCOPE لمعرفة مستوى الـ hierarchy الحالي في matrix عند حساب النسبة من الأصل.","ref":[{"t":"ISINSCOPE","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/isinscope-function-dax"},{"t":"Information functions","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/information-functions-dax"}]},{"id":"SM-D-021","d":"model","s":"design","type":"yesno","diff":2,"tag":"Information and window functions","q":"You are reviewing DAX measures written by a colleague. For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.","opts":["<code>SELECTEDVALUE(Customer[Country], \"All countries\")</code> returns \"All countries\" when two countries are in the filter context.","<code>HASONEVALUE(Customer[Country])</code> is equivalent to <code>COUNTROWS(VALUES(Customer[Country])) = 1</code>.","<code>INDEX(0, ALLSELECTED(DimDate[CalendarYear]), ORDERBY(DimDate[CalendarYear]))</code> returns the first year."],"ans":[true,true,false],"exp":"SELECTEDVALUE returns the value when the column is filtered to exactly one distinct value, otherwise it returns the alternate result. HASONEVALUE is documented as equivalent to COUNTROWS(VALUES(column)) = 1. INDEX uses a 1-based position: 1 is the first row and -1 the last row, and a position of 0, BLANK, or out of range returns an empty table.","why":["Yes. With multiple values, the alternate result is returned.","Yes. This is the documented equivalent expression.","No. Position 0 returns an empty table. Use INDEX(1, ...) for the first row."],"ar":"SELECTEDVALUE يعيد القيمة البديلة عند تعدد القيم، و INDEX يبدأ من 1 والقيمة 0 تعيد جدولا فارغا.","ref":[{"t":"SELECTEDVALUE","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/selectedvalue-function-dax"},{"t":"HASONEVALUE","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/hasonevalue-function-dax"},{"t":"INDEX","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/index-function-dax"}]},{"id":"SM-D-022","d":"model","s":"design","type":"multi","diff":3,"tag":"DAX variables","q":"You have the following measure:<pre>Avg Order Value =\nVAR Orders = VALUES(Sales[OrderID])\nVAR Result =\n    AVERAGEX(\n        Orders,\n        CALCULATE(SUM(Sales[Amount]))\n    )\nRETURN\n    IF(ISBLANK(Result), 0, Result)</pre>Which two statements are true? Each correct answer presents a complete solution. Select TWO.","opts":["AVERAGEX iterates the distinct order IDs in the current filter context and averages the total amount of each order.","CALCULATE inside AVERAGEX performs context transition, so each iteration sums only the rows of the current order.","The Result variable is evaluated again each time it is referenced in the RETURN expression.","When no orders exist in the filter context, the measure returns BLANK.","Removing CALCULATE would produce the same result because AVERAGEX creates a filter context automatically."],"ans":[0,1],"n":2,"exp":"AVERAGEX is an iterator that evaluates the expression for each row of the Orders table (the distinct OrderID values) and averages the results. CALCULATE without filters transitions the row context into a filter context, so SUM(Sales[Amount]) is limited to the current order. Variables are evaluated once and then behave as constants, which also improves performance and readability. ISBLANK detects an empty result and the IF returns 0.","why":["","","Variables are evaluated once where they are defined and then reused.","The IF with ISBLANK replaces BLANK with 0.","An iterator creates a row context, not a filter context. Without CALCULATE, SUM would return the total of all visible orders on every iteration."],"ar":"المتغيرات في DAX تُحسب مرة واحدة، و CALCULATE داخل المكرر ينفذ context transition.","ref":[{"t":"Use variables to improve your DAX formulas","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/best-practices/dax-variables"},{"t":"AVERAGEX","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/averagex-function-dax"},{"t":"CALCULATE","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/calculate-function-dax"}]},{"id":"SM-D-023","d":"model","s":"design","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"Calculation groups","q":"You open a semantic model in Power BI Desktop and select <b>Calculation group</b> in the Model view ribbon. A dialog prompts you to change a model property before the calculation group can be created. Which property must be enabled?","opts":["Auto date/time","Discourage implicit measures","Large semantic model storage format","Assume referential integrity"],"ans":1,"exp":"Calculation items apply only to explicit measures, not to implicit measures created by dragging columns into visuals. For that reason the Discourage implicit measures property must be enabled before a calculation group can be created. Power BI Desktop prompts you to turn it on when you add the first calculation group.","why":["Auto date/time controls automatic hidden date tables and is unrelated to calculation groups.","","Large semantic model storage format is a service setting for model size and XMLA write performance.","Assume referential integrity is a relationship property for DirectQuery tables."],"ar":"يجب تفعيل Discourage implicit measures قبل إنشاء calculation group لأنها تعمل فقط مع explicit measures.","ref":[{"t":"Create calculation groups","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/transform-model/calculation-groups"}]},{"id":"SM-D-024","d":"model","s":"design","type":"single","diff":3,"tag":"Calculation group precedence","q":"A semantic model contains a measure named [Units] that returns 20 in the current filter context. The model has two calculation groups:<br>- <b>Adjust</b>, precedence 10, with an item defined as <code>SELECTEDMEASURE() - 5</code><br>- <b>Scale</b>, precedence 50, with an item defined as <code>SELECTEDMEASURE() * 3</code><br>A card visual shows [Units] with both calculation items selected by slicers. What value does the card display?","opts":["55","45","20","15"],"ans":1,"exp":"Calculation groups are combined starting from the highest precedence. The SELECTEDMEASURE() in the highest-precedence item (Scale) is replaced by the next item (Adjust), giving (SELECTEDMEASURE() - 5) * 3, and then by the measure itself: (20 - 5) * 3 = 45. For simple arithmetic, the lower-precedence item is effectively evaluated first against the measure.","why":["55 is 20 * 3 - 5, which would apply the higher-precedence item first.","","20 ignores both calculation items.","15 applies only the Adjust item."],"ar":"تُدمج calculation groups بدءا من الأعلى precedence الذي يغلف تعبير الأقل، فيصبح الناتج <bdi dir='ltr'>(20-5)*3</bdi>.","ref":[{"t":"Calculation groups","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/analysis-services/tabular-models/calculation-groups"},{"t":"Create calculation groups","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/transform-model/calculation-groups"}]},{"id":"SM-D-025","d":"model","s":"design","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Dynamic format strings","q":"You have a measure named [Revenue] used in column charts and cards. Values range from a few hundred to several billion. You need values of one million or more to display as millions with one decimal (for example 12.3M) and smaller values to display with thousands separators. The measure must remain numeric so that charts can plot it. What should you do?","opts":["Wrap the measure in the FORMAT function with a conditional format code.","Create a dynamic format string for the measure that returns a different format string based on the value of SELECTEDMEASURE().","Create a field parameter that switches between two measures.","Set the Display units option of each visual to Auto."],"ans":1,"exp":"Dynamic format strings for measures apply a format string that is calculated by a separate DAX expression, while the measure keeps its numeric data type. This solves the problem with FORMAT, which returns text and breaks visuals that require numbers. Inside the format string expression you can reference the measure by name or by using SELECTEDMEASURE().","why":["FORMAT returns a string, so charts can no longer plot the measure as a number.","","A field parameter lets users choose fields, but it does not format values conditionally.","Display units Auto applies one unit per visual and can override the intended format. Microsoft suggests setting it to None when using dynamic format strings."],"ar":"Dynamic format strings تغيّر التنسيق حسب السياق مع الإبقاء على القياس رقميا بخلاف FORMAT.","ref":[{"t":"Create dynamic format strings for measures","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/create-reports/desktop-dynamic-format-strings"},{"t":"SELECTEDMEASURE","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/selectedmeasure-function-dax"}]},{"id":"SM-D-026","d":"model","s":"design","type":"multi","diff":1,"tag":"Field parameters","q":"You are designing a Power BI report on a semantic model. Report readers must use a slicer to switch the axis of a bar chart between Customer, Category, and Product, and use a second slicer to switch the values between the Sales Amount and Margin measures. Which two statements about implementing this with field parameters are true? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. Select TWO.","opts":["Create field parameters from the Modeling tab by selecting New parameter, then Fields.","Field parameters can use implicit measures, so you can add the SalesAmount column directly as a field.","Put the column-based field parameter in the axis field well and the measure-based field parameter in the values field well.","Field parameters can be created in a live connection report without a local model.","Field parameters must contain only columns or only measures, never a mix."],"ans":[0,2],"n":2,"exp":"You create field parameters in Power BI Desktop from Modeling, New parameter, Fields. A column-based field parameter is used in the axis field wells and a measure-based parameter in the values field wells. Field parameters cannot use implicit measures, so aggregations need explicit DAX measures. A live connection needs a local model (for example DirectQuery for Power BI semantic models) before you can add parameters. You can mix measures and dimensions within one parameter.","why":["","Implicit measures are not supported. Create an explicit measure.","","You cannot create parameters on a live connection without a local model.","You can mix measures and dimensions within the same parameter."],"ar":"أنشئ field parameters من Modeling ثم New parameter ثم Fields، وضع الأعمدة في المحور والقياسات في القيم.","ref":[{"t":"Use field parameters to let report readers change visuals","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/create-reports/power-bi-field-parameters"}]},{"id":"SM-D-027","d":"model","s":"design","type":"order","diff":2,"tag":"Calculation groups","q":"You have a semantic model with explicit measures named [Orders], [Sales Amount], and [Margin]. Discourage implicit measures is already enabled. You need to let users apply YTD and PY calculations to any of these measures from a single slicer, and the YOY% item must display as a percentage. Which four actions should you perform in sequence in Power BI Desktop?","opts":["In Model view, select Calculation group on the ribbon.","Rename the calculation group column to Time Calculation and define items such as <code>CALCULATE(SELECTEDMEASURE(), DATESYTD(DimDate[Date]))</code>.","Turn on Dynamic format string for the YOY% calculation item and set it to <code>\"#,##0.00%\"</code>.","Add the Time Calculation column to a slicer in the report.","Create separate YTD and PY measures for each base measure.","Create a field parameter that contains the three measures."],"ans":[0,1,2,3],"exp":"You create the calculation group from Model view, define calculation items that use SELECTEDMEASURE() as a placeholder for whatever measure is in context, and optionally add a dynamic format string to an item so it overrides the base measure format. Users then apply an item to multiple measures by placing the calculation group column in a slicer or on the matrix columns.","why":["Step 1: create the calculation group.","Step 2: define calculation items with SELECTEDMEASURE().","Step 3: set a dynamic format string on the percentage item.","Step 4: use the calculation group column in the report.","Distractor: separate measures are exactly the redundancy calculation groups remove.","Distractor: a field parameter switches fields but does not apply time calculations."],"ar":"calculation group تستخدم SELECTEDMEASURE لتطبيق حسابات مثل YTD على أي قياس دون تكرار القياسات.","ref":[{"t":"Create calculation groups","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/transform-model/calculation-groups"}]},{"id":"SM-D-028","d":"model","s":"design","type":"single","diff":3,"tag":"Dynamic format strings","q":"A semantic model contains a Currency Conversion calculation group (precedence 5) and a Time Intelligence calculation group (precedence 20). The [Sales] measure has its own dynamic format string. Both calculation groups define dynamic format strings on the items that users select. The Currency Conversion item uses the following format string expression:<pre>SELECTEDVALUE(\n    DimCurrency[FormatString],\n    SELECTEDMEASUREFORMATSTRING()\n)</pre>A visual shows [Sales] with one item from each calculation group applied. Which format string is applied?","opts":["The dynamic format string of the [Sales] measure.","The dynamic format string of the Currency Conversion item.","The dynamic format string of the Time Intelligence item.","The static format string of the [Sales] measure, because dynamic format strings are ignored when calculation groups are combined."],"ans":2,"exp":"Precedence determines which dynamic format string is applied to the combined expression: only the dynamic format string of the highest-precedence calculation group is applied. A dynamic format string defined on the measure itself is considered lower precedence than any calculation group. SELECTEDMEASUREFORMATSTRING returns the format string of the measure in context, which is useful as a fallback when several currencies are in context.","why":["A measure dynamic format string has lower precedence than any calculation group.","Currency Conversion has the lower precedence (5), so its format string is not the one applied.","","Dynamic format strings are not ignored; the highest-precedence one is used."],"ar":"عند دمج عدة calculation groups يُطبق فقط dynamic format string الخاص بالمجموعة ذات الـ precedence الأعلى.","ref":[{"t":"Calculation groups","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/analysis-services/tabular-models/calculation-groups"},{"t":"SELECTEDMEASUREFORMATSTRING","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/selectedmeasureformatstring-function-dax"}]},{"id":"SM-D-029","d":"model","s":"design","type":"multi","diff":2,"tag":"Large semantic model storage format","q":"You manage Import semantic models in a workspace that is assigned to a Fabric F64 capacity. Which two scenarios are valid reasons to enable the large semantic model storage format setting? Each correct answer presents a complete solution. Select TWO.","opts":["A model is expected to grow beyond 10 GB in the service through incremental refresh.","You deploy and update model metadata frequently by using XMLA-based tools such as Tabular Editor.","You need to upload a 15 GB .pbix file from Power BI Desktop.","You need to create a push semantic model for streaming data.","You need to use the model in a Pro workspace with XMLA write operations."],"ans":[0,1],"n":2,"exp":"The large semantic model storage format is required for models to grow beyond 10 GB in the service, where the limit becomes the capacity size or the maximum set by the admin. Microsoft also recommends enabling it for any model managed with XMLA write operations because it improves write performance. It does not change the Power BI Desktop upload limit, which remains 10 GB.","why":["","","The Desktop model upload size is still limited to 10 GB. Models grow beyond it only on refresh in the service.","Push semantic models do not support the large storage format.","Pro workspaces do not support XMLA write operations."],"ar":"فعّل large semantic model storage format لنماذج تتجاوز 10 GB في الخدمة أو عند استخدام XMLA للكتابة.","ref":[{"t":"Large semantic models in Power BI Premium","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/enterprise/powerbi/service-premium-large-models"},{"t":"Semantic model connectivity with the XMLA endpoint","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/enterprise/powerbi/service-premium-connect-tools"}]},{"id":"SM-D-030","d":"model","s":"design","type":"order","diff":1,"tag":"Large semantic model storage format","q":"You create a new Import model named SM1 in Power BI Desktop with an incremental refresh policy on the Sales table. You expect SM1 to reach 40 GB after loading historical data into a workspace on an F128 capacity in a supported region. You need to make sure the first full refresh succeeds beyond the default size limit. Which three actions should you perform in sequence?","opts":["Publish SM1 to the workspace.","In the semantic model settings, set Large semantic model storage format to On and select Apply.","Invoke a refresh to load historical data based on the incremental refresh policy.","Enable query scale-out before publishing.","Set the workspace license mode to Pro."],"ans":[0,1,2],"exp":"For a new model, you publish it, turn on Large semantic model storage format in the semantic model settings, and then invoke a refresh to load the history defined by the incremental refresh policy. For existing models, only the settings step is needed. You can also make it the default for new models in the workspace settings under Default storage format.","why":["Step 1: publish the model to the service.","Step 2: enable the large storage format in model settings.","Step 3: refresh to load historical data.","Distractor: scale-out is optional and actually requires the large format first.","Distractor: Pro workspaces limit models to 1 GB."],"ar":"انشر النموذج، ثم فعّل Large semantic model storage format من الإعدادات، ثم نفذ refresh لتحميل البيانات التاريخية.","ref":[{"t":"Large semantic models in Power BI Premium","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/enterprise/powerbi/service-premium-large-models"}]},{"id":"SM-D-031","d":"model","s":"design","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Composite models","q":"A central BI team publishes an enterprise semantic model named Enterprise Sales to a Fabric workspace. A regional analyst must add a local Excel table of regional targets and create new measures that combine both sources, without copying the enterprise data and without modifying Enterprise Sales. The tenant allows DirectQuery connections to Power BI semantic models. What should the analyst do in Power BI Desktop?","opts":["Connect to Enterprise Sales with a live connection and create report-level measures only.","Connect to Enterprise Sales, select Make changes to this model, and then import the Excel table.","Export Enterprise Sales data to CSV and import it together with the Excel table.","Download the .pbix of Enterprise Sales and add the Excel table to it."],"ans":1,"exp":"Selecting Make changes to this model converts the live connection to a DirectQuery connection and creates a local semantic model whose remote tables use DirectQuery storage mode. You can then add Import tables such as the Excel targets, create relationships, and define measures that use both sources. This is a composite model on a Power BI semantic model.","why":["A live connection has no local model, so you cannot add another data source.","","Exporting copies the data and loses the governance of the enterprise model.","Editing a copy of the enterprise model modifies and duplicates it, which violates the requirements."],"ar":"اختر Make changes to this model لتحويل live connection إلى DirectQuery وإنشاء composite model مع جداول Import.","ref":[{"t":"Use composite models in Power BI","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/transform-model/desktop-composite-models"}]},{"id":"SM-D-032","d":"model","s":"design","type":"yesno","diff":3,"tag":"Composite models","q":"You are building a composite model that uses DirectQuery for Power BI semantic models. For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.","opts":["Calculation groups defined on the remote semantic model are supported and return reliable results in the composite model.","You can define row-level security roles on the tables that come from the remote semantic model.","A semantic model stored in My workspace cannot be used as a DirectQuery source for a composite model."],"ans":[false,false,true],"exp":"Microsoft Learn lists limitations for DirectQuery for Power BI semantic models and Analysis Services: calculation groups on remote sources are not supported and give undefined query results, defining RLS on tables from a remote source is not supported, and semantic models stored in My workspace cannot be used as a DirectQuery source. Security on the source model, such as OLS, is still enforced because data is retrieved from the source with the user credentials.","why":["No. Calculation groups on remote sources are not supported.","No. RLS on remote tables is not supported.","Yes. My workspace models are listed as unsupported sources."],"ar":"في composite model على semantic model بعيد: لا دعم لـ calculation groups البعيدة ولا لـ RLS على جداولها، ولا يمكن استخدام نماذج My workspace.","ref":[{"t":"Use composite models in Power BI","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/transform-model/desktop-composite-models"}]},{"id":"SM-O-001","d":"model","s":"optimize","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"Performance analyzer","q":"You have a Power BI report connected to a semantic model named SM1 in a Fabric workspace. Users report that a table visual named Visual1 is slow to appear. You record the page by using Performance analyzer and get the following durations for Visual1: DAX query: 45 ms, Visual display: 3,800 ms, Other: 20 ms. Where is most of the time for Visual1 being spent?","opts":["Rendering the visual on the canvas, for example because it draws too many data points or images","Evaluating the DAX query in the semantic model","Waiting for an external DirectQuery source to return results","Waiting for other visuals on the page to complete"],"ans":0,"exp":"Performance analyzer splits each visual load into categories. <b>Visual display</b> is the time needed to draw the visual on screen, including retrieving web images or geocoding. Because the DAX query only takes 45 ms, tuning measures will not help much; simplifying the visual (fewer rows, columns, images or data points) is the fix.","why":["","The DAX query category is only 45 ms, so the model is not the bottleneck.","A DirectQuery source would show a separate Direct query duration, which is not reported here.","Waiting for other visuals and preparing queries is reported under Other, which is only 20 ms."],"ar":"في Performance analyzer إذا كان Visual display هو الأطول فالمشكلة في رسم الـ visual وليس في DAX.","ref":[{"t":"Use Performance Analyzer to examine report performance","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/create-reports/performance-analyzer"}]},{"id":"SM-O-002","d":"model","s":"optimize","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Query reduction","q":"You are designing a report in Power BI Desktop over a semantic model that uses DirectQuery to a Fabric warehouse named WH1. Report pages contain several multi-select slicers and filters. Each time a user selects a value, every visual sends new queries to WH1, even though users usually make several selections before reading the results. You need to reduce the number of queries sent to WH1 with the least configuration effort. What should you do?","opts":["On the Optimize ribbon, apply the Query reduction optimization preset","On the Optimize ribbon, apply the Interactivity optimization preset","Change the storage mode of all dimension tables to Dual","Enable automatic page refresh on each report page"],"ans":0,"exp":"The <b>Query reduction</b> preset turns off default cross-highlighting and cross-filtering and adds Apply buttons, so slicer and filter changes are sent only once when the user selects Apply. It is the recommended preset for DirectQuery reports and follows the DirectQuery report design guidance.","why":["","Interactivity is the default preset. It applies slicer changes instantly, which is exactly what generates the extra queries.","Dual mode affects how dimension tables are queried, but it does not stop visuals from refreshing after each slicer click.","Automatic page refresh sends additional queries on a schedule, increasing load instead of reducing it."],"ar":"استخدم Query reduction preset لإضافة زر Apply وتقليل عدد الاستعلامات في تقارير DirectQuery.","ref":[{"t":"Optimize ribbon in Power BI Desktop","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/create-reports/desktop-optimize-ribbon"},{"t":"DirectQuery optimization scenarios with the Optimize ribbon","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/create-reports/desktop-optimize-ribbon-scenarios"}]},{"id":"SM-O-003","d":"model","s":"optimize","type":"multi","n":2,"diff":2,"tag":"Report visuals","q":"You have a Power BI report page that uses a DirectQuery semantic model. The page contains five slicers and twelve card visuals that each show one measure. Users complain that the page takes a long time to load and that it becomes slow again every time they change several slicers. You need to improve the performance of the page. Which two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. Select TWO.","opts":["Replace the twelve card visuals with a single multi-row card visual","Add an Apply all slicers button to the page","Set the cross-filter direction of every relationship to Both","Turn off Assume referential integrity on all relationships","Change the default visual interaction to cross-highlighting"],"ans":[0,1],"exp":"Every visual on a page sends its own query, and the number of parallel queries is limited, so fewer visuals means less serialized query work. Replacing many cards with one multi-row card keeps the layout with fewer queries. An <b>Apply all slicers</b> button applies all slicer selections at once, so the other visuals refresh only one time instead of after every selection.","why":["","","Bidirectional filters should be limited to required cases because they can hurt DirectQuery performance.","Assume referential integrity allows INNER JOIN queries, which usually performs better. Turning it off does not help.","Cross-highlighting is already the default and still triggers queries on interaction."],"ar":"قلل عدد الـ visuals في الصفحة وأضف زر Apply all slicers لتقليل الاستعلامات.","ref":[{"t":"DirectQuery model guidance in Power BI Desktop","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/directquery-model-guidance"},{"t":"Create Apply all and Clear all slicers buttons in Power BI reports","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/create-reports/buttons-apply-all-clear-all-slicers"}]},{"id":"SM-O-004","d":"model","s":"optimize","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Aggregations","q":"You have a semantic model named SM1 that contains a Sales table with 3 billion rows and an aggregation table named SalesAgg that summarizes SalesAmount by DateKey, CustomerKey and ProductSubcategoryKey. Both tables currently use Import storage mode. In the Manage aggregations dialog for SalesAgg, you cannot select Sales as the Detail Table. You need to configure user-defined aggregations so that summary queries hit SalesAgg and detail queries still return results. What should you do first?","opts":["Change the storage mode of the Sales table to DirectQuery","Change the storage mode of the SalesAgg table to DirectQuery","Create a relationship between SalesAgg and Sales","Set the Precedence of SalesAgg to 10"],"ans":0,"exp":"The Manage aggregations dialog enforces validations, and one of them is that the <b>Detail Table must use DirectQuery storage mode, not Import</b>. The usual design is a DirectQuery detail table, an Import aggregation table, and Dual dimension tables so that aggregated queries are answered from memory.","why":["","The aggregation table should normally be Import so that it is cached. The blocking issue is the storage mode of the detail table.","Aggregation tables are not related directly to the detail table. They relate to shared dimension tables, and chained relationships are not how aggregations are mapped.","Precedence only decides which aggregation table is tried first when several exist. It does not remove the validation error."],"ar":"في user-defined aggregations يجب أن يكون الـ Detail Table بوضع DirectQuery وجدول الـ aggregation بوضع Import.","ref":[{"t":"User-defined aggregations","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/transform-model/aggregations-advanced"}]},{"id":"SM-O-005","d":"model","s":"optimize","type":"yesno","diff":3,"tag":"Aggregations","q":"You have a composite semantic model that contains a DirectQuery table named Sales, an Import aggregation table named SalesAgg, and dimension tables named Geography, Customer and Date. SalesAgg is configured in Manage aggregations. For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.","opts":["Report consumers query the Sales table, and the engine redirects matching queries to SalesAgg, which is hidden from Report view.","You can define a row-level security role whose filter expression is applied only to the SalesAgg table.","Setting the related dimension tables to Dual storage mode lets queries that group by dimension attributes and aggregate SalesAgg be answered from the in-memory cache."],"ans":[true,false,true],"exp":"Aggregation tables are hidden and users refer to the detail table; the engine redirects queries internally. RLS for aggregations must filter both the aggregation table and the detail table, and an RLS expression that filters only the aggregation table is not allowed. Dual dimension tables can act as Import or DirectQuery depending on the subquery, so aggregated queries can be served from memory.","why":["Yes. Aggregation tables are hidden and consumers query the detail table.","No. RLS that filters only the aggregation table is not allowed because it could not secure the detail table.","Yes. Dual dimensions allow cache hits when the query uses the Import aggregation table."],"ar":"جداول الـ aggregation مخفية، ولا يُسمح بـ RLS على جدول الـ aggregation وحده، واجعل الأبعاد Dual.","ref":[{"t":"User-defined aggregations","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/transform-model/aggregations-advanced"}]},{"id":"SM-O-006","d":"model","s":"optimize","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Cardinality","q":"You have an Import semantic model named SM1. The Sales table contains 500 million rows and a text column named OrderNumber that stores values such as SO123456. Every value starts with the prefix SO. VertiPaq Analyzer shows that OrderNumber is one of the largest columns in the model. The column must remain available for drillthrough. You need to reduce the memory used by OrderNumber. What should you do?","opts":["In Power Query, remove the SO prefix and convert OrderNumber to a whole number data type","Create a DAX calculated column that concatenates SO with the order number and hide the original column","Change the data type of OrderNumber to Text in the model and set Summarize by to None","Mark OrderNumber as a key column in a relationship to a new OrderNumber table"],"ans":0,"exp":"VertiPaq uses <b>value encoding</b> for numeric data and <b>hash encoding</b> for text, which requires a dictionary of unique values. Removing a constant text prefix and converting to a whole number can significantly reduce the size of high-cardinality columns. Set the default summarization to Do Not Summarize to avoid accidental sums.","why":["","A calculated column adds another high-cardinality text column and calculated columns usually compress less efficiently.","The column is already text. Changing summarization does not change storage or encoding.","A separate table with the same unique values adds another large dictionary and a relationship, increasing memory."],"ar":"حوّل الأعمدة النصية عالية الـ cardinality إلى أرقام عندما يمكن، لأن VertiPaq يضغط الأرقام بشكل أفضل.","ref":[{"t":"Data reduction techniques for Import modeling","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/import-modeling-data-reduction"}]},{"id":"SM-O-007","d":"model","s":"optimize","type":"multi","n":2,"diff":1,"tag":"Model size","q":"You are developing an Import semantic model in Power BI Desktop. The model is growing quickly and refresh times are increasing. You need to reduce the size of the model without losing any data required by the reports. Which two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. Select TWO.","opts":["Remove columns that are not used for reporting, relationships, calculations or security","Disable the Auto date/time option for the file","Replace Power Query custom columns with DAX calculated columns","Set all relationships to bidirectional cross-filtering","Move the model to a larger Fabric capacity SKU"],"ans":[0,1],"exp":"Removing unnecessary columns (vertical filtering) is one of the most effective data reduction techniques. The Auto date/time option creates a hidden calculated table for each date column, so disabling it removes those tables from the model.","why":["","","The guidance is the opposite: prefer Power Query custom columns because DAX calculated columns typically compress less efficiently and extend refresh time.","Cross-filter direction does not reduce model size and can hurt query performance.","A larger SKU provides more resources but does not make the model smaller."],"ar":"احذف الأعمدة غير المستخدمة وأوقف Auto date/time لتقليل حجم الـ semantic model.","ref":[{"t":"Data reduction techniques for Import modeling","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/import-modeling-data-reduction"}]},{"id":"SM-O-008","d":"model","s":"optimize","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"DAX variables","q":"You have the following measure in a semantic model:<pre>Sales YoY % =\nDIVIDE(\n  [Sales] - CALCULATE([Sales], SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR('Date'[Date])),\n  CALCULATE([Sales], SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR('Date'[Date]))\n)</pre>You need to improve the performance and readability of the measure without changing its result. What should you do?","opts":["Store the prior year sales expression in a variable by using VAR and reference the variable twice in RETURN","Replace DIVIDE with the / operator","Wrap the whole expression in IFERROR","Convert the measure to a calculated column in the Date table"],"ans":0,"exp":"The expression that calculates prior year sales is evaluated twice. Assigning it to a <b>variable</b> means it is evaluated once and reused, which can roughly halve query time for this pattern and makes the formula easier to read and debug.","why":["","The denominator can be zero or BLANK, so DIVIDE is the right choice here; the duplicated expression is the real cost.","IFERROR can add storage engine scans and degrade performance.","A calculated column is static and does not respond to report filters the way a measure does."],"ar":"استخدم VAR لتخزين تعبير مكرر مرة واحدة وتحسين أداء DAX.","ref":[{"t":"Use variables to improve your DAX formulas","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/best-practices/dax-variables"}]},{"id":"SM-O-009","d":"model","s":"optimize","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Filter arguments","q":"You have the following measure:<pre>Red Sales =\nCALCULATE(\n  [Sales],\n  FILTER('Product', 'Product'[Color] = \"Red\")\n)</pre>The Product table contains 2 million rows. You need to rewrite the measure to improve performance while preserving any existing filters on the Color column. Which expression should you use as the filter argument of CALCULATE?","opts":["<code>KEEPFILTERS('Product'[Color] = \"Red\")</code>","<code>FILTER(ALL('Product'), 'Product'[Color] = \"Red\")</code>","<code>FILTER(VALUES('Product'), 'Product'[Color] = \"Red\")</code>","<code>ALL('Product'[Color])</code>"],"ans":0,"exp":"Passing a <b>Boolean expression</b> as a filter argument is more efficient than a FILTER table expression that iterates the whole table, because in-memory column stores are optimized to filter single columns. KEEPFILTERS keeps any existing filter on Color instead of overwriting it. Use FILTER only when you need measures, multi-column comparisons or OR logic.","why":["","This still iterates every Product row and also removes all existing Product filters.","This still iterates the table and is not more efficient than the original.","ALL removes filters from Color and does not filter to red products at all."],"ar":"استخدم Boolean filter مع KEEPFILTERS بدل FILTER على جدول كامل داخل CALCULATE.","ref":[{"t":"Avoid using FILTER as a filter argument","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/best-practices/dax-avoid-avoid-filter-as-filter-argument"}]},{"id":"SM-O-010","d":"model","s":"optimize","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"DIVIDE","q":"A semantic model contains the following measure:<pre>Profit Margin =\nIF(\n  OR(ISBLANK([Sales]), [Sales] = 0),\n  BLANK(),\n  [Profit] / [Sales]\n)</pre>You need to produce the same result with a more efficient and concise expression. What should you use?","opts":["<code>DIVIDE([Profit], [Sales])</code>","<code>[Profit] / [Sales]</code>","<code>IFERROR([Profit] / [Sales], BLANK())</code>","<code>DIVIDE([Profit], [Sales], 0)</code>"],"ans":0,"exp":"DIVIDE returns BLANK when the denominator is zero or BLANK, and it is better optimized for testing the denominator than an IF expression. Use the / operator only when the denominator is a constant that can never be zero.","why":["","The operator does not handle a zero denominator and can return infinity or an error.","IFERROR can add extra storage engine scans and is slower than DIVIDE.","This returns 0 instead of BLANK, which changes the result and causes visuals to show rows with no data."],"ar":"استخدم DIVIDE عندما يمكن أن يكون المقام صفرا أو BLANK، فهي أسرع من IF.","ref":[{"t":"DIVIDE function vs. divide operator (/)","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/best-practices/dax-divide-function-operator"}]},{"id":"SM-O-011","d":"model","s":"optimize","type":"single","diff":3,"tag":"Context transition","q":"A semantic model contains a Sales table with 400 million rows and the following measures:<pre>Unit Margin = SUM(Sales[UnitPrice]) - SUM(Sales[UnitCost])\nTotal Margin = SUMX(Sales, [Unit Margin] * Sales[Quantity])</pre>DAX query timings show that Total Margin is very slow. Which statement explains the main cost and how to reduce it?","opts":["Referencing the model measure [Unit Margin] inside SUMX triggers context transition for every Sales row; rewrite the expression to use column references, such as <code>SUMX(Sales, (Sales[UnitPrice] - Sales[UnitCost]) * Sales[Quantity])</code>","SUMX ignores the row context when it evaluates a measure, so the measure returns the grand total for every row; add ALL(Sales) to fix it","Context transition happens only when CALCULATE is written explicitly, so the cost comes from Sales[Quantity]; convert Quantity to text","The measure is slow because SUMX cannot be used on fact tables; replace it with IFERROR(SUM(Sales[Quantity]), 0)"],"ans":0,"exp":"When a <b>model measure is used in row context</b>, such as inside an iterator, the engine automatically performs <b>context transition</b>, converting the current row into a filter context. Doing this for hundreds of millions of rows is expensive. Using plain column arithmetic inside the iterator avoids per-row context transition, or you can iterate a smaller table when the logic allows it.","why":["","Context transition converts the row context into filters, so the measure is evaluated for the current row, not for the grand total. ALL would make results wrong.","Context transition is automatic when a measure is referenced in row context, even without writing CALCULATE.","Iterators can be used on fact tables, and IFERROR tends to degrade performance."],"ar":"استدعاء measure داخل iterator يسبب context transition لكل صف، لذا استخدم أعمدة مباشرة لتحسين الأداء.","ref":[{"t":"CALCULATE function (DAX)","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/calculate-function-dax"},{"t":"Appropriate use of error functions","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/best-practices/dax-error-functions"}]},{"id":"SM-O-012","d":"model","s":"optimize","type":"yesno","diff":2,"tag":"DAX query tools","q":"You are troubleshooting slow measures in a published semantic model named SM1. You plan to capture the DAX queries generated by report visuals and test rewritten measures. For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.","opts":["In Power BI Desktop, Performance analyzer can copy the DAX query that a visual sends to the semantic model.","When you edit a report in the Power BI service, Performance analyzer provides a Run in DAX query view option for each visual.","In a DAX query, DEFINE MEASURE can redefine an existing model measure for the duration of that query only, which lets you test a rewrite before updating the model."],"ans":[true,false,true],"exp":"Performance analyzer shows each visual query and offers Copy query, and in Power BI Desktop it also offers Run in DAX query view. That run option is not available in the Power BI service, although you can paste the copied query into Write DAX queries. Query-scoped measures created with DEFINE MEASURE override the model definition only for that query. The same queries can also be run in SSMS or the open-source DAX Studio for timing analysis.","why":["Yes. Copy query is available for each visual.","No. Run in DAX query view is not available in the service; copy the query and run it in the web DAX query view instead.","Yes. A measure that already exists in the model uses the DEFINE MEASURE formula for that query."],"ar":"انسخ استعلام الـ visual من Performance analyzer واختبر التعديل باستخدام DEFINE MEASURE في DAX query view.","ref":[{"t":"Use Performance Analyzer to examine report performance","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/create-reports/performance-analyzer"},{"t":"DAX queries","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/dax-queries"}]},{"id":"SM-O-013","d":"model","s":"optimize","type":"single","diff":3,"tag":"Storage engine","q":"You have a Direct Lake semantic model named SM1 over a lakehouse named LH1. Delta tables in LH1 are written by Spark notebooks with V-Order disabled and frequent small appends. You capture a slow DAX query with server timings in DAX Studio and find that almost all of the duration is spent in storage engine scans, and cold-cache queries are much slower than warm-cache queries. You need to reduce the storage engine time. What should you do?","opts":["Compact the Delta tables with OPTIMIZE to reduce small files and row groups, and apply V-Order to the tables consumed by Direct Lake","Rewrite the report theme to use fewer colors","Set the Direct Lake behavior of SM1 to DirectQueryOnly","Turn off Keep your Direct Lake data up to date for SM1"],"ans":0,"exp":"When a DAX query runs, the <b>formula engine</b> builds the query plan and requests data and initial aggregations from the <b>storage engine</b>, which uses VertiPaq for Import and Direct Lake. Direct Lake storage engine and transcoding performance depends on well-tuned Delta tables: V-Order, a small number of Parquet files and large row groups. Enabling V-Order for Direct Lake tables improves cold-cache queries significantly.","why":["","The theme affects rendering, not storage engine scans.","DirectQueryOnly forces every query through the SQL analytics endpoint, which is usually slower, and it only applies to Direct Lake on SQL.","Automatic updates control when framing happens; they do not fix a poor file and row group layout."],"ar":"زمن الـ storage engine المرتفع في Direct Lake يُعالج بعمل OPTIMIZE و V-Order للـ Delta tables.","ref":[{"t":"Understand Direct Lake query performance","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-understand-storage"},{"t":"Cross-workload table maintenance and optimization in Microsoft Fabric","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/table-maintenance-optimization"}]},{"id":"SM-O-014","d":"model","s":"optimize","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"DirectLakeBehavior","q":"You are developing a semantic model named SM1 that uses Direct Lake on SQL analytics endpoints over a warehouse named WH1. During development, you want any query that cannot be answered in Direct Lake mode to fail with an error so that you can find and fix the cause, instead of silently running in DirectQuery mode. Which value should you set for the Direct Lake behavior property of SM1?","opts":["Automatic","DirectLakeOnly","DirectQueryOnly","Direct Lake on OneLake"],"ans":1,"exp":"<b>DirectLakeBehavior</b> controls DirectQuery fallback for Direct Lake on SQL analytics endpoints. <b>DirectLakeOnly</b> makes queries fail with an error when Direct Lake conditions are not met, which is recommended during development. <b>Automatic</b> (the default) silently falls back and is recommended for production, and <b>DirectQueryOnly</b> always uses DirectQuery, which is useful to measure fallback performance.","why":["Automatic is the default and falls back silently, which hides the problem.","","DirectQueryOnly sends every query to the SQL analytics endpoint, so nothing would fail and Direct Lake would never be used.","This is a storage mode option, not a value of the Direct Lake behavior property."],"ar":"اضبط DirectLakeBehavior على DirectLakeOnly أثناء التطوير لكشف أسباب الـ fallback، و Automatic في الإنتاج.","ref":[{"t":"How Direct Lake works","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-how-it-works"}]},{"id":"SM-O-015","d":"model","s":"optimize","type":"order","diff":3,"tag":"Framing","q":"You have a Direct Lake semantic model named SM1 over a lakehouse named LH1. A nightly Spark ETL process rewrites several Delta tables in LH1 over two hours. Users currently see partially loaded data during the ETL window. You need to ensure that users see only a consistent snapshot of the data until the ETL process completes, and then see the new data. Which three actions should you perform in sequence?","opts":["In the SM1 settings, turn off Keep your Direct Lake data up to date","Run the nightly ETL process to load the Delta tables in LH1","After the ETL process succeeds, refresh SM1 by using the Power BI REST API to perform framing","Set the Direct Lake behavior of SM1 to DirectQueryOnly","Configure an incremental refresh policy on the largest table in SM1"],"ans":[0,1,2],"exp":"<b>Keep your Direct Lake data up to date</b> is on by default and frames the model whenever Delta table changes are detected. Turning it off lets the model keep serving data as of the last framing while ETL runs. After the load completes, a refresh (framing) updates the model to reference the latest Delta table versions. The refresh can be manual, scheduled, or programmatic via the REST API or TOM.","why":["","","","DirectQueryOnly would return the latest, possibly partial, data from the SQL analytics endpoint and is slower.","Direct Lake tables do not support model table partitions, so incremental refresh policies do not apply."],"ar":"أوقف Keep your Direct Lake data up to date أثناء الـ ETL ثم نفّذ refresh (framing) بعد انتهاء التحميل.","ref":[{"t":"How Direct Lake works","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-how-it-works"},{"t":"Direct Lake overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-overview"}]},{"id":"SM-O-016","d":"model","s":"optimize","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Fallback diagnostics","q":"You have a semantic model named SM1 that uses Direct Lake on SQL analytics endpoints with the Direct Lake behavior set to Automatic. Some report pages are much slower than others, and you suspect that certain tables fall back to DirectQuery. You need to identify which tables fall back and the reason for each. What should you do?","opts":["Run <code>EVALUATE TABLETRAITS()</code> and review the DirectLakeFallbackInfo column","Review the Visual display duration in Performance analyzer","Open the Direct Lake tab in the refresh history of SM1","Set the Direct Lake behavior to DirectQueryOnly and compare page load times"],"ans":0,"exp":"The <b>TABLETRAITS()</b> DAX query returns a <b>DirectLakeFallbackInfo</b> column that shows the fallback reason for each table. A value of None means the table is using Direct Lake mode. Typical reasons include SQL views, SQL RLS, OLS or DDM at the endpoint, unframed tables and guardrail violations.","why":["","Visual display measures rendering time and says nothing about storage mode or fallback reasons.","The Direct Lake tab lists refresh (framing) failures, not per-table query fallback reasons.","This forces every table into DirectQuery, which measures fallback cost but does not tell you which tables fall back or why."],"ar":"استخدم EVALUATE TABLETRAITS() لمعرفة الجداول التي تعمل fallback إلى DirectQuery وسبب ذلك.","ref":[{"t":"How Direct Lake works","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-how-it-works"}]},{"id":"SM-O-017","d":"model","s":"optimize","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"V-Order","q":"You have a lakehouse named LH1 that contains a Delta table named FactSales. FactSales was written by Spark without V-Order, and it is now the main source for a Direct Lake semantic model. You need to apply V-Order to the existing Parquet files of FactSales. What should you run in a notebook?","opts":["<code>OPTIMIZE FactSales VORDER</code>","<code>VACUUM FactSales</code>","<code>ALTER TABLE FactSales SET TBLPROPERTIES(\"delta.parquet.vorder.enabled\" = \"true\")</code> only","<code>DESCRIBE HISTORY FactSales</code>"],"ans":0,"exp":"V-Order is a write-time Parquet optimization that improves read performance, especially cold-cache Direct Lake queries. Setting the table property affects only <b>future writes</b>, so existing files keep their current layout. To rewrite existing files with V-Order, run a compaction such as OPTIMIZE with VORDER.","why":["","VACUUM removes files that are no longer referenced; it does not rewrite active files with V-Order.","The table property applies only to future writes and does not change existing Parquet files.","DESCRIBE HISTORY only shows table history metrics."],"ar":"خاصية V-Order على الجدول تؤثر على الكتابات المستقبلية فقط، واستخدم OPTIMIZE مع VORDER لإعادة كتابة الملفات الحالية.","ref":[{"t":"Optimize Delta Lake tables with V-order","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-engineering/delta-optimization-and-v-order"},{"t":"Spark monitoring and performance optimization best practices","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-engineering/spark-monitoring-best-practices"}]},{"id":"SM-O-018","d":"model","s":"optimize","type":"yesno","diff":3,"tag":"Guardrails","q":"Your organization has an F32 capacity. You have two semantic models over the same lakehouse: SM1 uses Direct Lake on OneLake, and SM2 uses Direct Lake on SQL analytics endpoints with the Direct Lake behavior set to Automatic. A Delta table named Events grows to 1,400 Parquet files, which exceeds the F32 guardrail of 1,000 files per table. For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.","opts":["A refresh of SM1 fails and SM1 cannot be queried until the Events table is optimized to fall within the guardrails.","Queries to SM2 still return results, but they fall back to DirectQuery mode with slower performance.","For SM2, only queries that reference the Events table leave Direct Lake mode; all other tables in SM2 continue to use Direct Lake."],"ans":[true,true,false],"exp":"When guardrails are exceeded, <b>Direct Lake on OneLake</b> behaves like Import: refresh fails and the model cannot be queried until the Delta tables are optimized. <b>Direct Lake on SQL</b> falls back to DirectQuery when fallback is enabled; refresh succeeds with a warning. A single table that exceeds any guardrail prevents Direct Lake mode for the entire model. Running OPTIMIZE and VACUUM or moving to a larger SKU resolves it.","why":["Yes. Direct Lake on OneLake has no DirectQuery fallback, so exceeding guardrails blocks refresh and queries.","Yes. With Automatic behavior, Direct Lake on SQL falls back to DirectQuery.","No. One table that exceeds a guardrail prevents Direct Lake mode for the whole model."],"ar":"تجاوز الـ guardrails يُفشل Direct Lake on OneLake، بينما Direct Lake on SQL يعمل fallback إلى DirectQuery للنموذج كله.","ref":[{"t":"Direct Lake overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-overview"},{"t":"How Direct Lake works","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-how-it-works"}]},{"id":"SM-O-019","d":"model","s":"optimize","type":"yesno","diff":2,"tag":"Automatic updates","q":"You have a Direct Lake semantic model named SM1 created with default settings over a warehouse named WH1. For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.","opts":["The Keep your Direct Lake data up to date setting is enabled by default, so SM1 is framed automatically when changes are detected in the underlying Delta tables.","A refresh of SM1 copies all table data into the model, similar to an Import refresh.","If automatic updates are suspended because of a non-recoverable refresh error, they resume automatically after a later on-demand refresh completes without errors."],"ans":[true,false,true],"exp":"Automatic updates are on by default and frame only the tables where data changes are detected. A Direct Lake refresh is <b>framing</b>: a low-cost metadata operation that points the model to the latest Parquet files, not a full data copy. Power BI suspends automatic updates after a non-recoverable error and resumes them after a successful on-demand refresh.","why":["Yes. The setting is enabled by default in the Refresh section of the model settings.","No. Framing copies only metadata; column data is loaded on demand by transcoding.","Yes. A successful on-demand refresh resumes automatic updates."],"ar":"الـ refresh في Direct Lake هو framing للـ metadata فقط، والتحديث التلقائي مفعل افتراضيا.","ref":[{"t":"How Direct Lake works","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-how-it-works"}]},{"id":"SM-O-020","d":"model","s":"optimize","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Direct Lake on OneLake","q":"You need to create a semantic model named SM1 that uses Direct Lake storage for Delta tables from a lakehouse named LH1 in workspace WS1 and from a warehouse named WH2 in workspace WS2. Both workspaces are in the same region. SM1 must also include a small Import table loaded from an Excel file. Which Direct Lake option should you use for SM1?","opts":["Direct Lake on OneLake","Direct Lake on SQL analytics endpoints with the behavior set to Automatic","Direct Lake on SQL analytics endpoints with the behavior set to DirectLakeOnly","DirectQuery to the SQL analytics endpoint of LH1"],"ans":0,"exp":"<b>Direct Lake on OneLake</b> can use data from one or more Fabric items with Delta tables and supports composite models with Import tables added from other sources. <b>Direct Lake on SQL</b> can use only a single Fabric data source and cannot combine Direct Lake tables with other storage modes in the same model. Direct Lake on OneLake is the recommended option for new models, and it does not fall back to DirectQuery.","why":["","Direct Lake on SQL analytics endpoints supports a single Fabric source and does not support mixing with Import tables in the same model.","The behavior value does not change the single-source limitation.","DirectQuery would not provide Direct Lake storage or VertiPaq performance."],"ar":"اختر Direct Lake on OneLake عند الحاجة لعدة مصادر Fabric أو لإضافة جداول Import في نفس النموذج.","ref":[{"t":"Direct Lake overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-overview"}]},{"id":"SM-O-021","d":"model","s":"optimize","type":"single","diff":3,"tag":"Direct Lake on SQL","q":"You have a warehouse named WH1 that contains a non-materialized SQL view named vw_Sales. You cannot change WH1. You need to create a semantic model that includes a Direct Lake table based on vw_Sales. Queries must keep working even if they run slower than Direct Lake. Which configuration should you use?","opts":["Direct Lake on OneLake with the Direct Lake behavior set to Automatic","Direct Lake on SQL analytics endpoints with the Direct Lake behavior set to Automatic","Direct Lake on SQL analytics endpoints with the Direct Lake behavior set to DirectLakeOnly","Direct Lake on OneLake with the Direct Lake behavior set to DirectQueryOnly"],"ans":1,"exp":"Creating a Direct Lake on OneLake table based on a non-materialized SQL view is not supported. <b>Direct Lake on SQL analytics endpoints</b> can include SQL views, but those tables fall back to DirectQuery. With the default <b>Automatic</b> behavior the fallback is silent and queries succeed, at DirectQuery speed. The alternatives are to materialize the view as a Delta table or use Import for that table.","why":["Direct Lake on OneLake cannot use non-materialized SQL views, and the behavior property applies only to Direct Lake on SQL.","","DirectLakeOnly makes queries against the view fail, because the view cannot be served in Direct Lake mode.","Direct Lake on OneLake does not support DirectQuery fallback or views, so this combination is not valid."],"ar":"الـ SQL views غير الـ materialized تتطلب Direct Lake on SQL مع Automatic لأنها تعمل fallback إلى DirectQuery.","ref":[{"t":"Direct Lake overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-overview"},{"t":"How Direct Lake works","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-how-it-works"}]},{"id":"SM-O-022","d":"model","s":"optimize","type":"multi","n":2,"diff":3,"tag":"Direct Lake security","q":"You have a lakehouse named LH1 that has OneLake security enabled with roles that apply row-level security to the Sales table. Report consumers are Viewers in the workspace. You plan to build a semantic model named SM1 on LH1 that uses single sign-on (SSO). You need to ensure that the OneLake security rules are enforced for SM1 users and that queries are served from the in-memory cache without DirectQuery fallback. Which two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. Select TWO.","opts":["Create SM1 by using Direct Lake on OneLake","Add the report consumers to a OneLake security role on LH1 that grants read access","Create SM1 by using Direct Lake on SQL analytics endpoints and switch the SQL analytics endpoint of LH1 to user identity mode","Recreate the row filters as T-SQL security policies on the SQL analytics endpoint of LH1","Set the Direct Lake behavior of SM1 to DirectQueryOnly"],"ans":[0,1],"exp":"<b>Direct Lake on OneLake</b> checks permissions through OneLake security, not the SQL analytics endpoint. With OneLake security on, it uses the current user (with SSO) to resolve OneLake security roles and enforce OLS and RLS while still answering queries from memory. Viewers do not have read access in OneLake by default, so they must be added to a OneLake security role (or be granted Read and ReadAll).","why":["","","When the SQL analytics endpoint uses user identity, OneLake security roles become SQL granular access rules and Direct Lake on SQL falls back to DirectQuery for all queries.","SQL RLS at the endpoint causes Direct Lake on SQL to fall back to DirectQuery and is not applied by Direct Lake on OneLake.","DirectQueryOnly disables Direct Lake entirely, which contradicts the in-memory requirement."],"ar":"مع OneLake security استخدم Direct Lake on OneLake وأضف المستخدمين إلى OneLake security role لتطبيق RLS بدون fallback.","ref":[{"t":"Integrate Direct Lake security","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-security-integration"},{"t":"How Direct Lake works","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-how-it-works"}]},{"id":"SM-O-023","d":"model","s":"optimize","type":"order","diff":2,"tag":"Incremental refresh","q":"You have a Power BI Desktop file that imports a FactOrders table from an Azure SQL database. FactOrders contains an OrderDate column of the Date/Time data type. You need to configure incremental refresh so that only the last 10 days are refreshed and 5 years of history are kept in the service. Which four actions should you perform in sequence?","opts":["In Power Query Editor, create Date/Time parameters named RangeStart and RangeEnd","Filter OrderDate so that it is on or after RangeStart and before RangeEnd","In Table view, define the incremental refresh policy for FactOrders with a 5-year archive and a 10-day refresh period","Publish the model to a workspace and refresh it in the service","Create a DAX calculated column that converts OrderDate to an integer date key","Enable Detect data changes by using the OrderDate column"],"ans":[0,1,2,3],"exp":"Incremental refresh uses reserved, case-sensitive Date/Time parameters named <b>RangeStart</b> and <b>RangeEnd</b>. You filter the date column with an equal sign on only one side, define the policy (archive and refresh periods), and publish. The policy is applied after the first refresh in the service, which creates the partitions and loads the full history.","why":["","","","","Filtering must be done in Power Query so that it folds to the source; a DAX calculated column is not used for partition filtering.","The Detect data changes column should not be the same column used to partition the data with RangeStart and RangeEnd."],"ar":"أنشئ RangeStart و RangeEnd، ثم فلتر التاريخ، ثم عرّف السياسة، ثم انشر ونفّذ refresh في الخدمة.","ref":[{"t":"Configure incremental refresh and real-time data","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/connect-data/incremental-refresh-configure"}]},{"id":"SM-O-024","d":"model","s":"optimize","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Detect data changes","q":"You have a semantic model with an incremental refresh policy on a Sales table. The table is partitioned by OrderDate and the refresh period is the last 30 days. The source table also has a LastModified column that is updated whenever a row changes. Most days in the refresh period do not change between refreshes. You need to refresh only the periods that contain changed data. What should you configure?","opts":["Select Detect data changes and choose the LastModified column","Select Detect data changes and choose the OrderDate column","Select Only refresh complete days","Select Get the latest data in real time with DirectQuery"],"ans":0,"exp":"<b>Detect data changes</b> evaluates the maximum value of a chosen date/time column for each period in the incremental range and refreshes only periods where that value changed. The column should be an audit column such as LastModified, not the column used to partition the data with RangeStart and RangeEnd. Note that hard deletes are not detected; only soft deletes marked with an updated date are.","why":["","The change detection column should not be the same column used for partitioning.","Only refresh complete days avoids partial days but still refreshes every period in the refresh range.","This adds a DirectQuery partition for real-time data; it does not skip unchanged periods."],"ar":"استخدم Detect data changes مع عمود مثل LastModified وليس عمود التقسيم.","ref":[{"t":"Configure incremental refresh and real-time data","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/connect-data/incremental-refresh-configure"},{"t":"Incremental refresh and real-time data for semantic models","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/connect-data/incremental-refresh-overview"}]},{"id":"SM-O-025","d":"model","s":"optimize","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Hybrid tables","q":"You have an Import semantic model in a workspace on a Fabric F64 capacity. The Transactions table has an incremental refresh policy and is refreshed once per night from an Azure SQL database. Business users need reports to include transactions from today, but you must not increase the refresh frequency. What should you do?","opts":["In the incremental refresh policy, select Get the latest data in real time with DirectQuery","In the incremental refresh policy, select Detect data changes","Enable automatic page refresh on the report pages","Change the storage mode of the Transactions table to Dual"],"ans":0,"exp":"Selecting <b>Get the latest data in real time with DirectQuery</b> creates a <b>hybrid table</b>: Import partitions for history and the refresh period, plus a DirectQuery partition for rows after the last refresh. It requires a Premium or Fabric capacity, and the source query must fold because nonfolding transformations cannot be used with the DirectQuery partition. Only refresh complete days is enabled automatically.","why":["","Detect data changes reduces which periods are refreshed; it does not return data added after the last refresh.","Page refresh only re-queries the model; an Import model still shows data as of the last refresh.","Dual mode does not apply to incremental refresh partitions and would not add a real-time partition to the table."],"ar":"لإظهار بيانات اليوم بدون زيادة الـ refresh استخدم hybrid table بإضافة DirectQuery partition في سياسة incremental refresh.","ref":[{"t":"Configure incremental refresh and real-time data","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/connect-data/incremental-refresh-configure"},{"t":"Incremental refresh and real-time data for semantic models","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/connect-data/incremental-refresh-overview"}]},{"id":"SM-O-026","d":"model","s":"optimize","type":"multi","n":2,"diff":1,"tag":"Query folding","q":"You configure incremental refresh on a table named Orders in Power BI Desktop. After you apply the RangeStart and RangeEnd filter, loading the filtered data takes a very long time and the incremental refresh policy dialog shows a query folding warning. You need to ensure that the filter is folded to the source. Which two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. Select TWO.","opts":["Ensure that both the parameters and the filtered column use the Date/Time data type","Remove or move the transformation step that breaks query folding before the RangeStart and RangeEnd filter step","Rename the parameters to StartDate and EndDate","Change the filter to use greater than or equal to RangeStart and less than or equal to RangeEnd","Enable Detect data changes on the Orders table"],"ans":[0,1],"exp":"Incremental refresh depends on the RangeStart and RangeEnd filter being <b>folded</b> to the source as a single query. A data type mismatch between the parameters and the column prevents folding, as do transformations that do not fold. You can verify folding with View Native Query, Query Diagnostics or a source trace such as SQL Profiler.","why":["","","RangeStart and RangeEnd are reserved, case-sensitive names, so renaming breaks incremental refresh.","An equal sign on both boundaries can place a row in two partitions and create duplicates; use it on only one side.","Detect data changes does not make a query fold."],"ar":"تأكد أن نوع البيانات Date/Time متطابق وأن الخطوات تسمح بالـ query folding قبل فلتر RangeStart و RangeEnd.","ref":[{"t":"Troubleshoot incremental refresh and real-time data","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/connect-data/incremental-refresh-troubleshoot"},{"t":"Configure incremental refresh and real-time data","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/connect-data/incremental-refresh-configure"}]},{"id":"MT-S-001","d":"maintain","s":"security","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"Workspace roles","q":"You have a Fabric workspace named WS1. A data engineer named User1 must be able to create, edit, and run notebooks and pipelines in WS1. User1 must NOT be able to add other people to WS1. You need to assign a workspace role to User1 by following the principle of least privilege. Which role should you assign?","opts":["Admin","Member","Contributor","Viewer"],"ans":2,"exp":"The Contributor role can create, modify, write, delete, and execute items such as notebooks, pipelines, lakehouses, and warehouses. It cannot manage workspace access: only Admins and Members can add people to the workspace. Contributor is therefore the least privileged role that meets the requirement.","why":["Admin can do everything, including adding or removing any user and deleting the workspace, which exceeds the requirement.","Member can add other users with the same or lower permissions, which violates the requirement.","","Viewer can view and read content and view execution output, but cannot create, modify, or run notebooks or pipelines."],"ar":"دور Contributor يسمح بإنشاء وتشغيل notebooks وpipelines بدون صلاحية إضافة مستخدمين للـ workspace.","ref":[{"t":"Roles in workspaces in Microsoft Fabric","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/roles-workspaces"}]},{"id":"MT-S-002","d":"maintain","s":"security","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Workspace roles","q":"Contoso has a Fabric workspace named WS1. The team lead, who is a Member of WS1, needs to connect WS1 to an Azure DevOps Git repository and create a workspace identity for WS1. The operation fails. Which workspace role is required to perform both tasks?","opts":["Member","Admin","Contributor with Reshare permission on every item","Viewer with Build permission"],"ans":1,"exp":"According to the Fabric workspace roles matrix, only the Admin role can connect a workspace to a Git repository and create a workspace identity. Members can add users with lower permissions and share items, but these two workspace-level configuration tasks are reserved for Admins.","why":["Members cannot connect a workspace to Git or create a workspace identity, which is why the operation failed.","","Contributor is below Member, and item Reshare permission does not grant workspace-level configuration rights.","Viewer is read-only for the workspace; Build is a semantic model permission unrelated to Git integration."],"ar":"ربط الـ workspace بـ Git وإنشاء workspace identity من صلاحيات دور Admin فقط.","ref":[{"t":"Roles in workspaces in Microsoft Fabric","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/roles-workspaces"}]},{"id":"MT-S-003","d":"maintain","s":"security","type":"yesno","diff":3,"tag":"Workspace roles","q":"You have a Fabric workspace named WS1 that contains a semantic model named SM1. SM1 has row-level security (RLS) roles defined. User1 is a Member of WS1.<br>For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.","opts":["User1 can add a new user to WS1 as a Contributor.","User1 can remove an existing user from the Admin role of WS1.","If User2 is added to WS1 as a Contributor, the RLS roles in SM1 restrict the rows that User2 can see."],"ans":[true,false,false],"exp":"Members can add users with the same or lower permissions, so adding a Contributor is allowed. Members cannot remove users or change existing roles; only Admins can add or remove any user. Semantic model RLS applies only to users with Viewer (read) access; Admin, Member, and Contributor have edit permission on the semantic model, so RLS does not apply to them.","why":["Members can add members or others with lower permissions.","Only Admins can add or remove people, including other admins.","RLS only restricts users in the Viewer role; Contributors see all data."],"ar":"الـ Member يضيف مستخدمين بصلاحيات أقل لكنه لا يحذفهم، وRLS في semantic model لا تُطبق إلا على Viewer.","ref":[{"t":"Roles in workspaces in Power BI","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/collaborate-share/service-roles-new-workspaces"},{"t":"Row-level security (RLS) with Power BI","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/security/service-admin-row-level-security"}]},{"id":"MT-S-004","d":"maintain","s":"security","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Workspace roles","q":"You have a Fabric workspace named WS1 that contains a lakehouse named LH1. An analyst named User1 is a Viewer in WS1. User1 can query LH1 tables through the SQL analytics endpoint but receives an access error when reading the same Delta tables from a Spark notebook by using the OneLake path. User1 must be able to read LH1 data from Spark but must NOT be able to write to any item in WS1. What should you do?","opts":["Change User1 to the Contributor role in WS1.","Grant User1 the Read all with Apache Spark (ReadAll) permission on LH1.","Grant User1 the Build permission on a semantic model that uses LH1.","Grant User1 SELECT on the LH1 tables by using T-SQL in the SQL analytics endpoint."],"ans":1,"exp":"The Viewer role includes ReadData (T-SQL through the SQL analytics endpoint) but not ReadAll, which is required to read lakehouse data through Spark and OneLake APIs. Granting the Read all with Apache Spark item permission on LH1 adds ReadAll without granting write access. Contributor would work for reading but also grants write access to items.","why":["Contributor includes ReadAll but also allows writing and deleting items, which violates the requirement.","","Build is a semantic model permission and does not affect OneLake or Spark access.","T-SQL GRANT statements govern the SQL engine only; they do not grant access to files through Spark or OneLake APIs."],"ar":"الـ Viewer يملك ReadData عبر SQL فقط، وللقراءة من Spark يحتاج صلاحية ReadAll على الـ lakehouse.","ref":[{"t":"Roles in workspaces in Microsoft Fabric","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/roles-workspaces"},{"t":"How lakehouse sharing works","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-engineering/lakehouse-sharing"}]},{"id":"MT-S-005","d":"maintain","s":"security","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"Item permissions","q":"You have a Fabric warehouse named WH1 in a workspace named WS1. You need to share WH1 with a finance analyst who is not a member of WS1. The analyst must be able to read all tables and views in WH1 by using T-SQL from SQL Server Management Studio. The analyst must NOT be able to read the underlying files by using Spark. Which permission should you select when sharing WH1?","opts":["No additional permissions (Read only)","Read all data using SQL (ReadData)","Read all data using Apache Spark (ReadAll)","Build permission on a semantic model that uses WH1"],"ans":1,"exp":"ReadData (\"Read all data using SQL\") lets the recipient read all tables and views in the warehouse in read-only mode through T-SQL, similar to the db_datareader role. ReadData and ReadAll are separate, non-overlapping permissions, so the analyst gets no Spark or OneLake file access.","why":["Read alone only allows connecting to the endpoint (like CONNECT); the analyst could not query any table without additional GRANT statements.","","ReadAll grants access to the files through Spark and OneLake, which must be avoided.","Build applies to a semantic model and does not provide T-SQL access to the warehouse tables. (Default semantic models are no longer created automatically for new warehouses.)"],"ar":"صلاحية ReadData تكفي لقراءة كل الجداول عبر T-SQL دون الوصول للملفات عبر Spark.","ref":[{"t":"Share your data and manage permissions","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/share-warehouse-manage-permissions"}]},{"id":"MT-S-006","d":"maintain","s":"security","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Item permissions","q":"You have a Fabric warehouse named WH1 that contains 40 tables. A partner analyst must be able to query only the <code>sales.Orders</code> and <code>sales.Customers</code> tables. The analyst must not be added to the workspace. You need to configure access by following the principle of least privilege. What should you do?","opts":["Add the analyst to the workspace as a Viewer.","Share WH1 with the Read all data using SQL permission, and then run DENY statements on the other 38 tables.","Share WH1 with no additional permissions selected, and then run GRANT SELECT on the two tables for the analyst.","Share WH1 with the Read all data using Apache Spark permission."],"ans":2,"exp":"Sharing a warehouse with no additional permissions grants only Read, which is equivalent to CONNECT. Microsoft recommends this approach for granular access: share with Read only, then use T-SQL GRANT to provide access to the specific objects. The analyst can query only the two granted tables.","why":["Viewers get ReadData on the whole warehouse and access to other items in the workspace, and the analyst must not join the workspace.","This starts from full read access and relies on maintaining DENY statements, which is not least privilege and becomes fragile when new tables are added.","","ReadAll exposes all warehouse files through Spark and OneLake."],"ar":"شارك الـ warehouse بصلاحية Read فقط ثم استخدم GRANT SELECT على الجداول المطلوبة.","ref":[{"t":"Share your data and manage permissions","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/share-warehouse-manage-permissions"}]},{"id":"MT-S-007","d":"maintain","s":"security","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"Item permissions","q":"You have a semantic model named SM1 in a Fabric workspace named WS1. A business analyst who is not a member of WS1 must be able to create new Power BI reports that connect to SM1 and use Analyze in Excel against SM1. The analyst must NOT be able to modify SM1. Which permission should you grant on SM1?","opts":["Read only","Read and Build","Read and Write","Read and Reshare"],"ans":1,"exp":"Build permission allows users to build new content from a semantic model, such as reports, composite models, and Analyze in Excel, and to query it through XMLA. Read allows viewing reports that use the model. Write is only needed to republish or modify the model.","why":["Read alone lets the user interact with existing reports but not build new content on the model.","","Write allows republishing and modifying the model, which exceeds the requirement.","Reshare lets the user grant access to others; it does not allow building content."],"ar":"صلاحية Build على الـ semantic model تسمح بإنشاء تقارير واستخدام Analyze in Excel بدون تعديل النموذج.","ref":[{"t":"Semantic model permissions","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/connect-data/service-datasets-permissions"},{"t":"Build permission for shared semantic models","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/connect-data/service-datasets-build-permissions"}]},{"id":"MT-S-008","d":"maintain","s":"security","type":"yesno","diff":2,"tag":"Item permissions","q":"You have a semantic model named SM1 that has dynamic row-level security (RLS) roles. You share SM1 with several users who are not members of the workspace.<br>For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.","opts":["A user who has the Reshare permission on SM1 can grant other users access to SM1.","A user who has the Build permission on SM1 can query SM1 through the XMLA endpoint.","A Viewer who has the Build permission on SM1 sees all rows, ignoring RLS, when using Analyze in Excel."],"ans":[true,true,false],"exp":"Reshare allows a user to grant semantic model access to others. Build allows building new content, using Analyze in Excel, and querying by using external APIs such as XMLA. RLS still applies to users with only Read or Build permission; only Write permission (for example through Admin, Member, or Contributor roles) bypasses RLS.","why":["Reshare allows the user to grant semantic model access.","Build allows querying using external APIs such as XMLA.","Even if Viewers have Build permission, RLS still applies, including in Analyze in Excel."],"ar":"Build لا يتجاوز RLS، فقط صلاحية Write تجعل المستخدم يرى كل البيانات.","ref":[{"t":"Semantic model permissions","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/connect-data/service-datasets-permissions"},{"t":"Row-level security (RLS) with Power BI","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/security/service-admin-row-level-security"}]},{"id":"MT-S-009","d":"maintain","s":"security","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Item permissions","q":"You have a Fabric workspace named WS1 that contains a lakehouse named LH1. User1 is a Contributor in WS1. User1 needs to share LH1 with members of the auditing team, who must not get access to any other item in WS1. The Share option is unavailable to User1. You need to enable User1 to share LH1 by following the principle of least privilege. What should you do?","opts":["Change User1 to the Admin role in WS1.","Grant User1 the Share (Reshare) permission on LH1.","Grant User1 the Read all with Apache Spark permission on LH1.","Add User1 to a OneLake security role that grants Read on all folders of LH1."],"ans":1,"exp":"Workspace Admins and Members can share lakehouses by default. Contributors and Viewers can share a lakehouse only if they are granted the Share (Reshare) permission on it. Granting Reshare on LH1 alone is the least privileged option.","why":["Admin would allow sharing but also full control of the workspace, which is excessive.","","ReadAll controls data access through Spark and OneLake, not the ability to share.","OneLake security roles grant data read access only; they do not grant sharing rights."],"ar":"الـ Contributor لا يستطيع مشاركة lakehouse إلا إذا حصل على صلاحية Reshare على العنصر.","ref":[{"t":"How lakehouse sharing works","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-engineering/lakehouse-sharing"},{"t":"Roles in workspaces in Microsoft Fabric","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/roles-workspaces"}]},{"id":"MT-S-010","d":"maintain","s":"security","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"Semantic model RLS","q":"You are developing a semantic model named SM1 in Power BI Desktop. SM1 contains a table named <code>SalesRep</code> that has a column named <code>RepEmail</code> storing each sales rep's Microsoft Entra sign-in name. You need to create a single RLS role that filters the data so that each rep sees only their own rows after SM1 is published. Which DAX filter expression should you use on the <code>SalesRep</code> table?","opts":["<code>[RepEmail] = USERPRINCIPALNAME()</code>","<code>[RepEmail] = CUSTOMDATA()</code>","<code>[RepEmail] = USERCULTURE()</code>","<code>[RepEmail] = USEROBJECTID()</code>"],"ans":0,"exp":"Dynamic RLS commonly compares a user mapping column to USERPRINCIPALNAME(), which returns the signed-in user's UPN (for example user@contoso.com). One role then filters data differently for each user. The filter propagates from SalesRep to related fact tables through relationships.","why":["","CUSTOMDATA() returns a custom string passed by an embedding application, not the signed-in user's identity.","USERCULTURE() returns the user's locale, such as en-US.","USEROBJECTID() returns the Entra object ID (a GUID), which does not match an email or UPN column."],"ar":"في dynamic RLS قارن عمود البريد بالدالة USERPRINCIPALNAME() داخل role واحد.","ref":[{"t":"Row-level security (RLS) with Power BI","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/security/service-admin-row-level-security"}]},{"id":"MT-S-011","d":"maintain","s":"security","type":"order","diff":2,"tag":"Semantic model RLS","q":"You are implementing row-level security for an import semantic model named SM1 that will be published to a Fabric workspace named WS1. Regional managers will be Viewers in WS1 and must see only their region's data. You need to implement and validate RLS. Which four actions should you perform in sequence?","opts":["In Power BI Desktop, open Manage roles and create a role that has a DAX filter on the Region table.","Publish SM1 to WS1.","In the Power BI service, open the Security page of SM1 and add the managers' security group to the role.","Use Test as role in the Power BI service to validate the filtered results.","In Power BI Desktop, add the managers' email addresses as members of the role.","Create a security policy with CREATE SECURITY POLICY in the SQL analytics endpoint."],"ans":[0,1,2,3],"exp":"The RLS workflow is: define roles and DAX rules in Power BI Desktop, publish the model, add members to roles in the Power BI service (Security page), and validate by using Test as role. You cannot assign users to a role inside Power BI Desktop.","why":["Step 1: define roles and rules.","Step 2: publish.","Step 3: add members in the service.","Step 4: validate with Test as role.","Role membership cannot be assigned in Power BI Desktop.","Security policies are warehouse RLS, not semantic model RLS."],"ar":"عرّف الـ roles في Desktop، انشر النموذج، أضف الأعضاء في الخدمة، ثم تحقق باستخدام Test as role.","ref":[{"t":"Row-level security (RLS) with Power BI","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/security/service-admin-row-level-security"}]},{"id":"MT-S-012","d":"maintain","s":"security","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Warehouse RLS","q":"You have a Fabric warehouse named WH1 that contains a table named <code>sales.Orders</code>. You need to ensure that each sales rep can read only the rows where the <code>SalesRep</code> column matches their user name when querying WH1 by using T-SQL. You plan to use <code>CREATE SECURITY POLICY ... ADD FILTER PREDICATE</code>. Which type of object must you create to use as the filter predicate?","opts":["A view that filters sales.Orders by USER_NAME()","An inline table-valued function created WITH SCHEMABINDING","A stored procedure that returns the allowed rows","A scalar function that returns a bit value"],"ans":1,"exp":"In Fabric Data Warehouse, row-level security is implemented with CREATE SECURITY POLICY and predicates created as inline table-valued functions. The function returns a row (for example SELECT 1 AS result) when the access condition, such as @SalesRep = USER_NAME(), is met, and the security policy binds it to the table.","why":["A view can filter rows, but users could still query the base table and a view cannot be used as a security predicate.","","Stored procedures cannot be used as security predicates.","Security predicates must be inline table-valued functions, not scalar functions."],"ar":"الـ RLS في Warehouse تستخدم security policy مع inline table-valued function كـ predicate.","ref":[{"t":"Row-level security in Fabric Data Warehouse","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/row-level-security"}]},{"id":"MT-S-013","d":"maintain","s":"security","type":"order","diff":3,"tag":"Warehouse RLS","q":"You have a Fabric warehouse named WH1. A security policy named <code>SalesFilter</code> uses the inline function <code>Security.tvf_securitypredicate</code> as a filter predicate on <code>sales.Orders</code>. The policy was created with the default SCHEMABINDING setting. You need to change the logic of the predicate function so that the regional director can see all rows. Which three actions should you perform in sequence?","opts":["Run <code>DROP SECURITY POLICY SalesFilter</code>.","Run <code>ALTER FUNCTION Security.tvf_securitypredicate</code> with the new logic.","Run <code>CREATE SECURITY POLICY SalesFilter ADD FILTER PREDICATE Security.tvf_securitypredicate(SalesRep) ON sales.Orders WITH (STATE = ON)</code>.","Run <code>ALTER TABLE sales.Orders DROP COLUMN SalesRep</code>.","Grant the director the UNMASK permission on sales.Orders."],"ans":[0,1,2],"exp":"A function used as a predicate in a schema-bound security policy cannot be modified; attempting to do so returns an error. You must first drop the security policy, alter the function, and then re-create the policy. Microsoft Learn shows this exact sequence for Fabric Data Warehouse.","why":["Step 1: remove the schema binding by dropping the policy.","Step 2: change the predicate logic.","Step 3: re-create and enable the policy.","Altering columns referenced by the predicate is blocked and would break the security design.","UNMASK relates to dynamic data masking, not row-level security."],"ar":"لتعديل دالة الـ predicate يجب حذف الـ security policy أولًا ثم تعديل الدالة ثم إعادة إنشاء الـ policy.","ref":[{"t":"Row-level security in Fabric Data Warehouse","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/row-level-security"}]},{"id":"MT-S-014","d":"maintain","s":"security","type":"multi","n":2,"diff":3,"tag":"Warehouse RLS","q":"You have a Fabric warehouse named WH1. You create and enable a security policy that applies a filter predicate to the <code>sales.Orders</code> table. A Power BI semantic model named SM1 uses Direct Lake on SQL analytics endpoints, with single sign-on and the default Direct Lake behavior (Automatic), to read WH1. Which two statements are true? Each correct answer presents a complete solution. Select TWO.","opts":["The filter predicate also filters rows for members of the db_owner role unless the predicate logic explicitly allows them.","SM1 queries against sales.Orders fall back to DirectQuery so that RLS is enforced.","Users can no longer INSERT rows that the predicate would filter out.","The security policy automatically applies to every table in the sales schema."],"ans":[0,1],"exp":"Security policies apply to all users, including dbo and db_owner members, so the predicate must be written to let administrators see all rows if needed. With Direct Lake on SQL analytics endpoints, queries that reference a table with SQL RLS fall back to DirectQuery so that the RLS is enforced for the report user. (Direct Lake on OneLake would not apply SQL-based RLS, because it reads the Delta files through OneLake.) Filter predicates do not block INSERT, and each table needs its own RLS definition.","why":["","","The application can INSERT rows even if they will be filtered for other operations.","Each table must have its own row-level security defined separately."],"ar":"الـ security policy تُطبق على الجميع بما فيهم db_owner، وDirect Lake on SQL يتحول إلى DirectQuery لاحترام RLS.","ref":[{"t":"Row-level security in Fabric Data Warehouse","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/row-level-security"}]},{"id":"MT-S-015","d":"maintain","s":"security","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"Column-level security","q":"You have a Fabric warehouse named WH1 that contains a table named <code>dbo.Customers</code> with the columns CustomerID, Name, City, and CreditCard. You need to allow a Microsoft Entra group named SupportTeam to query every column except CreditCard. Which T-SQL statement should you run?","opts":["<code>GRANT SELECT ON dbo.Customers (CustomerID, Name, City) TO [SupportTeam];</code>","<code>ALTER TABLE dbo.Customers ALTER COLUMN CreditCard ADD MASKED WITH (FUNCTION = 'default()');</code>","<code>CREATE SECURITY POLICY CustFilter ADD FILTER PREDICATE dbo.fn_cc(CreditCard) ON dbo.Customers;</code>","<code>GRANT SELECT ON dbo.Customers TO [SupportTeam];</code>"],"ans":0,"exp":"Column-level security in Fabric Data Warehouse is implemented with the GRANT statement listing only the permitted columns. Queries that reference CreditCard, including SELECT *, fail with a permission error for the group.","why":["","Dynamic data masking obfuscates values but still lets users query the column, so it does not prevent access.","A security policy filters rows, not columns.","This grants access to all columns, including CreditCard."],"ar":"Column-level security في Warehouse تتم عبر GRANT SELECT على أعمدة محددة.","ref":[{"t":"Column-level security in Fabric Data Warehouse","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/column-level-security"}]},{"id":"MT-S-016","d":"maintain","s":"security","type":"yesno","diff":2,"tag":"Column-level security","q":"You have a Fabric warehouse named WH1. You run <code>GRANT SELECT ON dbo.Employees (EmployeeID, Name, Dept) TO [Analysts];</code>. The Employees table also contains a Salary column.<br>For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.","opts":["When a member of Analysts runs <code>SELECT * FROM dbo.Employees</code>, the query fails with a permission error on Salary.","A member of Analysts can successfully run <code>SELECT EmployeeID, Name FROM dbo.Employees</code>.","Column-level security in WH1 can be enforced for users who connect by using SQL authentication logins."],"ans":[true,true,false],"exp":"With column-level security, a query that includes a denied column, such as SELECT *, fails with \"The SELECT permission was denied on the column\". Queries that reference only the granted columns succeed. Only Microsoft Entra authentication is supported.","why":["SELECT * includes Salary, so the query is denied.","The query references only granted columns, so it succeeds.","Only Microsoft Entra authentication is supported in Fabric Warehouse."],"ar":"مع CLS يفشل SELECT * إذا شمل عمودًا محظورًا، بينما ينجح الاستعلام على الأعمدة المسموح بها فقط.","ref":[{"t":"Column-level security in Fabric Data Warehouse","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/column-level-security"}]},{"id":"MT-S-017","d":"maintain","s":"security","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Object-level security","q":"You have an import semantic model named SM1 that contains an Employee table. Report viewers in the Sales group must not be able to see the Salary column, including its name and metadata. Viewers in the HR group must see the column. When Sales users open a report that uses Salary, the visual should behave as if the column does not exist. What should you implement?","opts":["A row-level security role with a DAX filter on the Employee table","Object-level security in a role that sets the Salary column permission to None","Set the Salary column property Is hidden to Yes in Model view","Dynamic data masking on the Salary column"],"ans":1,"exp":"Object-level security (OLS) secures specific tables or columns, including their names and metadata, from report viewers. OLS is defined within model roles by setting the table or column metadataPermission to None. For users without permission, it is as if the secured column does not exist.","why":["RLS restricts rows, not columns; a user who sees a row sees all its columns.","","Hiding a column only affects the field list; it is not a security feature, and the data can still be queried.","Dynamic data masking is a Fabric Data Warehouse feature and does not apply to import semantic models."],"ar":"لإخفاء عمود وبياناته الوصفية في semantic model استخدم Object-level security بصلاحية None.","ref":[{"t":"Object-level security (OLS)","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/security/service-admin-object-level-security"}]},{"id":"MT-S-018","d":"maintain","s":"security","type":"multi","n":2,"diff":2,"tag":"Object-level security","q":"You need to define object-level security (OLS) for a table named Payroll in a semantic model named SM1 before publishing it. Which two tools or experiences can you use to define the OLS rule? Each correct answer presents a complete solution. Select TWO.","opts":["TMDL view in Power BI Desktop, by running a createOrReplace script that sets metadataPermission: none","Tabular Editor, by setting the Payroll table permission for the role to None","The Manage roles dialog in Power BI Desktop, by entering a DAX filter of FALSE() for Payroll","The Security page of SM1 in the Power BI service"],"ans":[0,1],"exp":"OLS is defined within model roles by using TMDL view or external tools such as Tabular Editor, where you set the table or column permission to None or Read. The Power BI service Security page is used only to assign members to roles, not to define OLS rules.","why":["","","A DAX filter of FALSE() is an RLS rule that hides rows, but the table and its metadata remain visible.","The Security page only assigns members and tests roles; it cannot define OLS."],"ar":"يتم تعريف OLS عبر TMDL view أو Tabular Editor وليس من واجهة Manage roles.","ref":[{"t":"Object-level security (OLS)","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/security/service-admin-object-level-security"}]},{"id":"MT-S-019","d":"maintain","s":"security","type":"yesno","diff":3,"tag":"Object-level security","q":"You publish a semantic model named SM1 that has a role named RestrictedRole. RestrictedRole sets the metadataPermission of the <code>Customer[Phone]</code> column to none. Users are assigned to RestrictedRole.<br>For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.","opts":["A Viewer in RestrictedRole who opens a report that uses Phone receives a message that the field cannot be found.","A Contributor in the workspace who is also in RestrictedRole cannot see the Phone column.","Row-level security could be used instead to hide only the Phone column from RestrictedRole users."],"ans":[true,false,false],"exp":"Users without permission receive a \"field can't be found\" type message for visuals that use the secured column. OLS applies only to Viewers; Admin, Member, and Contributor have edit permission on the semantic model, so OLS does not apply to them. RLS cannot restrict columns; if a user can see a row, they see all its columns.","why":["Visuals using a secured field show that the field cannot be found.","OLS only applies to Viewers in a workspace.","RLS secures rows only; use OLS for columns."],"ar":"OLS تُطبق على Viewer فقط، ويظهر للمستخدم أن الحقل غير موجود.","ref":[{"t":"Object-level security (OLS)","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/security/service-admin-object-level-security"},{"t":"Row-level security (RLS) with Power BI","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/security/service-admin-row-level-security"}]},{"id":"MT-S-020","d":"maintain","s":"security","type":"order","diff":2,"tag":"OneLake security","q":"You have a lakehouse named LH1 in a workspace named WS1. Members of a group named FinanceReaders are Viewers in WS1. You need to ensure that FinanceReaders can read only the files in the <code>Files/Finance</code> folder of LH1 by using OneLake security. Which three actions should you perform in sequence?","opts":["Open LH1 and select Manage OneLake security, confirming that data access roles are enabled.","Select New role, choose Selected folders, and select Files/Finance.","Assign the role with Read permission to the FinanceReaders group and save.","Share LH1 with FinanceReaders and select Read all with Apache Spark.","Run GRANT SELECT on the Finance folder in the SQL analytics endpoint."],"ans":[0,1,2],"exp":"OneLake security (data access roles) is configured from the lakehouse ribbon by selecting Manage OneLake security, creating a role scoped to selected folders, and assigning people or groups to it. Folder permissions inherit to all subfolders. The lakehouse UI labels this experience as preview at the time of writing, so check the current release status.","why":["Step 1: open OneLake security management.","Step 2: create a role scoped to the folder.","Step 3: assign members.","ReadAll places users in the DefaultReader role, which gives access to all data in LH1.","T-SQL GRANT cannot secure folders in the Files section of OneLake."],"ar":"استخدم OneLake security role بنطاق Selected folders لإعطاء قراءة لمجلد محدد فقط.","ref":[{"t":"How lakehouse sharing works","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-engineering/lakehouse-sharing"},{"t":"How OneLake security controls data access","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/onelake/security/data-access-control-model"}]},{"id":"MT-S-021","d":"maintain","s":"security","type":"single","diff":3,"tag":"OneLake security","q":"A lakehouse named LH1 has the following structure:<pre>Files/\n  folder1/\n    file11.txt\n    subfolder11/\n      file111.txt\n      subfolder111/\n        file1111.txt\n  folder2/\n    file21.txt</pre>You create a OneLake security role named Role1 that grants Read on <code>Files/folder1/subfolder11</code> and assign User1, who is a Viewer, to Role1. Which files can User1 read?","opts":["file111.txt only","file111.txt and file1111.txt","file11.txt, file111.txt, and file1111.txt","All files in the Files section"],"ans":1,"exp":"OneLake folder permissions inherit downward to files and subfolders, so User1 can read file111.txt and everything in subfolder111. User1 can traverse folder1 to reach subfolder11, but traversal does not grant access to sibling items such as file11.txt.","why":["Permissions inherit to subfolders, so file1111.txt is also readable.","","Traversal through folder1 does not grant access to its sibling file file11.txt.","Only the granted folder and its descendants are accessible."],"ar":"صلاحيات OneLake على مجلد تُورث للمجلدات الفرعية، والمرور عبر المجلد الأب لا يعطي وصولًا للملفات المجاورة.","ref":[{"t":"How OneLake security controls data access","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/onelake/security/data-access-control-model"}]},{"id":"MT-S-022","d":"maintain","s":"security","type":"single","diff":3,"tag":"OneLake security","q":"You configure OneLake security on a lakehouse named LH1 so that a role named EUOnly grants Read only to the <code>Files/EU</code> folder. User2 is assigned to EUOnly and is a Contributor in the workspace. User2 can still read and write every folder in LH1. You need EUOnly to restrict User2's data access. What should you do?","opts":["Remove the DefaultReader role from LH1.","Change User2's workspace role to Viewer.","Add a Deny role for the other folders.","Grant User2 the ReadWrite permission in EUOnly."],"ans":1,"exp":"OneLake security mainly governs users with Read access, such as Viewers. Admin, Member, and Contributor roles already have data access through the workspace Write permission, so OneLake security roles do not restrict them. Changing User2 to Viewer makes the EUOnly role effective.","why":["DefaultReader applies to users with ReadAll; it does not affect the elevated access that a Contributor has.","","OneLake security supports only Grant roles; Deny roles are not supported.","ReadWrite has no effect for Admin, Member, or Contributor, and it would add write access rather than restrict it."],"ar":"أدوار OneLake security لا تقيّد Admin أو Member أو Contributor، بل تُطبق على Viewer.","ref":[{"t":"How OneLake security controls data access","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/onelake/security/data-access-control-model"},{"t":"Data security in OneLake","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/onelake/security/get-started-security"}]},{"id":"MT-S-023","d":"maintain","s":"security","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"Dynamic data masking","q":"You have a Fabric warehouse named WH1 that contains a table named <code>dbo.Customers</code>. Support agents must see email addresses in the format <code>aXXX@XXXX.com</code>, exposing only the first letter. The stored data must not change. Which statement should you run?","opts":["<code>ALTER TABLE dbo.Customers ALTER COLUMN Email ADD MASKED WITH (FUNCTION = 'email()');</code>","<code>ALTER TABLE dbo.Customers ALTER COLUMN Email ADD MASKED WITH (FUNCTION = 'default()');</code>","<code>ALTER TABLE dbo.Customers ALTER COLUMN Email ADD MASKED WITH (FUNCTION = 'random(1, 100)');</code>","<code>GRANT SELECT ON dbo.Customers (CustomerID, Name) TO [SupportAgents];</code>"],"ans":0,"exp":"Dynamic data masking hides sensitive values in query results without changing the stored data. The email() masking function exposes the first letter and a constant .com suffix, producing aXXX@XXXX.com.","why":["","default() fully masks strings as XXXX, so the first letter is not exposed.","random() is for numeric columns only.","Column-level security blocks the column entirely instead of showing a masked value."],"ar":"دالة email() في dynamic data masking تُظهر الحرف الأول فقط بصيغة aXXX@XXXX.com.","ref":[{"t":"Dynamic data masking in Fabric Data Warehouse","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/dynamic-data-masking"}]},{"id":"MT-S-024","d":"maintain","s":"security","type":"multi","n":3,"diff":2,"tag":"Dynamic data masking","q":"You have a Fabric warehouse named WH1 in a workspace named WS1. You apply a dynamic data mask to the <code>Phone</code> column of <code>dbo.Customers</code>. Which three users will see unmasked phone numbers when querying the table? Each correct answer presents a complete solution. Select THREE.","opts":["User1, a Member of WS1","User2, a Contributor in WS1","User3, a Viewer in WS1 who has only SELECT on dbo.Customers","User4, a Viewer in WS1 who has been granted UNMASK on the Phone column"],"ans":[0,1,3],"exp":"Admin, Member, and Contributor roles have CONTROL permission on the warehouse database by design, which includes UNMASK, so they see unmasked data. Other users see masked values unless they are granted UNMASK on the column or CONTROL on the database. SELECT alone returns masked values.","why":["","","SELECT only allows viewing masked data.",""],"ar":"أدوار Admin وMember وContributor ترى البيانات بدون قناع، وغيرهم يحتاج صلاحية UNMASK.","ref":[{"t":"Dynamic data masking in Fabric Data Warehouse","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/dynamic-data-masking"}]},{"id":"MT-S-025","d":"maintain","s":"security","type":"yesno","diff":3,"tag":"Sensitivity labels","q":"Your Fabric tenant has sensitivity labels enabled and the <b>Automatically apply sensitivity labels to downstream content</b> tenant setting turned on. A semantic model named SM1 has five existing downstream reports. Report R1 has a manually applied label of Confidential.<br>For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.","opts":["If you change the label of SM1 to Highly Confidential, the label of R1 is overwritten with Highly Confidential.","If the downstream setting is later turned off, a new report created from SM1 still inherits the label of SM1.","If you change the label of SM1 to a less restrictive label, downstream reports that have a more restrictive label are downgraded."],"ans":[false,true,false],"exp":"Downstream inheritance never overwrites manually applied labels and never replaces a label with a less restrictive one. Inheritance upon creation is a separate feature: a new report created from a labeled semantic model inherits its label even if the downstream inheritance setting is disabled.","why":["Downstream inheritance never overwrites labels applied manually.","Inheritance upon creation happens regardless of the downstream setting.","Downstream inheritance never applies a less restrictive label."],"ar":"الـ downstream inheritance لا يستبدل label يدوي ولا يطبق label أقل تقييدًا.","ref":[{"t":"Sensitivity label downstream inheritance","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/governance/service-security-sensitivity-label-downstream-inheritance"}]},{"id":"MT-S-026","d":"maintain","s":"security","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"Sensitivity labels","q":"Fabrikam has sensitivity labels from Microsoft Purview published to all users. User1 has a Power BI Pro license and is a Viewer in a workspace named WS1. User1 must apply the Confidential label to a lakehouse named LH1 in WS1, but the sensitivity option is unavailable. You need to enable User1 to apply the label. What should you do?","opts":["Grant User1 the Build permission on a semantic model that uses LH1.","Assign User1 a role that provides edit permissions on LH1, such as Contributor.","Share LH1 with User1 and select Read all with Apache Spark.","Endorse LH1 as Certified."],"ans":1,"exp":"To apply a sensitivity label to a Fabric item, a user needs a Power BI Pro or PPU license and edit permissions on the item. A Viewer does not have edit permission, so assigning a role such as Contributor enables labeling.","why":["Build lets a user create content from a semantic model; it is not edit permission on LH1.","","ReadAll is a data read permission, not edit permission.","Endorsement is unrelated to the ability to apply sensitivity labels."],"ar":"لتطبيق sensitivity label تحتاج رخصة Pro أو PPU وصلاحية تعديل على العنصر.","ref":[{"t":"Apply sensitivity labels to Fabric items","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/apply-sensitivity-labels"}]},{"id":"MT-S-027","d":"maintain","s":"security","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"Endorsement","q":"Your organization wants a small group of data stewards to mark semantic models as Certified after a quality review. A report developer who is an Admin of the workspace that contains the semantic model SM1 tries to certify SM1 but the Certified option is disabled. Who can certify SM1?","opts":["Any user who has write permission on SM1","Any workspace Admin","Only users specified by a Fabric administrator in the certification settings","Only the owner of SM1"],"ans":2,"exp":"Any user can request certification, but only users specified by a Fabric administrator can certify items. Certification must be enabled by a Fabric admin, and its enablement can be delegated to domain admins to define different reviewers per domain.","why":["Write permission is enough to promote an item, not to certify it.","Being a workspace Admin does not grant certification rights.","","Item ownership does not grant certification rights."],"ar":"الـ Certified يمنحه فقط المستخدمون الذين يحددهم Fabric administrator.","ref":[{"t":"Endorsement overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/governance/endorsement-overview"}]},{"id":"MT-S-028","d":"maintain","s":"security","type":"multi","n":2,"diff":2,"tag":"Endorsement","q":"You manage endorsement for a Fabric tenant. Which two statements about endorsement badges are true? Each correct answer presents a complete solution. Select TWO.","opts":["Any user who has write permissions on an item can promote the item.","The Master data badge can be applied only to items that contain data, such as lakehouses and semantic models.","Power BI dashboards can be certified.","Workspace Viewers can promote any item in the workspace."],"ans":[0,1],"exp":"Promoted can be applied by any user with write permissions on an item. Master data can be applied only to items that contain data, and only by users specified by the Fabric administrator. All Fabric and Power BI items except Power BI dashboards can be promoted or certified.","why":["","","Dashboards are the exception: they cannot be promoted or certified.","Viewers do not have write permissions, so they cannot promote items."],"ar":"الـ Promoted لمن لديه صلاحية write، والـ Master data فقط للعناصر التي تحتوي على بيانات.","ref":[{"t":"Endorsement overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/governance/endorsement-overview"}]},{"id":"MT-L-001","d":"maintain","s":"lifecycle","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"Git integration","q":"Contoso has a Fabric workspace named WS1 on an F64 capacity. The team wants to version the items in WS1 by using the built-in Git integration in the workspace settings. Which Git providers can you select when you connect WS1?","opts":["Azure DevOps and GitHub (cloud-based repositories)","Azure DevOps Server hosted on-premises only","Bitbucket Cloud and GitLab","Any Git server that exposes an HTTPS clone URL"],"ans":0,"exp":"Fabric Git integration works at the workspace level and supports cloud-based Azure DevOps, GitHub and GitHub Enterprise repositories. A workspace admin connects the workspace to a branch and folder in the repository, and item definitions are then committed and updated from the Source control pane.","why":["","On-premises Azure DevOps Server is not supported. Only the cloud-based Azure DevOps service is supported.","Bitbucket and GitLab are not supported Git providers for Fabric Git integration.","Fabric does not accept a generic Git remote. You must use one of the supported providers."],"ar":"تكامل Git في Fabric يدعم Azure DevOps و GitHub السحابيين فقط على مستوى الـ workspace.","ref":[{"t":"What is Microsoft Fabric Git integration?","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/cicd/git-integration/intro-to-git-integration"}]},{"id":"MT-L-002","d":"maintain","s":"lifecycle","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Git integration","q":"You have a Fabric workspace named WS1. You are assigned the Member role in WS1 and you have Contribute permission on an Azure DevOps repository named Repo1. You need to connect WS1 to the main branch of Repo1. You open Workspace settings, but you cannot connect the workspace. What is the minimum change required so that you can connect WS1 to Repo1?","opts":["Be assigned the Admin role in WS1","Be granted Build permission on every semantic model in WS1","Be granted the Project Administrator role in the Azure DevOps project","Be assigned the Fabric administrator role in the tenant"],"ans":0,"exp":"Connecting, syncing and disconnecting a workspace from a Git repository are workspace Admin operations. After an admin connects the workspace, Members and Contributors can commit and update items, depending on their permissions in the Git repository.","why":["","Build permission lets users create content on a semantic model. It does not grant rights to change the workspace Git connection.","For Azure Repos, connecting only requires Read permission in the repository. The blocker is the Fabric workspace role, not the Azure DevOps role.","A Fabric administrator manages tenant settings but does not need to be involved. The workspace Admin role is what is required."],"ar":"ربط الـ workspace بمستودع Git يتطلب دور Admin في الـ workspace، أما Commit و Update فيكفي لهما Contributor.","ref":[{"t":"Basic concepts in Git integration","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/cicd/git-integration/git-integration-process"},{"t":"Get started with Git integration","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/cicd/git-integration/git-get-started"}]},{"id":"MT-L-003","d":"maintain","s":"lifecycle","type":"yesno","diff":2,"tag":"Git integration","q":"You have a Fabric workspace named WS1 that contains a lakehouse, a notebook, a semantic model and a report. You plan to connect WS1 to a GitHub repository.<br>For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.","opts":["If WS1 contains an item type that Git integration does not support, WS1 can still be connected and the unsupported item is ignored by commit and update operations.","When you commit the semantic model to Git, the imported table data is stored in the repository together with the model metadata.","If both WS1 and the target branch already contain items when you connect, you must choose the direction of the initial sync."],"ans":[true,false,true],"exp":"Unsupported items do not block the connection: they are not saved or synced, and they are not deleted. Git integration stores item definitions (metadata) only, never the data. During the initial connect, if both the workspace and branch have content, Fabric asks whether to commit the workspace to Git or update the workspace from Git.","why":["Yes. Unsupported items are not saved or synced to Git, and they are not deleted from the workspace.","No. Only item definitions (metadata) are exported to Git. Data is not exported.","Yes. When both sides have content you must pick commit to Git or update the workspace before you can continue."],"ar":"Git في Fabric يحفظ تعريفات العناصر (metadata) فقط وليس البيانات، والعناصر غير المدعومة يتم تجاهلها.","ref":[{"t":"What is Microsoft Fabric Git integration?","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/cicd/git-integration/intro-to-git-integration"},{"t":"Basic concepts in Git integration","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/cicd/git-integration/git-integration-process"},{"t":"Git integration tenant settings","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/admin/git-integration-admin-settings"}]},{"id":"MT-L-004","d":"maintain","s":"lifecycle","type":"single","diff":3,"tag":"Git branches","q":"You have a Fabric workspace named WS1 that is connected to the dev branch of an Azure DevOps repository. User1 has the Contributor role in WS1, write permission on all items in WS1 and Create branch permission in the repository. User1 reports that the <b>Switch branch</b> and <b>Checkout new branch</b> options are unavailable in the Source control pane. You need to allow User1 to switch WS1 to another branch without granting the Admin role. What should you do?","opts":["As a workspace admin, enable the Allow users with at least Contributor role to change Git branch setting for WS1","Grant User1 Build permission on all semantic models in WS1","Ask the Fabric administrator to enable the Users can synchronize workspace items with their Git repositories tenant setting","Assign User1 the Project Administrator role in the Azure DevOps project"],"ans":0,"exp":"By default, switching branches and checking out a new branch require the workspace Admin role. A workspace admin can enable the workspace-level setting that allows Members and Contributors with write access to all items to change the Git branch. Connecting and disconnecting remain Admin-only.","why":["","Build permission is unrelated to Git branch operations.","This tenant setting is already enabled, because WS1 is connected to Git. It does not change who can switch branches.","The Git role is not the blocker. Branch switching is restricted by the Fabric workspace role unless the workspace setting is enabled."],"ar":"تغيير الفرع (Switch branch) يتطلب Admin افتراضيا، ويمكن للـ Admin تفعيل إعداد يسمح للـ Contributor بذلك.","ref":[{"t":"Get started with Git integration","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/cicd/git-integration/git-get-started"},{"t":"Basic concepts in Git integration","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/cicd/git-integration/git-integration-process"}]},{"id":"MT-L-005","d":"maintain","s":"lifecycle","type":"order","diff":2,"tag":"Git branches","q":"You have a Fabric workspace named WS1 that is connected to the main branch of a GitHub repository. You need to develop a change to a notebook in isolation and then bring the change back into WS1 after a code review. Which four actions should you perform in sequence?","opts":["From the Source control pane in WS1, select Branch out to another workspace to create a workspace connected to a new feature branch","Modify the notebook in the new workspace and commit the change to the feature branch","Create and complete a pull request that merges the feature branch into main","In WS1, open the Source control pane and update the workspace from Git","Disconnect WS1 from Git and reconnect it to the feature branch","Use a deployment pipeline to deploy the new workspace to WS1"],"ans":[0,1,2,3],"exp":"Branch out creates a feature workspace connected to a new branch based on the last commit of the source workspace. You commit your work there, merge it through a pull request in the Git provider, and then run Update from Git in WS1 so that the workspace picks up the merged changes from main.","why":["Step 1: creates an isolated feature workspace and branch.","Step 2: commits the change to the feature branch.","Step 3: code review and merge into main happen in the Git provider.","Step 4: WS1 pulls the merged commit from main.","Distractor: disconnecting WS1 is unnecessary and would move the shared workspace off main.","Distractor: deployment pipelines promote between stages, not from a feature workspace back into its Git-connected source."],"ar":"نمط العمل: Branch out ثم Commit ثم Pull request ثم Update من Git في الـ workspace الأصلي.","ref":[{"t":"Development process using branch workspace","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/cicd/git-integration/branched-workspace"},{"t":"Basic concepts in Git integration","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/cicd/git-integration/git-integration-process"}]},{"id":"MT-L-006","d":"maintain","s":"lifecycle","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"Power BI project (.pbip)","q":"Three developers at Fabrikam co-author a semantic model and a report in Power BI Desktop. They need to store the work in a Git repository, see line-level differences in pull requests, and edit measures in Visual Studio Code. Which file format should they save the work in?","opts":["A Power BI project (.pbip)","A Power BI template (.pbit)","A Power BI Desktop file (.pbix) stored in OneDrive","A Power BI data source file (.pbids)"],"ans":0,"exp":"A Power BI project saves the report and semantic model as folders of human-readable text files (TMDL for the model, PBIR for the report), which makes Git diffs, merges and external editing practical. Microsoft recommends .pbip when you plan to use source control, Git integration or multiple co-authors. Power BI Desktop projects are currently in preview.","why":["","A .pbit is intended for reusable starting templates, not for managing content during development.","A .pbix is a binary file, so Git cannot show meaningful differences or merge changes.","A .pbids file only describes a single data source connection. It contains no model or report."],"ar":"استخدم .pbip عندما تحتاج Source control والتعاون بين عدة مطورين لأن الملفات نصية وقابلة للمقارنة.","ref":[{"t":"Power BI Desktop projects","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/developer/projects/projects-overview"},{"t":"Power BI implementation planning: Develop content and manage changes","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/powerbi-implementation-planning-content-lifecycle-management-develop-manage"}]},{"id":"MT-L-007","d":"maintain","s":"lifecycle","type":"multi","n":2,"diff":2,"tag":"Power BI project (.pbip)","q":"You save a Power BI Desktop report named Sales as a Power BI project in a new folder. Power BI Desktop creates a .gitignore file. Which two files are excluded from source control by the default .gitignore? Each correct answer presents a complete solution. Select TWO.","opts":["cache.abf","localSettings.json","definition.pbism","definition.pbir","diagramLayout.json"],"ans":[0,1],"exp":"The default .gitignore created with a Power BI project contains **/.pbi/localSettings.json and **/.pbi/cache.abf. localSettings.json holds user and machine specific settings, and cache.abf is a local Analysis Services backup that contains cached data, so neither belongs in the repository.","why":["","","definition.pbism is required. It holds the overall semantic model definition and must be committed.","definition.pbir is required. It defines the report and its reference to the semantic model.","diagramLayout.json is part of the model folder and is committed with the project."],"ar":"ملفا cache.abf و localSettings.json يتم استبعادهما من Git لأنهما خاصان بالجهاز ويحتويان بيانات مخزنة.","ref":[{"t":"Power BI Desktop projects","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/developer/projects/projects-overview"},{"t":"Power BI Desktop project semantic model folder","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/developer/projects/projects-dataset"}]},{"id":"MT-L-008","d":"maintain","s":"lifecycle","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"TMDL","q":"You save a Power BI project with the <b>Store semantic model using TMDL format</b> option enabled. A developer wants to review the changes to a single table named Sales in a pull request without scrolling through the entire model definition. Where is the definition of the Sales table stored?","opts":["In its own .tmdl file inside the definition folder of the .SemanticModel folder","Inside a single model.bim file that uses TMSL JSON","Inside the report.json file of the .Report folder","Inside the .pbip file at the root of the project"],"ans":0,"exp":"When a project uses TMDL, the model.bim file is replaced by a definition folder that contains separate files for each table, role, culture and perspective. This folder structure keeps diffs small and readable, which improves source control and co-development.","why":["","model.bim is used only when the project is saved in TMSL format. TMDL replaces it.","report.json belongs to the report (PBIR-Legacy) and does not contain semantic model tables.","The .pbip file is only a pointer to the report folder. It contains no model metadata."],"ar":"مع TMDL يتم حفظ كل جدول في ملف منفصل داخل مجلد definition بدلا من ملف model.bim واحد.","ref":[{"t":"Power BI Desktop project semantic model folder","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/developer/projects/projects-dataset"}]},{"id":"MT-L-009","d":"maintain","s":"lifecycle","type":"yesno","diff":3,"tag":"PBIR","q":"Your team stores a Power BI project in Git. The report uses the PBIR format and the semantic model uses TMDL. A new developer clones the repository.<br>For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.","opts":["When the developer opens the .pbip file, Power BI Desktop opens the report and the semantic model definition without data, because cache.abf is not in the repository.","In the PBIR format, each report page and each visual is stored in its own JSON file.","The .pbip file contains the imported model data so that the project can be published without a refresh."],"ans":[true,true,false],"exp":"Power BI Desktop can open a project without cache.abf. It loads the full model definition but no data, so the developer must refresh. PBIR splits the report into a folder structure with separate files for pages, visuals and bookmarks, which simplifies merges. The .pbip file itself only points to the report folder. PBIR and Power BI projects are currently preview features.","why":["Yes. Without cache.abf the model opens with its definition but no data.","Yes. PBIR stores pages, visuals and bookmarks as individual JSON files.","No. The .pbip file only contains a pointer to the report folder, and data is never part of the committed project."],"ar":"مشروع .pbip بدون cache.abf يفتح النموذج بدون بيانات، و PBIR يحفظ كل صفحة و visual في ملف JSON مستقل.","ref":[{"t":"Power BI Desktop project semantic model folder","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/developer/projects/projects-dataset"},{"t":"Power BI Desktop project report folder","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/developer/projects/projects-report"}]},{"id":"MT-L-010","d":"maintain","s":"lifecycle","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"Deployment pipelines","q":"You are creating a Fabric deployment pipeline for Contoso. The release process requires Development, Integration, UAT, Pre-production and Production stages. What should you know about the number of stages?","opts":["A pipeline can have between 2 and 10 stages, and the number of stages cannot be changed after the pipeline is created","A pipeline always has exactly three stages named Development, Test and Production","A pipeline can have up to 5 stages, and stages can be added at any time","A pipeline can have unlimited stages, but only three can be assigned workspaces"],"ans":0,"exp":"A deployment pipeline has three default stages, but you can rename them and define anywhere from 2 to 10 stages when you create the pipeline. The number of stages is permanent after creation, so plan the stage layout before you select Create.","why":["","Three stages is only the default. You can change the number and the names.","The limit is 10 stages, and the count cannot be changed after creation.","Every stage can be assigned one workspace, and the number of stages is limited to 10."],"ar":"الـ deployment pipeline يدعم من 2 إلى 10 مراحل، وعدد المراحل ثابت بعد الإنشاء.","ref":[{"t":"Get started with deployment pipelines","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/cicd/deployment-pipelines/get-started-with-deployment-pipelines"}]},{"id":"MT-L-011","d":"maintain","s":"lifecycle","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Deployment rules","q":"You have a deployment pipeline with Development, Test and Production stages. An Import mode semantic model named SM1 connects to a SQL Server database named SalesDev. You own SM1. After deployment to Production, SM1 must connect to a SQL Server database named SalesProd. SM1 does not use query parameters. What should you configure?","opts":["A data source rule for SM1 in the Production stage","A data source rule for SM1 in the Development stage","A parameter rule for SM1 in the Production stage","A default lakehouse rule for SM1 in the Production stage"],"ans":0,"exp":"Deployment rules are defined on the target stage for a specific item. A data source rule replaces the data source value when content is deployed into that stage, and it only works when switching to a data source of the same type. The rule takes effect at the next deployment into Production.","why":["","Deployment rules cannot be created in the development stage. They are set on the target stage.","A parameter rule requires a parameter in the model. SM1 does not use parameters.","Default lakehouse rules apply only to notebooks."],"ar":"قاعدة data source تنشأ على المرحلة الهدف (مثل Production) وتطبق عند النشر التالي.","ref":[{"t":"Create deployment rules","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/cicd/deployment-pipelines/create-rules"}]},{"id":"MT-L-012","d":"maintain","s":"lifecycle","type":"multi","n":2,"diff":2,"tag":"Deployment pipelines","q":"You deploy a semantic model named SM1 and a report named R1 from the Development stage to an empty Test stage of a deployment pipeline. Which two settings of SM1 must you configure again in the Test workspace because they are NOT copied by the deployment? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. Select TWO.","opts":["The scheduled refresh configuration","The data source credentials","The measures and calculated columns","The relationships between tables","The report pages of R1"],"ans":[0,1],"exp":"Deployment copies metadata such as model metadata, relationships, report pages, visuals, data sources and parameters. It does not copy data, permissions, role assignments, refresh schedules, data source credentials, query caching settings or endorsement. After the first deployment you must set credentials and a refresh schedule in the target stage and refresh the model.","why":["","","Measures and calculated columns are model metadata, which is copied.","Relationships are part of model metadata and item relationships, which are copied.","Report pages and visuals are copied during deployment."],"ar":"النشر عبر pipeline ينسخ الـ metadata فقط، ولا ينسخ البيانات أو بيانات الاعتماد أو جدول التحديث.","ref":[{"t":"The deployment pipelines process","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/cicd/deployment-pipelines/understand-the-deployment-process"}]},{"id":"MT-L-013","d":"maintain","s":"lifecycle","type":"single","diff":3,"tag":"Deployment rules","q":"You have a deployment pipeline. The Development workspace contains a lakehouse named LH1 and a semantic model named SM1 that uses Direct Lake on SQL analytics endpoints over LH1. You deploy LH1 and SM1 to the Test stage. Reports in Test show data from the Development lakehouse. You need SM1 in Test to use the lakehouse in the Test workspace. What should you do?","opts":["Create a data source rule for SM1 in the Test stage that points to the Test lakehouse, and then deploy SM1 again","Refresh SM1 in the Test workspace so that it binds automatically to the paired lakehouse","Create a default lakehouse rule for SM1 in the Test stage","Create a parameter rule for LH1 in the Test stage"],"ans":0,"exp":"Unlike other semantic models, a Direct Lake semantic model is not automatically bound to the paired item in the target stage. After deployment it still points to the lakehouse in the source stage. Microsoft documents using a data source rule to bind it to the item in the target stage; the rule applies at the next deployment. Note that deployment pipeline rules to rebind the data source are supported for Direct Lake on SQL analytics endpoints but not for Direct Lake on OneLake.","why":["","Refreshing does not change the binding. Direct Lake models do not autobind to the target stage item.","Default lakehouse rules are only available for notebooks.","Lakehouses do not support parameter rules, and the binding belongs to the semantic model, not the lakehouse."],"ar":"نموذج Direct Lake لا يرتبط تلقائيا بالـ lakehouse في المرحلة الهدف، استخدم data source rule.","ref":[{"t":"Direct Lake overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-overview"},{"t":"The deployment pipelines process","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/cicd/deployment-pipelines/understand-the-deployment-process"},{"t":"Assign a workspace to a deployment pipeline","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/cicd/deployment-pipelines/assign-pipeline"}]},{"id":"MT-L-014","d":"maintain","s":"lifecycle","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Deployment pipelines","q":"Contoso already has a production workspace named WS_Prod that contains 20 items. You create a deployment pipeline with Development, Test and Production stages and assign WS_Prod to the Production stage. The Development and Test stages are empty. You need to populate the Test stage with the content of WS_Prod. What should you do?","opts":["Deploy all content backward from the Production stage to the empty Test stage","Select only the semantic models in Production and deploy them backward to Test","Assign WS_Prod to the Test stage as well as the Production stage","Create a deployment rule in the Test stage that references WS_Prod"],"ans":0,"exp":"Backward deployment lets you deploy content from a later stage to an earlier stage. It is supported only when the target stage is empty, and you must deploy all items: selective backward deployment is not supported. This is the typical way to build lower stages from an existing production workspace.","why":["","Backward deployment cannot be selective. All items must be deployed.","A workspace can be assigned to only one stage and only one pipeline.","Deployment rules change data sources or parameters. They do not copy content between stages."],"ar":"النشر العكسي (Backward deployment) ممكن فقط إلى مرحلة فارغة ولجميع العناصر.","ref":[{"t":"Deploy content using deployment pipelines","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/cicd/deployment-pipelines/deploy-content"}]},{"id":"MT-L-015","d":"maintain","s":"lifecycle","type":"yesno","diff":3,"tag":"Deployment pipelines","q":"You manage a deployment pipeline named Pipe1 that has Development and Test stages. The workspaces WS_Dev and WS_Test are on a Fabric capacity. A semantic model named SM1 in WS_Test is owned by User2.<br>For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.","opts":["To assign WS_Test to the Test stage, you must be an admin of Pipe1 and an admin of WS_Test.","A user who is an admin of Pipe1 and has the Viewer role in both WS_Dev and WS_Test can deploy items from Development to Test.","To create a data source rule for SM1, you must be the owner of SM1."],"ans":[true,false,true],"exp":"Assigning a workspace requires pipeline admin permission and the Admin role in the workspace being assigned. Deploying to the next stage requires pipeline admin permission plus at least Contributor in both the source and target workspaces. Deployment rules can only be created by the owner of the item; otherwise the rule options are grayed out.","why":["Yes. Assignment requires pipeline admin and workspace admin of the workspace being assigned.","No. Deployment requires at least the Contributor role in both workspaces.","Yes. Only the item owner can create deployment rules for it."],"ar":"النشر يتطلب Pipeline admin مع Contributor على الأقل في المرحلتين، وإنشاء القواعد يتطلب ملكية العنصر.","ref":[{"t":"The deployment pipelines process","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/cicd/deployment-pipelines/understand-the-deployment-process"},{"t":"Create deployment rules","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/cicd/deployment-pipelines/create-rules"}]},{"id":"MT-L-016","d":"maintain","s":"lifecycle","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"Impact analysis","q":"You have a Fabric workspace named WS1 that contains a lakehouse named LH1. Semantic models and reports in several other workspaces use LH1. You plan to drop a table from LH1. You need to identify every downstream item in all workspaces that might be affected. What should you use?","opts":["Impact analysis for LH1","The lineage view of WS1","The Monitoring hub","The workspace usage metrics report of WS1"],"ans":0,"exp":"Impact analysis lists the workspaces and items that depend on an item, either as direct children or as all downstream items, including items in other workspaces. Lineage view shows only the items in the current workspace plus upstream sources one level up, so it does not reveal downstream items in other workspaces.","why":["","Lineage view does not show downstream items located in other workspaces.","The Monitoring hub tracks activity runs, not dependencies between items.","Usage metrics show report views, not the dependency chain from LH1."],"ar":"استخدم Impact analysis لمعرفة العناصر المتأثرة في جميع الـ workspaces، لأن Lineage view يعرض الـ workspace الحالي فقط.","ref":[{"t":"Impact analysis","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/governance/impact-analysis"},{"t":"Lineage in Fabric","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/governance/lineage"}]},{"id":"MT-L-017","d":"maintain","s":"lifecycle","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Impact analysis","q":"You have a Fabric warehouse named WH1. You plan to rename a column in the dbo.Customer table next week. Semantic models in four workspaces use WH1. You need to send an email about the planned change to the contact lists of the affected workspaces with the least effort. What should you do?","opts":["Open impact analysis for WH1 and select Notify contacts","Open the lineage view of the WH1 workspace and select Share on each downstream item","Endorse WH1 as Certified and add a description of the change","Apply a sensitivity label to WH1 that includes a change notice"],"ans":0,"exp":"The impact analysis pane includes a Notify contacts button. It sends your message to the contact lists of all impacted workspaces, including workspaces that you cannot access, and shows your name so recipients can reply.","why":["","Sharing grants access to items. It does not notify workspace contacts about a change.","Endorsement signals quality. It does not send any notification.","Sensitivity labels classify data. They do not notify downstream owners."],"ar":"زر Notify contacts في Impact analysis يرسل بريدا لقوائم الاتصال في كل الـ workspaces المتأثرة.","ref":[{"t":"Impact analysis","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/governance/impact-analysis"}]},{"id":"MT-L-018","d":"maintain","s":"lifecycle","type":"multi","n":2,"diff":2,"tag":"Lineage view","q":"You have a Fabric workspace named WS1 that contains a Dataflow Gen2 named DF1, a lakehouse named LH1 loaded by DF1, and a semantic model named SM1 that uses LH1. DF1 reads from an Azure SQL database. Which two statements about lineage view and impact analysis are true? Each correct answer presents a complete solution. Select TWO.","opts":["Lineage view of WS1 shows upstream items that are outside WS1 one level up.","Impact analysis for the Azure SQL data source shows only its direct child items.","Users with the Viewer role in WS1 see the data source cards in lineage view.","Lineage view of WS1 shows reports in other workspaces that use SM1.","Only Fabric administrators can open impact analysis."],"ans":[0,1],"exp":"Lineage view shows all items in the workspace and upstream connections outside it, one level up. For data sources, impact analysis shows only direct children; to go further downstream you run impact analysis on those children. Viewers can open lineage view but do not see data sources.","why":["","","Viewers can open lineage view but data sources are hidden from them.","Downstream items in other workspaces are not shown in lineage view. Use impact analysis instead.","Impact analysis is available to users with write permission on the item, not only to administrators."],"ar":"Lineage view يعرض المصادر خارج الـ workspace بمستوى واحد للأعلى، و Impact analysis لمصدر البيانات يعرض الأبناء المباشرين فقط.","ref":[{"t":"Lineage in Fabric","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/governance/lineage"},{"t":"Impact analysis","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/governance/impact-analysis"}]},{"id":"MT-L-019","d":"maintain","s":"lifecycle","type":"single","diff":3,"tag":"Impact analysis","q":"You have a shared semantic model named SM1 that is used by 35 reports and dashboards across 12 workspaces. You plan to remove a measure from SM1. You need to prioritize testing on the downstream reports that have the most distinct viewers over the last 30 days. What should you use?","opts":["Semantic model impact analysis for SM1, which shows views and viewers for each impacted report and dashboard","The lineage view of the workspace that contains SM1, highlighting the SM1 card","The OneLake catalog, filtered to items endorsed in your organization","The deployment pipeline compare view for SM1"],"ans":0,"exp":"Semantic model impact analysis summarizes the potentially impacted workspaces, reports and dashboards, and includes usage metrics: the number of distinct viewers and views for each report and dashboard over the last 30 days, excluding today. This lets you focus on the most used content first.","why":["","Lineage view shows only items in the current workspace and has no usage metrics.","The OneLake catalog helps discover items but does not show downstream usage of SM1.","Compare view shows differences between pipeline stages, not downstream usage."],"ar":"Impact analysis للنموذج الدلالي يعرض عدد المشاهدات والمستخدمين لكل تقرير متأثر خلال آخر 30 يوما.","ref":[{"t":"Semantic model impact analysis","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/collaborate-share/service-dataset-impact-analysis"}]},{"id":"MT-L-020","d":"maintain","s":"lifecycle","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"XMLA endpoint","q":"You have a semantic model named SM1 in a workspace on a Fabric F64 capacity. A developer must connect Tabular Editor to SM1 and add a calculation group. The XMLA Endpoint setting of the capacity is set to Read Only. What should you do first?","opts":["Set the XMLA Endpoint setting of the capacity to Read Write","Enable the large semantic model storage format for SM1","Grant the developer Build permission on SM1 only","Move the workspace to a Pro license"],"ans":0,"exp":"Tabular Editor needs XMLA read-only for queries but read-write for metadata operations such as adding calculation groups. The capacity admin changes the XMLA Endpoint setting to Read Write for the Power BI workload, and the setting applies to all workspaces on that capacity.","why":["","Large model format is recommended to speed up write operations but it does not enable writes.","Build permission alone does not allow metadata changes through the XMLA endpoint.","Pro workspaces do not support the XMLA endpoint. Fabric, Premium or PPU capacity is required."],"ar":"لتعديل النموذج عبر Tabular Editor يجب ضبط XMLA Endpoint على Read Write في إعدادات الـ capacity.","ref":[{"t":"Semantic model connectivity with the XMLA endpoint","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/enterprise/powerbi/service-premium-connect-tools"}]},{"id":"MT-L-021","d":"maintain","s":"lifecycle","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"XMLA endpoint","q":"You published an Import semantic model named SM1 from Power BI Desktop to a workspace on a Fabric F64 capacity. SM1 does not use incremental refresh or automatic aggregations. A developer then uses SQL Server Management Studio through the XMLA endpoint to add a perspective to SM1. An analyst who has the Contributor role in the workspace now needs a local copy of SM1 and selects <b>Download this file</b> for SM1 in the Power BI service. What is the result?","opts":["The analyst can download SM1 as a .pbix file.","The download option is unavailable because SM1 was modified through the XMLA endpoint.","The download fails until the analyst is given the Admin role in the workspace.","The download fails until SM1 is converted to the large semantic model storage format."],"ans":0,"exp":"Historically, a semantic model that was modified through the XMLA endpoint could no longer be downloaded as a .pbix file. Since the September 2025 Power BI update, the service supports downloading .pbix files for semantic models altered through the XMLA endpoint. The remaining download limitations include models with incremental refresh, Direct Lake models, and models with automatic aggregations, and the user must be at least a Contributor in the workspace. Keeping a .pbip project in source control is still the recommended way to manage models that are edited with external tools.","why":["","This restriction was removed in the September 2025 update; XMLA-modified models can now be downloaded.","Contributor is the minimum workspace role required to download a .pbix file.","The large storage format is not a prerequisite for downloading; it only prevents downloads through REST APIs."],"ar":"منذ تحديث سبتمبر 2025 يمكن تنزيل نموذج تم تعديله عبر XMLA endpoint كملف .pbix، مع بقاء قيود مثل incremental refresh وDirect Lake.","ref":[{"t":"Download a report from the Power BI service to Power BI Desktop","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/create-reports/service-export-to-pbix"},{"t":"Previous monthly updates to Power BI Desktop and the Power BI service","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/fundamentals/desktop-latest-update-archive"}]},{"id":"MT-L-022","d":"maintain","s":"lifecycle","type":"single","diff":3,"tag":"XMLA endpoint","q":"You have a semantic model named SM1 in a workspace on a Fabric capacity with XMLA read/write enabled. SM1 has an incremental refresh policy that stores five years of partitions. You added two measures to SM1 in Power BI Desktop. You need to deploy only the new measures without replacing the model and without losing the historical partitions. What should you use?","opts":["ALM Toolkit to perform a metadata-only deployment through the XMLA endpoint","Publish from Power BI Desktop and select Replace when prompted","DAX Studio connected to the XMLA endpoint","Export a .pbit file and upload it to the workspace"],"ans":0,"exp":"Publishing from Power BI Desktop replaces the whole model, which can drop the historical partitions created by incremental refresh. ALM Toolkit compares the local model with the service model and deploys only selected differences, such as new measures, through the XMLA endpoint while retaining partitions.","why":["","Replacing the model overwrites it with the smaller Desktop version and loses the refreshed history.","DAX Studio is a query and performance tool that needs only read access. It is not used to deploy metadata.","A .pbit template cannot be uploaded to update an existing model and contains no partitions."],"ar":"استخدم ALM Toolkit لنشر metadata فقط عبر XMLA دون فقدان partitions الخاصة بـ incremental refresh.","ref":[{"t":"Advanced incremental refresh and real-time data with the XMLA endpoint","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/connect-data/incremental-refresh-xmla"},{"t":"Semantic model connectivity with the XMLA endpoint","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/enterprise/powerbi/service-premium-connect-tools"}]},{"id":"MT-L-023","d":"maintain","s":"lifecycle","type":"order","diff":2,"tag":"XMLA endpoint","q":"You have a semantic model named SM1 in a workspace named WS1 that is assigned to a Fabric capacity. The XMLA endpoint is currently Read Only. You need to add object-level security to SM1 by using Tabular Editor. Which four actions should you perform in sequence?","opts":["Ask the capacity admin to set the XMLA Endpoint setting of the capacity to Read Write","Copy the workspace connection string of WS1 from the workspace settings","Connect Tabular Editor to the workspace connection string and open SM1","Define the object-level security on the role and save the changes to the connected model","Download SM1 as a .pbix file and open the file in Tabular Editor","Set the XMLA endpoint to Read Only for WS1 in the workspace settings"],"ans":[0,1,2,3],"exp":"Object-level security is a modeling capability that is authored through the XMLA endpoint with read-write access. The capacity admin enables Read Write, you copy the workspace connection string (powerbi://api.powerbi.com/v1.0/myorg/WS1), connect Tabular Editor to it, make the change and save it back to the model.","why":["Step 1: write operations require XMLA Read Write on the capacity.","Step 2: the connection string identifies the workspace as an Analysis Services server.","Step 3: Tabular Editor connects to the model through the XMLA endpoint.","Step 4: saving pushes the metadata change to SM1.","Distractor: editing a downloaded .pbix does not change the model in the service.","Distractor: the XMLA endpoint setting is configured per capacity, and Read Only would block the change."],"ar":"لإضافة OLS: فعل Read Write ثم انسخ connection string ثم اتصل عبر Tabular Editor واحفظ التغيير.","ref":[{"t":"Semantic model connectivity with the XMLA endpoint","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/enterprise/powerbi/service-premium-connect-tools"},{"t":"Power BI usage scenarios: Advanced data model management","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/powerbi-implementation-planning-usage-scenario-advanced-data-model-management"}]},{"id":"MT-L-024","d":"maintain","s":"lifecycle","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"Power BI template (.pbit)","q":"Your BI team built a finance report in Power BI Desktop that has a corporate theme, a date table, standard DAX measures and a query parameter for the server name. You need to give other report authors a reusable starting point that contains the pages, data model and queries but no data. What should you create?","opts":["A Power BI template (.pbit) by using File > Export > Power BI template","A Power BI data source (.pbids) file by using Export PBIDS","A copy of the .pbix file saved with the data","A Power BI project (.pbip)"],"ans":0,"exp":"A .pbit template contains report pages and visuals, the data model definition (schema, relationships, measures) and all query definitions including parameters, but no data. When an author opens it, Power BI Desktop prompts for parameter values and then loads data with their own credentials.","why":["","A .pbids file contains only one data source connection. It has no pages or model.","A .pbix copy includes the data, which increases size and can expose data inappropriately.","A .pbip is designed for source control of ongoing development, not as a distributable template."],"ar":"ملف .pbit قالب يحتوي الصفحات والنموذج والاستعلامات والـ parameters بدون بيانات.","ref":[{"t":"Create and use report templates in Power BI Desktop","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/create-reports/desktop-templates"}]},{"id":"MT-L-025","d":"maintain","s":"lifecycle","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Power BI data source (.pbids)","q":"New report authors at Fabrikam struggle to find the correct SQL Server and database in the Get Data dialog. You need to give them a file that, when opened, launches Power BI Desktop already pointed at the Sales database, prompts for credentials, and lets them choose the tables to load in Navigator. What should you distribute?","opts":["A Power BI data source (.pbids) file","A Power BI template (.pbit) file","A Power BI project (.pbip) folder","A report that uses a live connection to a shared semantic model"],"ans":0,"exp":"A .pbids file describes a single data source connection. Opening it launches Power BI Desktop, prompts for credentials and opens Navigator so the author can select tables. You can generate it from File > Options and settings > Data source settings > Export PBIDS.","why":["","A .pbit also brings a predefined model, queries and pages rather than letting authors choose tables from the source.","A .pbip is a source-control format for a complete report and model, not a connection shortcut.","A live connection reuses an existing model. Authors could not select source tables in Navigator."],"ar":"ملف .pbids يفتح Power BI Desktop متصلا بمصدر بيانات واحد ويطلب بيانات الاعتماد واختيار الجداول.","ref":[{"t":"Get data in Power BI Desktop","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/connect-data/desktop-data-sources"}]},{"id":"MT-L-026","d":"maintain","s":"lifecycle","type":"yesno","diff":2,"tag":"Reusable assets","q":"You are preparing reusable assets for report authors at Contoso.<br>For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.","opts":["A single .pbids file can define connections to both a SQL Server database and a SharePoint list.","A .pbids file stores the credentials needed to authenticate to the data source.","When an author opens a .pbit file that contains query parameters, Power BI Desktop prompts for parameter values."],"ans":[false,false,true],"exp":"A .pbids file supports only one data source; specifying more than one results in an error. It does not include authentication information or table and schema information. A .pbit template asks for any parameters defined in the original report and then prompts for data access with the author credentials.","why":["No. A .pbids file supports a single data source only.","No. Credentials are never stored. The author is prompted to authenticate.","Yes. Parameter values are requested when the template is opened."],"ar":"ملف .pbids يدعم مصدرا واحدا فقط ولا يخزن بيانات الاعتماد، وملف .pbit يطلب قيم الـ parameters عند الفتح.","ref":[{"t":"Get data in Power BI Desktop","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/connect-data/desktop-data-sources"},{"t":"Create and use report templates in Power BI Desktop","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/create-reports/desktop-templates"}]},{"id":"MT-L-027","d":"maintain","s":"lifecycle","type":"multi","n":2,"diff":3,"tag":"Shared semantic models","q":"You have a certified semantic model named SM1 in a workspace named WS_Data. Analysts who have the Contributor role in a separate workspace named WS_Reports must build their own reports on SM1 and use Analyze in Excel. They must not be able to modify SM1 or see other items in WS_Data. Which two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. Select TWO.","opts":["Grant the analysts Build permission on SM1","Have the analysts connect from Power BI Desktop by using Get data > Power BI semantic models and publish their reports to WS_Reports","Add the analysts to the Contributor role of WS_Data","Have the analysts download SM1 as a .pbix file and publish a copy to WS_Reports","Distribute a .pbids file that points to the data sources of SM1"],"ans":[0,1],"exp":"Build permission on a shared semantic model lets users create new content on it, such as live connected reports and Analyze in Excel workbooks, without workspace access. Connecting through Power BI semantic models creates a live connection, so the report uses SM1 in place and RLS still applies, and the report can be published to a different workspace.","why":["","","Contributor on WS_Data would let the analysts edit SM1 and see all other items in the workspace.","Copying the model creates duplicate, unmanaged models and breaks the single source of truth.","A .pbids file makes them build new models from the sources instead of reusing SM1."],"ar":"امنح Build permission على النموذج المشترك واتصل عبر live connection لبناء تقارير دون تعديل النموذج.","ref":[{"t":"Build permission for shared semantic models","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/connect-data/service-datasets-build-permissions"},{"t":"Connect to semantic models in the Power BI service from Power BI Desktop","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/connect-data/desktop-report-lifecycle-datasets"}]}],"cases":[{"id":"CS-1","title":"Contoso Retail","scenario":"<h4>Overview</h4><p>Contoso, Ltd. is a retail company that operates 120 stores in North America. The analytics team is moving its reporting platform to Microsoft Fabric. All Fabric workspaces are assigned to a Fabric capacity named Cap1 that uses the F64 SKU.</p><h4>Existing environment</h4><ul><li>Contoso has three workspaces named SalesDev, SalesTest, and SalesProd.</li><li>SalesDev contains a lakehouse named LH1, a warehouse named WH1, and an Import mode semantic model named SalesModel.</li><li>An external ETL tool writes point-of-sale (POS) transactions in Delta format to a container named pos in an Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 account named storage1. The container holds about 2 TB of data and receives new files every 15 minutes.</li><li>WH1 contains the DimProduct, DimStore, and DimCustomer tables. DimCustomer contains columns named CustomerID, CustomerName, City, LoyaltyEmail, and ModifiedDate.</li><li>A staging table named WH1.stg.Customer is loaded nightly from a CRM export. The CRM export often contains several rows for the same CustomerID with different ModifiedDate values.</li><li>SalesModel imports four years of data from a table named FactSales that has an OrderDate column. A full refresh of SalesModel takes five hours.</li><li>Contoso has four Microsoft Entra security groups named DataEng, Analysts, StoreManagers, and BIAdmins.</li></ul><h4>Requirements</h4><h5>Planned changes</h5><ul><li>Create a new semantic model named SM2 that combines the POS data in LH1 with the dimension tables in WH1.</li><li>Create a deployment pipeline named SalesPipeline that uses SalesDev, SalesTest, and SalesProd as the Development, Test, and Production stages.</li><li>Connect SalesDev to a Git repository in Azure DevOps.</li></ul><h5>Technical requirements</h5><ul><li>The POS data must be available as a table in LH1 without copying the data from storage1.</li><li>DimCustomer must contain exactly one row per CustomerID, and that row must be the version that has the latest ModifiedDate.</li><li>SM2 must use Direct Lake storage for the POS data and the dimension tables, and it must never fall back to DirectQuery mode.</li><li>The daily refresh of SalesModel must reload only the last three days of FactSales while keeping four years of history.</li><li>In SalesProd, SM2 must read data from the lakehouse in SalesProd, not from the lakehouse in SalesDev.</li></ul><h5>Security requirements</h5><ul><li>Members of Analysts must be able to query WH1 by using T-SQL, but must be prevented from reading the LoyaltyEmail column.</li><li>Only members of BIAdmins must be able to apply the Certified endorsement badge to items.</li><li>StoreManagers must see only the data for their own store in reports.</li></ul>","questions":[{"id":"CS1-01","d":"prep","s":"get","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"Shortcuts","q":"You need to make the POS data available in LH1. The solution must meet the technical requirements. What should you create?","opts":["A OneLake shortcut in the Tables section of LH1 that points to the pos container in storage1","A mirrored database that replicates storage1 into Fabric","A Copy job that copies the pos container into the Files section of LH1 every 15 minutes","A Dataflow Gen2 that loads the pos container into a new Delta table in LH1"],"ans":0,"exp":"An ADLS Gen2 shortcut in the Tables section of LH1 references the Delta data where it lives in storage1, so no data is copied. Because the target is in Delta format, the lakehouse automatically recognizes the shortcut folder as a table that Spark, the SQL analytics endpoint, and Direct Lake can read.","why":["","Mirroring replicates operational databases into OneLake. It creates a copy and is not used to reference Delta files in ADLS Gen2.","A Copy job physically copies the data, which violates the requirement to avoid copying data from storage1.","A Dataflow Gen2 ingests and stores a new copy of the data in LH1, which violates the no-copy requirement."],"ar":"استخدم shortcut في قسم Tables لإتاحة بيانات Delta الموجودة في ADLS Gen2 دون نسخها.","ref":[{"t":"OneLake shortcuts","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/onelake/onelake-shortcuts"}]},{"id":"CS1-02","d":"prep","s":"transform","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Duplicates","q":"You need to load DimCustomer from WH1.stg.Customer. The solution must meet the technical requirements. Which T-SQL expression should you use to identify the rows to keep?<pre>INSERT INTO dbo.DimCustomer\nSELECT CustomerID, CustomerName, City, LoyaltyEmail, ModifiedDate\nFROM (\n  SELECT *, [expression] AS rn\n  FROM stg.Customer\n) AS s\nWHERE rn = 1;</pre>","opts":["<code>ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY CustomerID ORDER BY ModifiedDate DESC)</code>","<code>ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY ModifiedDate ORDER BY CustomerID DESC)</code>","<code>ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY CustomerID ORDER BY ModifiedDate ASC)</code>","<code>COUNT(*) OVER (PARTITION BY CustomerID)</code>"],"ans":0,"exp":"ROW_NUMBER restarts numbering for each partition. Partitioning by CustomerID creates one group per customer, and ordering by ModifiedDate DESC assigns 1 to the most recent version, so filtering on rn = 1 keeps exactly one row per customer with the latest ModifiedDate.","why":["","Partitioning by ModifiedDate groups rows by date instead of by customer, so a customer can still appear several times.","Ordering ascending assigns 1 to the oldest version, so the query would keep the earliest row instead of the latest.","COUNT(*) returns the number of versions per customer. Filtering on 1 keeps only customers that have a single row and drops all customers that have duplicates."],"ar":"استخدم ROW_NUMBER مع PARTITION BY على المفتاح و ORDER BY DESC على التاريخ للاحتفاظ بأحدث صف فقط.","ref":[{"t":"ROW_NUMBER (Transact-SQL)","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/row-number-transact-sql"},{"t":"Remove duplicate rows from a SQL Server table","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/sql/database-engine/development/remove-duplicate-rows-sql-server-tab"}]},{"id":"CS1-03","d":"model","s":"design","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Direct Lake","q":"You need to recommend a table storage mode for SM2. The solution must meet the planned changes and the technical requirements. What should you recommend?","opts":["Direct Lake on OneLake","Direct Lake on SQL analytics endpoint","DirectQuery","Import"],"ans":0,"exp":"Direct Lake on OneLake can use Delta tables from one or more Fabric items, including tables exposed through shortcuts, so SM2 can combine LH1 and WH1. It runs only in DirectLakeOnly mode and does not fall back to DirectQuery, which satisfies the requirement that SM2 never falls back.","why":["","Direct Lake on SQL analytics endpoint can use only a single Fabric data source, so it cannot combine LH1 and WH1 in Direct Lake mode, and it supports DirectQuery fallback.","DirectQuery sends every query to the source and does not meet the requirement to use Direct Lake storage.","Import mode copies the data into the model during scheduled refreshes, which is costly for 2 TB of POS data that changes every 15 minutes, and it does not meet the requirement to use Direct Lake storage."],"ar":"اختر Direct Lake on OneLake عند الجمع بين أكثر من Fabric item وعند الحاجة لعدم الرجوع إلى DirectQuery.","ref":[{"t":"Direct Lake overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-overview"},{"t":"Develop Direct Lake semantic models","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-develop"}]},{"id":"CS1-04","d":"model","s":"optimize","type":"order","diff":2,"tag":"Incremental refresh","q":"You need to configure SalesModel to meet the refresh requirement. Which four actions should you perform in sequence?","opts":["Define an incremental refresh policy on FactSales that archives four years of data and incrementally refreshes three days.","In Power Query Editor, create two Date/Time parameters named RangeStart and RangeEnd.","Set the DirectLakeBehavior property of SalesModel to DirectLakeOnly.","Filter FactSales so that OrderDate is greater than or equal to RangeStart and less than RangeEnd.","Publish SalesModel to the Power BI service and perform the first refresh.","Enable Detect data changes on the policy by using the OrderDate column."],"ans":[1,3,0,4],"exp":"Incremental refresh requires the reserved, case-sensitive Date/Time parameters RangeStart and RangeEnd. Next, you filter the date column with them (one boundary inclusive, the other exclusive) so the Incremental refresh option is enabled. Then you define the policy (store period of four years, refresh period of three days), publish, and run the first refresh, which creates the partitions and loads history.","why":["Third step: the policy can only be enabled after the table is filtered on RangeStart and RangeEnd.","First step: the parameters must exist before they can be used in a filter.","Distractor: DirectLakeBehavior applies to Direct Lake models, not to an Import model such as SalesModel.","Second step: the filter on OrderDate is what enables the incremental refresh policy.","Fourth step: the policy is applied only in the service, starting with the first refresh.","Distractor: Detect data changes needs an audit column that is different from the column used to partition the data."],"ar":"لإعداد incremental refresh: أنشئ RangeStart و RangeEnd، ثم طبّق الفلتر، ثم عرّف الـ policy، ثم انشر ونفّذ أول refresh.","ref":[{"t":"Configure incremental refresh and real-time data","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/connect-data/incremental-refresh-configure"},{"t":"Incremental refresh overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/connect-data/incremental-refresh-overview"}]},{"id":"CS1-05","d":"maintain","s":"security","type":"multi","n":2,"diff":2,"tag":"CLS and certification","q":"You need to meet the security requirements for Analysts and BIAdmins. Which two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. Select TWO.","opts":["In WH1, run <code>GRANT SELECT ON dbo.DimCustomer (CustomerID, CustomerName, City, ModifiedDate) TO [Analysts];</code>","In the Fabric admin portal, enable the Certification tenant setting and specify BIAdmins as the only security group that can certify items.","Add BIAdmins to the Admin role of SalesProd.","In SalesModel, create an object-level security (OLS) role that hides the LoyaltyEmail column."],"ans":[0,1],"exp":"Column-level security in a Fabric warehouse is implemented with GRANT SELECT and a column list, so Analysts can query every column except LoyaltyEmail by using T-SQL. Certification is enabled in the tenant settings, and only members of the security groups specified there are authorized to apply the Certified badge.","why":["","","Workspace Admins cannot certify items unless they are in a security group that the tenant Certification setting authorizes, and this action does not stop other authorized users.","OLS in a semantic model applies only to queries against that model. It does not restrict T-SQL queries against WH1."],"ar":"طبّق column-level security في الـ Warehouse باستخدام GRANT SELECT مع قائمة أعمدة، وحدد مجموعة الـ certifiers من Tenant settings.","ref":[{"t":"Column-level security in Fabric Data Warehouse","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/column-level-security"},{"t":"Enable item certification","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/admin/endorsement-certification-enable"}]},{"id":"CS1-06","d":"maintain","s":"lifecycle","type":"yesno","diff":3,"tag":"Deployment pipelines","q":"You create SalesPipeline and deploy SM2 and SalesModel from SalesDev to SalesTest, and then to SalesProd. For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.","opts":["After SM2 is deployed to SalesProd, it automatically reads from the lakehouse in SalesProd without any additional configuration.","After the first deployment of SalesModel to the empty SalesTest stage, SalesModel must be refreshed in SalesTest before its reports show data.","When you redeploy SalesModel from SalesTest to SalesProd, the refresh schedule configured in SalesProd is replaced by the schedule from SalesTest."],"ans":[false,true,false],"exp":"Direct Lake semantic models do not autobind to items in the target stage: after deployment, SM2 still reads from the lakehouse in the source stage. SM2 uses Direct Lake on OneLake, for which deployment pipeline data source rules are not supported, so you must rebind it by another method after deployment, for example by updating its data source expression with TMDL view or an XMLA-based tool. (For Direct Lake on SQL analytics endpoints, a data source rule can rebind the model.) Deployment pipelines copy metadata, not data, so a newly deployed semantic model must be refreshed. Semantic model refresh schedules are not copied during deployment, so each stage keeps its own schedule.","why":["No: a Direct Lake model stays bound to the source stage lakehouse after deployment and must be rebound explicitly.","Yes: data is not copied between stages, only metadata.","No: the refresh schedule is among the semantic model properties that are not copied."],"ar":"deployment pipelines تنسخ الـ metadata فقط، ونموذج Direct Lake لا يرتبط تلقائيا بـ Lakehouse المرحلة الهدف ويجب إعادة ربطه.","ref":[{"t":"The deployment pipelines process","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/cicd/deployment-pipelines/understand-the-deployment-process"},{"t":"Direct Lake overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-overview"}]}]},{"id":"CS-2","title":"Fabrikam Healthcare Analytics","scenario":"<h4>Overview</h4><p>Fabrikam Health operates 14 hospitals. The clinical analytics team uses Microsoft Fabric for operational monitoring and for regulatory reporting. All workspaces are assigned to a Fabric capacity named Cap2 that uses the F128 SKU. The semantic model workload settings of Cap2 have never been changed from their default values.</p><h4>Existing environment</h4><ul><li>A workspace named ClinicalOps contains an eventstream named ES1 and an eventhouse named EH1. EH1 contains a KQL database named KDB1.</li><li>ES1 ingests about 50,000 events per minute from bedside monitors into a KDB1 table named DeviceTelemetry. The table has columns named DeviceId, WardId, Timestamp, HeartRate, and SpO2.</li><li>A workspace named ClinicalDW contains a warehouse named WH2 and a semantic model named ClinicalModel. WH2 contains tables named Admissions, Patient, Diagnosis, and PatientDiagnosis.</li><li>A patient can have many diagnoses, and a diagnosis can apply to many patients. PatientDiagnosis contains one row for each PatientID and DiagnosisCode pair. Admissions contains one row per admission and includes PatientID.</li><li>ClinicalModel uses Direct Lake on SQL analytics endpoint over WH2. WH2 has a T-SQL security policy that applies row-level security (RLS) to Admissions by HospitalID.</li><li>A workspace named ClinicalDS contains a lakehouse named LH2 that data scientists use with Spark notebooks.</li><li>Members of a security group named BIDev are Members of ClinicalDW and use Tabular Editor.</li><li>The Fabric administrator disabled the tenant settings named <b>Allow XMLA endpoints and Analyze in Excel with on-premises semantic models</b> and <b>Automatically apply sensitivity labels to downstream content</b>.</li></ul><h4>Requirements</h4><h5>Planned changes</h5><ul><li>BIDev will deploy changes to ClinicalModel from Tabular Editor by using the XMLA endpoint.</li><li>Data scientists will analyze DeviceTelemetry from notebooks in LH2.</li><li>A real-time dashboard will show vital signs for each device.</li></ul><h5>Technical requirements</h5><ul><li>DeviceTelemetry must be readable from LH2 as a Delta table. Do not add a second ingestion path from ES1 and do not schedule copy jobs.</li><li>The dashboard must show the average HeartRate per device for each five-minute interval during the last hour.</li><li>In ClinicalModel, filtering by Diagnosis must filter Admissions correctly through the patients that have that diagnosis.</li><li>The team must understand how the existing RLS in WH2 affects ClinicalModel query performance.</li></ul><h5>Security requirements</h5><ul><li>WH2 and ClinicalModel are labeled Highly Confidential. When a compliance officer changes the label on ClinicalModel, the new label must be applied automatically to all existing downstream reports, even when the officer does not have edit permissions on those reports.</li><li>Follow the principle of least privilege.</li></ul>","questions":[{"id":"CS2-01","d":"prep","s":"get","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Eventhouse OneLake availability","q":"You need to make DeviceTelemetry available to the data scientists. The solution must meet the technical requirements. What should you do?","opts":["Turn on OneLake availability for DeviceTelemetry in KDB1, and then create a OneLake shortcut in LH2 that points to the table.","Add LH2 as a second destination in ES1.","Create a Data pipeline in ClinicalDS that copies DeviceTelemetry to LH2 every five minutes.","Create a KQL database shortcut in KDB1 that points to LH2."],"ans":0,"exp":"Turning on OneLake availability exposes KQL database data in Delta Lake format in OneLake without a separate ingestion process. A lakehouse can then reference that Delta data through a OneLake shortcut, so notebooks in LH2 read it like any other table.","why":["","A lakehouse destination in ES1 creates a second ingestion path, which the requirements prohibit.","A scheduled pipeline copy violates the requirement to avoid copy jobs and duplicates the data.","A shortcut in KDB1 that points to LH2 goes in the wrong direction. It exposes lakehouse data to KQL, not telemetry data to the lakehouse."],"ar":"فعّل OneLake availability في الـ KQL database ثم أنشئ shortcut من الـ Lakehouse لقراءة البيانات بصيغة Delta.","ref":[{"t":"Turn on OneLake availability for an eventhouse","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/real-time-intelligence/event-house-onelake-availability"},{"t":"OneLake shortcuts","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/onelake/onelake-shortcuts"}]},{"id":"CS2-02","d":"prep","s":"query","type":"single","diff":1,"tag":"KQL","q":"You need to write the KQL query for the real-time dashboard. Which query should you use?","opts":["<pre>DeviceTelemetry\n| where Timestamp > ago(1h)\n| summarize avg(HeartRate) by DeviceId, bin(Timestamp, 5m)</pre>","<pre>DeviceTelemetry\n| where Timestamp > ago(5m)\n| summarize avg(HeartRate) by DeviceId, bin(Timestamp, 1h)</pre>","<pre>DeviceTelemetry\n| where Timestamp > ago(1h)\n| summarize avg(HeartRate) by bin(Timestamp, 5m)</pre>","<pre>DeviceTelemetry\n| where Timestamp > ago(1h)\n| summarize count() by DeviceId, bin(Timestamp, 5m)</pre>"],"ans":0,"exp":"The where clause with ago(1h) keeps the last hour of events. The summarize operator calculates avg(HeartRate) for each DeviceId and for each five-minute bucket that bin(Timestamp, 5m) creates.","why":["","This query swaps the values: it keeps only five minutes of data and groups it into one-hour buckets.","Without DeviceId in the by clause, the query returns one average for all devices instead of one per device.","count() returns the number of events, not the average heart rate."],"ar":"استخدم ago لتحديد النافذة الزمنية و bin داخل summarize لتجميع القيم في فترات ثابتة.","ref":[{"t":"summarize operator","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/kusto/query/summarize-operator?view=microsoft-fabric"},{"t":"KQL quick reference","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/kusto/query/kql-quick-reference?view=microsoft-fabric"}]},{"id":"CS2-03","d":"model","s":"optimize","type":"yesno","diff":3,"tag":"Direct Lake fallback","q":"You are evaluating how the existing RLS in WH2 affects ClinicalModel. For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.","opts":["With the default settings, DAX queries against ClinicalModel that reference Admissions fall back to DirectQuery mode.","If you set the DirectLakeBehavior property of ClinicalModel to DirectLakeOnly, queries that reference Admissions return an error instead of falling back.","If you rebuild ClinicalModel by using Direct Lake on OneLake, the T-SQL RLS policy on Admissions is enforced for report users."],"ans":[true,true,false],"exp":"Direct Lake on SQL analytics endpoint falls back to DirectQuery when a referenced table has RLS defined at the SQL analytics endpoint, and the default DirectLakeBehavior value is Automatic. DirectLakeOnly disables fallback, so those queries fail. Direct Lake on OneLake reads the Delta files through OneLake, so SQL-based RLS is not applied. You would need OneLake security or semantic model RLS instead.","why":["Yes: SQL RLS on a referenced table triggers DirectQuery fallback in Automatic mode.","Yes: DirectLakeOnly makes queries fail when Direct Lake conditions are not met.","No: Direct Lake on OneLake does not observe SQL-based RLS defined in the warehouse."],"ar":"RLS المعرّف في SQL يسبب fallback إلى DirectQuery في Direct Lake on SQL، ولا يُطبَّق في Direct Lake on OneLake.","ref":[{"t":"How Direct Lake works","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-how-it-works"},{"t":"Direct Lake overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-overview"}]},{"id":"CS2-04","d":"model","s":"design","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Bridge tables","q":"You need to configure the relationships in ClinicalModel to meet the Diagnosis filtering requirement. Patient is related to Admissions by using a one-to-many relationship on PatientID. What should you do?","opts":["Create one-to-many relationships from Diagnosis to PatientDiagnosis and from Patient to PatientDiagnosis, and set the cross-filter direction of the Patient to PatientDiagnosis relationship to Both.","Create one-to-many relationships from Diagnosis to PatientDiagnosis and from Patient to PatientDiagnosis, and set the cross-filter direction of the Diagnosis to PatientDiagnosis relationship to Both.","Create a many-to-many relationship between Diagnosis and Admissions on the DiagnosisCode column.","Add a calculated column to Patient that concatenates all the diagnosis codes for each patient, and filter on that column."],"ans":0,"exp":"PatientDiagnosis is a bridge table between two dimensions. A filter on Diagnosis flows to PatientDiagnosis through a normal one-to-many relationship, but it must then flow from the many side back to Patient, which requires Both on the Patient to PatientDiagnosis relationship. From Patient, the filter continues to Admissions.","why":["","A filter already flows from Diagnosis to PatientDiagnosis. Setting Both on that relationship does not let the filter reach Patient.","Admissions does not contain DiagnosisCode, and diagnoses belong to patients, not to admissions.","A concatenated text column is not a proper star schema design and makes accurate filtering by a single diagnosis unreliable."],"ar":"في علاقات many-to-many بين dimensions استخدم bridge table مع علاقة واحدة ثنائية الاتجاه لتمرير الفلتر إلى الـ fact.","ref":[{"t":"Many-to-many relationship guidance","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/relationships-many-to-many"}]},{"id":"CS2-05","d":"maintain","s":"lifecycle","type":"multi","n":2,"diff":2,"tag":"XMLA endpoint","q":"You need to ensure that BIDev can deploy changes to ClinicalModel by using Tabular Editor. The solution must follow the principle of least privilege. Which two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. Select TWO.","opts":["Set the XMLA Endpoint setting of Cap2 to Read Write.","Enable the Allow XMLA endpoints and Analyze in Excel with on-premises semantic models tenant setting for the BIDev group.","Grant BIDev the Build permission on ClinicalModel.","Connect ClinicalDW to a Git repository in Azure DevOps."],"ans":[0,1],"exp":"By default, the XMLA endpoint of a capacity is read-only, so a capacity admin must set it to Read Write for write operations such as deployments from Tabular Editor. The tenant setting Allow XMLA endpoints and Analyze in Excel with on-premises semantic models must also be enabled, and it can be scoped to specific security groups. BIDev are already workspace Members, which gives them write permission on the semantic model.","why":["","","Build permission is equivalent to a database reader over XMLA. It does not allow deployments, and BIDev already have write permission through the Member role.","Git integration provides version control but is not required to write to the model through the XMLA endpoint."],"ar":"للنشر عبر XMLA endpoint فعّل Read Write على الـ capacity وفعّل إعداد الـ tenant الخاص بـ XMLA endpoints.","ref":[{"t":"Semantic model connectivity with the XMLA endpoint","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/enterprise/powerbi/service-premium-connect-tools"}]},{"id":"CS2-06","d":"maintain","s":"security","type":"single","diff":2,"tag":"Sensitivity labels","q":"You need to meet the sensitivity label requirement for ClinicalModel. What should you do?","opts":["Enable the Automatically apply sensitivity labels to downstream content tenant setting.","Configure a default label policy in Microsoft Purview that applies Highly Confidential to new items.","Enable the Apply sensitivity labels from data sources to their data in Power BI tenant setting.","Add the compliance officers to the Member role of every workspace that contains a downstream report."],"ans":0,"exp":"The Automatically apply sensitivity labels to downstream content tenant setting turns on fully automated downstream inheritance. In that mode, a label applied to a semantic model propagates to existing downstream content without regard to the edit permissions of the user who changes it.","why":["","A default label policy labels unlabeled items when they are created or edited. It does not propagate a changed label to existing reports.","Inheritance from data sources applies labels from supported upstream sources to semantic models. It does not push labels to downstream reports.","Granting Member access violates least privilege, and the requirement says the officers do not have edit permissions on the reports."],"ar":"لتوريث الـ sensitivity label تلقائيا إلى العناصر الموجودة بدون صلاحيات تعديل فعّل fully automated downstream inheritance.","ref":[{"t":"Sensitivity label downstream inheritance","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/governance/service-security-sensitivity-label-downstream-inheritance"},{"t":"Information protection in Microsoft Fabric","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/governance/information-protection"}]}]}]};
